"use strict";(self.webpackChunkcasdoor_website=self.webpackChunkcasdoor_website||[]).push([["50832"],{49645(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"captcha-for-login-pages","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/captcha-for-login-pages","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-captcha-for-login-pages.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-captcha-for-login-pages.mdx","title":"Comparaci\xf3n de proveedores CAPTCHA para p\xe1ginas de inicio de sesi\xf3n: reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Turnstile, GeeTest y Alibaba Cloud en Casdoor","description":"Compare reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Cloudflare Turnstile, GeeTest, Alibaba Cloud Captcha y el captcha integrado para las p\xe1ginas de inicio de sesi\xf3n y registro, y \xfaselos en Casdoor.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"CAPTCHA","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/captcha"},{"inline":true,"label":"reCAPTCHA","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/re-captcha"},{"inline":true,"label":"hCaptcha","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/h-captcha"},{"inline":true,"label":"Turnstile","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/turnstile"},{"inline":true,"label":"GeeTest","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/gee-test"},{"inline":true,"label":"Alibaba Cloud","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/alibaba-cloud"},{"inline":true,"label":"Security","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/security"}],"readingTime":8.84,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"captcha-for-login-pages","title":"Comparaci\xf3n de proveedores CAPTCHA para p\xe1ginas de inicio de sesi\xf3n: reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Turnstile, GeeTest y Alibaba Cloud en Casdoor","description":"Compare reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Cloudflare Turnstile, GeeTest, Alibaba Cloud Captcha y el captcha integrado para las p\xe1ginas de inicio de sesi\xf3n y registro, y \xfaselos en Casdoor.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","CAPTCHA","reCAPTCHA","hCaptcha","Turnstile","GeeTest","Alibaba Cloud","Security"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Customize the Login Page in Casdoor: Custom Styling of the Authentication UI Without Forking","permalink":"/es/blog/customize-login-page"}},"content":"Para la mayor\xeda de las p\xe1ginas de inicio de sesi\xf3n y registro en Internet a nivel global, Cloudflare Turnstile es la opci\xf3n m\xe1s sencilla: el plan gratuito no tiene l\xedmite de desaf\xedos, y su modo predeterminado muestra una casilla de verificaci\xf3n solo a los visitantes de riesgo. Si muchos de sus usuarios est\xe1n en China continental, utilice en su lugar Alibaba Cloud Captcha o GeeTest, ya que Cloudflare indica que Turnstile no est\xe1 soportado all\xed. Si necesita que el captcha funcione sin ning\xfan servicio externo, utilice el captcha de imagen integrado de Casdoor. Casdoor admite todos estos, adem\xe1s de Google reCAPTCHA (v2 y v3) y hCaptcha, como [proveedores de captcha](/docs/provider/captcha/overview), y usted elige cu\xe1ndo aparece el captcha por aplicaci\xf3n.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## La comparaci\xf3n {#the-comparison}\\n\\nLos precios y la disponibilidad indicados a continuaci\xf3n se verificaron contra el sitio web de cada proveedor el 2026-11-02. Donde un proveedor no publica una cifra, la tabla lo indica en lugar de estimarla.\\n\\n| Proveedor | Lo que ve el usuario | Modo invisible | Datos y privacidad | Nivel gratuito y precios | China continental | Tipo de Casdoor |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Predeterminado de Casdoor | Escribe cinco d\xedgitos desde una imagen | No | Generado y verificado por su servidor Casdoor; sin terceros | Gratuito, parte de Casdoor | Funciona, sin llamadas externas | `Default` |\\n| Google reCAPTCHA v2 | Casilla de verificaci\xf3n; algunos usuarios obtienen un desaf\xedo de imagen | v2 tambi\xe9n tiene una variante invisible que desaf\xeda solo al tr\xe1fico sospechoso | Google; establece la cookie `_GRECAPTCHA` para an\xe1lisis de riesgo | El nivel Essentials es gratuito hasta 10,000 evaluaciones por mes por organizaci\xf3n; Premium cuesta $8 fijos hasta 100,000, luego $1 por cada 1,000 | Google recomienda usar `www.recaptcha.net` donde `www.google.com` no sea accesible | `reCAPTCHA`, `reCAPTCHA v2` (casilla de verificaci\xf3n) |\\n| Google reCAPTCHA v3 | Nada; devuelve una puntuaci\xf3n | S\xed | Igual que v2 | Igual que v2 | Igual que v2 | `reCAPTCHA v3` |\\n| hCaptcha | Casilla de verificaci\xf3n con desaf\xedos de imagen en el plan gratuito | Modo pasivo en Pro (\\"99.9% pasivo\\") y un modo sin CAPTCHA en Enterprise | Indica que el plan gratuito cumple con GDPR, CCPA, LGPD y PIPL; certificado conforme al Marco de Privacidad de Datos UE-EE. UU. | Basic es gratuito; Pro cuesta $139 al mes (o $99 al mes facturados anualmente) con 100,000 evaluaciones, luego $0.99 por cada 1,000; Enterprise a trav\xe9s de ventas | No documentado por hCaptcha | `hCaptcha` |\\n| Cloudflare Turnstile | El modo Managed muestra una casilla de verificaci\xf3n solo cuando es necesario | Modos de widget Non-interactive e Invisible | Cloudflare indica que procesa solo los datos estrictamente necesarios y no lee las entradas del formulario; no se requiere proxy de Cloudflare | Plan gratuito: desaf\xedos ilimitados, hasta 20 widgets; Enterprise a trav\xe9s de ventas | Cloudflare: \\"no compatible en China continental\\" | `Cloudflare Turnstile` |\\n| GeeTest CAPTCHA v4 | Un clic, rompecabezas deslizante, clic en texto, tres en l\xednea | No listado en la p\xe1gina de producto v4 | Proveedor chino; pol\xedtica de privacidad espec\xedfica del producto | Sin lista de precios p\xfablica; prueba gratuita de 30 d\xedas, luego ventas | Proveedor chino | `GEETEST` |\\n| Alibaba Cloud Captcha 2.0 | Un clic, deslizador, rompecabezas, restauraci\xf3n de imagen | Modo sin huellas (Traceless) en las ediciones Standard y Enterprise | Se ejecuta en Alibaba Cloud | Pago por uso: 0.005 CNY por llamada en China continental, 0.007 CNY fuera; tambi\xe9n disponibles ediciones de suscripci\xf3n y paquetes de recursos | S\xed; Casdoor verifica contra el endpoint de la regi\xf3n de Shangh\xe1i | `Aliyun Captcha` |\\n\\nDos notas sobre las filas de reCAPTCHA. Google ha trasladado reCAPTCHA a Google Cloud Fraud Defense con niveles Essentials, Premium y Enterprise, y la asignaci\xf3n gratuita de 10,000 evaluaciones se comparte entre toda la organizaci\xf3n de Google Cloud. Casdoor se comunica con reCAPTCHA mediante una clave de sitio y una clave secreta a trav\xe9s del endpoint est\xe1ndar `siteverify`, y carga el script desde `recaptcha.net` en lugar de `google.com`, tanto para el script del navegador como para la verificaci\xf3n del lado del servidor.\\n\\n## Cu\xe1l elegir {#which-one-to-pick}\\n\\n### SaaS global {#global-saas}\\n\\nComience con [Cloudflare Turnstile](/docs/provider/captcha/cloudflareTurnstile). El plan gratuito no mide los desaf\xedos, el modo Managed evita que la mayor\xeda de los usuarios hagan clic en nada, y no necesita enrutar su sitio a trav\xe9s de Cloudflare. Si ya opera en Google Cloud y desea las se\xf1ales de riesgo de reCAPTCHA, [reCAPTCHA](/docs/provider/captcha/recaptcha) est\xe1 bien, pero vigile las 10,000 evaluaciones gratuitas: una p\xe1gina de inicio de sesi\xf3n muy concurrida o un ataque de env\xedo masivo de SMS pueden agotarlas en un d\xeda. [hCaptcha](/docs/provider/captcha/hcaptcha) es otra elecci\xf3n com\xfan; su plan gratuito siempre muestra un desaf\xedo, y los modos pasivos son de pago.\\n\\n### Una aplicaci\xf3n con usuarios en China continental {#an-app-with-users-in-mainland-china}\\n\\nTurnstile queda descartado, seg\xfan la propia documentaci\xf3n de Cloudflare. Para reCAPTCHA, Google documenta `www.recaptcha.net` como el dominio a utilizar cuando `www.google.com` no es alcanzable, y Casdoor ya lo utiliza, pero a\xfan debe probarlo desde las redes de sus usuarios antes de depender de ello. hCaptcha no publica ninguna declaraci\xf3n sobre China, por lo que se aplica el mismo consejo.\\n\\nLas dos opciones seguras son [Alibaba Cloud Captcha](/docs/provider/captcha/aliyunCaptcha) y [GeeTest](/docs/provider/captcha/geetest). Alibaba Cloud publica precios por llamada y le permite comenzar con pago por uso; su modo sin huellas (en Casdoor, use el subtipo `Popup` para esto) se acerca a la experiencia invisible de Turnstile. GeeTest vende a trav\xe9s de su equipo de ventas despu\xe9s de una prueba de 30 d\xedas. Si atiende tanto a China como al resto del mundo desde un solo Casdoor, recuerde que una aplicaci\xf3n tiene un solo proveedor de captcha, as\xed que divida las audiencias en dos aplicaciones si necesitan diferentes captchas.\\n\\n### Usuarios sensibles a la privacidad en la UE {#privacy-sensitive-users-in-the-eu}\\n\\nTodos los captcha alojados env\xedan algunos datos sobre el navegador del visitante al proveedor. Si su equipo legal no desea ning\xfan tercero en la ruta de inicio de sesi\xf3n, el [captcha predeterminado](/docs/provider/captcha/default) de Casdoor es la \xfanica opci\xf3n de la tabla que mantiene todo en su propio servidor. Entre los alojados, Turnstile y hCaptcha describen la privacidad como parte del producto (datos limitados a lo necesario para la verificaci\xf3n, RGPD y el Marco de Privacidad de Datos UE-EE. UU., respectivamente). reCAPTCHA establece una cookie `_GRECAPTCHA`; Google indica que servir desde `www.recaptcha.net` evita otras cookies que `www.google.com` podr\xeda establecer. Independientemente de cu\xe1l elija, incl\xfayalo en su aviso de privacidad.\\n\\n### Autohospedado, intranet o sin conexi\xf3n {#self-hosted-intranet-or-offline}\\n\\nUse el captcha predeterminado. No requiere claves ni acceso a la red externa, por lo que funciona en instalaciones aisladas (air-gapped). Comb\xednelo con la regla `Internet-Only` (m\xe1s abajo) para que las personas en la red de la oficina nunca lo vean, mientras que las solicitudes desde internet p\xfablico s\xed lo hagan.\\n\\nEl captcha predeterminado es el m\xe1s d\xe9bil del grupo frente a solucionadores automatizados, ya que es una imagen simple de d\xedgitos distorsionados. Para una p\xe1gina de inicio de sesi\xf3n p\xfablica con mucho tr\xe1fico, comb\xednela con [autenticaci\xf3n multifactor](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/mfa-methods-compared) o mueva a los usuarios a [inicio de sesi\xf3n sin contrase\xf1a](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/passwordless-authentication), lo que elimina la adivinanza de contrase\xf1as que los captchas existen para ralentizar.\\n\\n## C\xf3mo funciona CAPTCHA en Casdoor {#how-captcha-works-in-casdoor}\\n\\nAgregue un proveedor con **Category** establecido en `Captcha` y uno de estos tipos: `Default`, `reCAPTCHA`, `reCAPTCHA v2`, `reCAPTCHA v3`, `hCaptcha`, `Cloudflare Turnstile`, `GEETEST` o `Aliyun Captcha`. Luego agr\xe9guelo a una aplicaci\xf3n en la pesta\xf1a **Providers**. Una aplicaci\xf3n solo puede contener un proveedor de captcha; Casdoor se niega a guardar un segundo. Los pasos de configuraci\xf3n para cada proveedor est\xe1n en la [documentaci\xf3n del proveedor de captcha](/docs/provider/captcha/overview).\\n\\n### D\xf3nde solicita Casdoor {#where-casdoor-asks-for-it}\\n\\nEl mismo proveedor protege varias acciones, y Casdoor verifica el token en el servidor, no solo en el navegador:\\n\\n- Inicio de sesi\xf3n con contrase\xf1a.\\n- Env\xedo del formulario de registro.\\n- Env\xedo de un c\xf3digo de verificaci\xf3n por correo electr\xf3nico o SMS, lo que cubre el paso de c\xf3digo del registro, la p\xe1gina de recuperaci\xf3n de contrase\xf1a y el inicio de sesi\xf3n basado en c\xf3digos. Esta es la protecci\xf3n contra bots que agotan su presupuesto de SMS.\\n\\n### Cu\xe1ndo solicita Casdoor {#when-casdoor-asks-for-it}\\n\\nLa **Rule** del proveedor de captcha en la aplicaci\xf3n decide cu\xe1ndo aparece:\\n\\n| Rule         | Behavior                                                               |\\n|---|---|\\n| `None` | Nunca; el captcha est\xe1 desactivado |\\n| `Dynamic` | Solo despu\xe9s de que los inicios de sesi\xf3n fallidos del usuario alcancen el **Failed signin limit** de la aplicaci\xf3n |\\n| `Always` | Cada vez |\\n| `Internet-Only` | Para solicitudes desde direcciones IP p\xfablicas; no para direcciones privadas, de bucle invertido u otras no p\xfablicas |\\n\\n`Dynamic` es la configuraci\xf3n m\xe1s suave para el inicio de sesi\xf3n: los usuarios honestos nunca ven un desaf\xedo, y alguien que adivina contrase\xf1as encuentra uno despu\xe9s de unos pocos intentos fallidos. Sin embargo, solo se aplica a cuentas que ya existen. Un registro, o un c\xf3digo enviado a una direcci\xf3n que a\xfan no tiene cuenta, no lo activa. Si el abuso de SMS o correo electr\xf3nico en el registro es su preocupaci\xf3n, use `Always` o `Internet-Only`.\\n\\n### D\xf3nde aparece en la p\xe1gina {#where-it-appears-on-the-page}\\n\\nEn los elementos de inicio de sesi\xf3n de la aplicaci\xf3n, el elemento **Captcha** tiene su propia regla: `Pop up` muestra el captcha en un cuadro de di\xe1logo cuando es necesario, e `Inline` lo coloca directamente en la p\xe1gina. Consulte [Cambiar el captcha](/docs/application/signin-items-table#captcha-rules). Alibaba Cloud Captcha tiene un **Sub type** adicional: `Embed` lo muestra dentro del cuadro de di\xe1logo de Casdoor, y `Popup` entrega el control a la ventana emergente propia de Alibaba Cloud, que es lo que necesitan las escenas sin huellas (traceless).\\n\\nUse el bot\xf3n **Preview** en la p\xe1gina del proveedor para verificar las claves antes de activar la regla.\\n\\n## Referencias {#references}\\n\\n- Versiones de Google reCAPTCHA: https://developers.google.com/recaptcha/docs/versions\\n- Preguntas frecuentes de Google reCAPTCHA (`recaptcha.net`, cookie `_GRECAPTCHA`): https://developers.google.com/recaptcha/docs/faq\\n- Facturaci\xf3n de reCAPTCHA en Google Cloud Fraud Defense (verificado 2026-11-02): https://docs.cloud.google.com/recaptcha/docs/billing-information\\n- Precios de hCaptcha (verificado 2026-11-02): https://www.hcaptcha.com/pricing\\n- Pol\xedtica de privacidad de hCaptcha: https://www.hcaptcha.com/privacy\\n- Resumen de Cloudflare Turnstile: https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/\\n- Planes de Cloudflare Turnstile (verificado 2026-11-02): https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/plans/\\n- Preguntas frecuentes de Cloudflare China Network (Turnstile en China continental): https://developers.cloudflare.com/china-network/faq/\\n- GeeTest CAPTCHA v4 (verificado 2026-11-02): https://www.geetest.com/adaptive-captcha\\n- Resumen de Alibaba Cloud Captcha 2.0: https://help.aliyun.com/zh/captcha/captcha2-0/product-overview/what-is-alibaba-cloud-captcha-2\\n- Ediciones y precios de Alibaba Cloud Captcha 2.0 (verificado 2026-11-02): https://help.aliyun.com/zh/captcha/captcha2-0/product-overview/version-description"},{"id":"customize-login-page","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/customize-login-page","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-customize-login-page.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-customize-login-page.mdx","title":"Customize the Login Page in Casdoor: Custom Styling of the Authentication UI Without Forking","description":"Style Casdoor\'s hosted login and sign-up pages without forking: themes, CSS, HTML, logo, background, form position, sign-in fields and organization choice.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Login Page","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/login-page"},{"inline":true,"label":"UI Customization","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/ui-customization"},{"inline":true,"label":"CSS","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/css"},{"inline":true,"label":"Themes","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/themes"},{"inline":true,"label":"Branding","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/branding"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":9.28,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"customize-login-page","title":"Customize the Login Page in Casdoor: Custom Styling of the Authentication UI Without Forking","description":"Style Casdoor\'s hosted login and sign-up pages without forking: themes, CSS, HTML, logo, background, form position, sign-in fields and organization choice.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Login Page","UI Customization","CSS","Themes","Branding","IAM","SSO"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Comparaci\xf3n de proveedores CAPTCHA para p\xe1ginas de inicio de sesi\xf3n: reCAPTCHA, hCaptcha, Turnstile, GeeTest y Alibaba Cloud en Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/captcha-for-login-pages"},"nextItem":{"title":"Jenkins OIDC, Gitea OIDC and Grafana SSO with Casdoor: One Login for a Self-Hosted DevOps Stack","permalink":"/es/blog/devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana"}},"content":"Yes, Casdoor supports custom styling of its authentication UI, and you don\'t need to fork the frontend to get it. Every application has a **UI Customization** tab where you set the logo, favicon, background image, custom CSS for desktop and mobile, the position of the form, a side panel, footer HTML and a theme. Two tables, **Signin items** and **Signup items**, decide which fields the login and sign-up pages show, in which order, with which labels and placeholders. All of it is stored per application in the database, so one Casdoor instance can serve many differently branded login pages, and organization admins can edit their own pages within safe limits.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What to change and where\\n\\n| What you want to change | Where in Casdoor | Docs |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Primary color, corner radius, dark or compact style | **Theme** of the organization, or of the application with **Follow organization theme** off | [Customize the theme](/docs/organization/customize-theme) |\\n| A complete ready-made look | **Identity \u2192 Marketplace**, sign-in themes | [Marketplace](/docs/marketplace/overview) |\\n| Logo, dark logo, favicon, page title | Application edit page | [Application settings](/docs/application/terminology) |\\n| Background image (desktop and phone) | **Background URL**, **Background URL Mobile** | [UI customization](/docs/application/ui-customization#1-background-image) |\\n| Look of the form | **Custom CSS**, **Custom CSS Mobile** | [UI customization](/docs/application/ui-customization#2-login-panel-style) |\\n| Form on the left, center or right, or next to a panel | **Form position**, **Side panel HTML** | [UI customization](/docs/application/ui-customization#3-panel-position) |\\n| Text below the form | **Footer HTML** | [Application settings](/docs/application/terminology#custom-html) |\\n| Which fields the login page shows, order, placeholders | **Signin items** | [Sign-in items table](/docs/application/signin-items-table) |\\n| Password, code, magic link, WebAuthn, LDAP and their order | **Signin methods** | [Sign-in methods](/docs/application/signin-methods) |\\n| Sign-up fields, required flags, labels, regex | **Signup items** | [Sign-up items table](/docs/application/signup-items-table) |\\n| Language selector | **Languages** sign-in item, **Languages** of the organization | [Sign-in items table](/docs/application/signin-items-table#language-selector-rules) |\\n| Let users pick or type their organization | **Org choice mode** | [Specify login organization](/docs/application/specify-login-organization) |\\n\\nIn the Chinese console the tab is called \u754C\u9762\u5B9A\u5236 and the sign-in table \u767B\u5F55\u9879, so if you are looking for how to change the \u767B\u5F55\u754C\u9762 of an application, start there.\\n\\n## Themes: organization, application and Marketplace\\n\\nThe quickest change is the theme. A theme in Casdoor is a small object with a style (Default, Dark, Document or Blossom in the editor), a primary color, a border radius and a compact switch. You can set it on the organization, where it applies to the console for its members and to the entry pages of every application that follows the organization theme, or on a single application by turning off **Follow organization theme**. The application edit page has a live preview, so you can see the result before you save.\\n\\nIf you want more than colors, the [Casdoor Marketplace](/docs/marketplace/overview) has sign-in themes. Installing one writes the theme data, form position, form CSS for desktop and mobile, side panel HTML, footer HTML and background URLs of one application. The casdoor-integrations repository currently holds 24 themes, from Midnight and Split to Terminal, Nord and Solarized. Uninstalling a theme restores what the application had before, so trying one costs nothing. Browse them at [marketplace.casdoor.com](https://marketplace.casdoor.com).\\n\\n## Branding: logo, favicon and background\\n\\nEach application has its own **Logo**, **Favicon** and **Title**, separate from the organization. One organization can run several products that share users but show different logos. There is also an optional **Logo dark**, shown when the page is in dark mode; without it, Casdoor keeps using the normal logo.\\n\\n**Background URL** sets the image behind the form. Set **Background URL Mobile** too if the desktop image crops badly on phones. The preview updates as soon as the URL loads.\\n\\n## Custom CSS and form layout\\n\\n**Custom CSS** takes plain CSS rules; Casdoor wraps them in a style element itself, so don\'t add one of your own. The main containers to target are `.login-panel` and `.login-form`:\\n\\n```css\\n.login-panel {\\n  border-radius: 12px;\\n  background-color: #ffffff;\\n  box-shadow: 0 0 30px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2);\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**Custom CSS Mobile** holds rules for phones, so you don\'t need to write media queries for the common case.\\n\\n**Form position** places the form on the left, center or right. The fourth option, **Enable side panel**, centers the form and puts a panel next to it whose content comes from **Side panel HTML**. That field takes HTML, so a style element inside it is fine.\\n\\n**Footer HTML** is rendered below the sign-in and sign-up forms. If the application is the default application of its organization, its footer also replaces \\"Powered by Casdoor\\" in the admin console for that organization\'s users.\\n\\nWhen you are happy with a design on staging, **Export JSON** at the bottom of the application edit page copies the look (logo, favicon, backgrounds, CSS, form position, side panel, theme and the custom HTML fields) to the clipboard, and **Import JSON** on the same application on another instance applies it. The export contains no secrets or providers.\\n\\n## Fields, labels and order\\n\\nThe **Signin items** table lists every element of the login page: the logo, the username and password fields, the forgot-password link, the agreement checkbox, the language selector, the captcha, provider buttons and so on. For each row you can set **Visible**, a custom **Placeholder**, per-item **Custom CSS** and a rule, and move it up or down. You can also add custom items whose label is HTML.\\n\\nA few rules worth knowing:\\n\\n- Hiding **Forgot password?** turns off password reset in the API too, not just the link.\\n- The **Captcha** rule switches between a pop-up and an inline captcha. If you are choosing a captcha provider, see [CAPTCHA providers compared for login pages](/blog/captcha-for-login-pages).\\n- The **Languages** rule switches between a globe icon and a labeled selector. The list itself is limited to the **Languages** of the organization, so an organization that only serves English and Japanese users shows only those two.\\n\\nThe sign-in methods (password, verification code, magic link, WebAuthn, LDAP, Face ID and others) are a separate table, **Signin methods**, with its own order and display names.\\n\\nThe **Signup items** table does the same for registration, with **Required**, **Regex** validation, custom **Label** and rules such as \\"No verification\\" for email. `POST /api/signup` also clears every field whose item is hidden, so API clients can\'t set fields your form doesn\'t show. **Prompted** asks users for missing fields, such as a phone number, after they sign in through a provider that didn\'t supply one.\\n\\n## Specify the login organization\\n\\nIf several organizations share one Casdoor, users need a way to reach the right one. **Org choice mode** on the default application of an organization (or on `app-built-in`) adds an organization step to `/login`: **Input** shows a text field, **Select** shows a dropdown. After the user picks, Casdoor sends them to `/login/<organization>`, which shows that organization\'s own styled page. The browser remembers the last organization in `localStorage`, so a user whose console session expired lands on their own login page again.\\n\\n## What tenant admins can safely change\\n\\nLetting every customer style their own login page raises an obvious question: what stops an organization admin from adding a script that copies passwords, or a CSS selector that leaks each typed character through background image requests? Casdoor checks this on the server. When an admin who is not a global admin adds or saves an application, every custom HTML and CSS field that changed is validated, and the save is rejected if any of them breaks the rules:\\n\\n- HTML may only use layout and text elements such as `div`, `span`, `p`, headings, lists, links, images, tables, `style` and basic SVG shapes. `script`, `form`, `input` and `iframe` are refused, as are `on...` event handlers, `srcset`, `formaction`, `srcdoc` and similar attributes. `href` is allowed only on links with relative, `http`, `https`, `mailto` or `tel` URLs.\\n- CSS may not contain backslashes, `<`, `image(`, `image-set(` and a few other functions. Every `url()` must be relative, a `data:` URL, or start with an allowed prefix: Google Fonts, Fontsource on jsDelivr, the Casdoor CDNs, this Casdoor\'s own origin, the domains of the global storage providers, or an entry of `formCssAllowedUrls`.\\n- `@import` is allowed only from Google Fonts and Fontsource, because an imported stylesheet isn\'t checked itself.\\n\\nTo let tenants load images from your own host, add it to `conf/app.conf` (see [Configuration](/docs/basic/configuration#backend-options)):\\n\\n```ini\\nformCssAllowedUrls = \\"https://img.example.com/\\"\\n```\\n\\nOn top of the save-time check, the sign-in pages sanitize custom HTML before rendering it, unless the application belongs to the `built-in` organization or a global admin has turned on **Enable custom scripts** for it. Only a global admin can turn that switch on, and while it is on only a global admin can change that application\'s HTML. The same rules apply when a tenant installs a Marketplace theme: a theme that breaks them can only be installed by a global admin.\\n\\n**Signin HTML** and **Signup HTML** deserve a separate note: they replace the whole built-in page, form included. Since tenants can\'t add forms or scripts, these two fields are mainly for global admins; for branding, CSS, the side panel and the footer are the better tools.\\n\\n## Hosted login page or your own UI?\\n\\nYou can build your own login screen on Casdoor\'s API, but the hosted page is usually the better choice. With OAuth 2.0 or OpenID Connect, users type their password only on the Casdoor origin and your application receives an authorization code, never the credentials. Captcha, MFA, social and enterprise providers, forgot-password, sign-up validation and the organization picker keep working without code on your side, and they keep getting fixes when you upgrade Casdoor. A custom UI has to reimplement each of those flows and puts passwords through your own servers. Guides for wiring the hosted page into an app: [Spring Boot SSO with OIDC](/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc) and [Nginx SSO authentication](/blog/nginx-sso-authentication).\\n\\nOther hosted providers make the same trade. Idura Verify, for example, lets you add a stylesheet URL and custom HTML around its login box from its dashboard, under a strict Content Security Policy with an explicit list of style origins for fonts and images. Casdoor\'s `formCssAllowedUrls` plays the same role, with the difference that Casdoor is open source and self-hosted, so you control the list.\\n\\n## Referencias {#references}\\n\\n- [Customize the look of the sign-in page](/docs/application/ui-customization)\\n- [Personalizar el tema](/docs/organization/customize-theme)\\n- [Sign-in items table](/docs/application/signin-items-table) and [Sign-up items table](/docs/application/signup-items-table)\\n- [Specify login organization](/docs/application/specify-login-organization)\\n- [Marketplace](/docs/marketplace/overview) and [casdoor/casdoor-integrations](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-integrations)\\n- Casdoor source: [`object/application_util.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/application_util.go) and [`object/application_html.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/application_html.go)\\n- Idura Verify: [Custom styling](https://docs.idura.app/verify/guides/custom-styling/)"},{"id":"devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana.mdx","title":"Jenkins OIDC, Gitea OIDC and Grafana SSO with Casdoor: One Login for a Self-Hosted DevOps Stack","description":"Use Casdoor as the OIDC provider for Gitea, Jenkins, Grafana, Argo CD, MinIO, Portainer, Harbor and GitLab: callback URLs, config keys and admin group mapping.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Jenkins","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/jenkins"},{"inline":true,"label":"Gitea","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/gitea"},{"inline":true,"label":"Grafana","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/grafana"},{"inline":true,"label":"Argo CD","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/argo-cd"},{"inline":true,"label":"MinIO","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/min-io"},{"inline":true,"label":"DevOps","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/dev-ops"}],"readingTime":9.15,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana","title":"Jenkins OIDC, Gitea OIDC and Grafana SSO with Casdoor: One Login for a Self-Hosted DevOps Stack","description":"Use Casdoor as the OIDC provider for Gitea, Jenkins, Grafana, Argo CD, MinIO, Portainer, Harbor and GitLab: callback URLs, config keys and admin group mapping.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","OIDC","SSO","Jenkins","Gitea","Grafana","Argo CD","MinIO","DevOps"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Customize the Login Page in Casdoor: Custom Styling of the Authentication UI Without Forking","permalink":"/es/blog/customize-login-page"},"nextItem":{"title":"DingTalk, WeCom, Lark, Infoflow and WeChat OAuth Login with Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/dingtalk-wecom-lark-login"}},"content":"Every tool in a typical self-hosted DevOps stack can act as an OpenID Connect client: Gitea and Argo CD speak OIDC natively, Jenkins does through the `oic-auth` plugin, Grafana and Portainer through generic OAuth 2.0, and MinIO, Harbor and GitLab through their OIDC settings. Point all of them at one Casdoor instance, give each tool its own Casdoor application, and put your people in Casdoor groups. Each tool then reads the `groups` claim and decides who is an administrator. This post lists the callback URL, the config keys and the group mapping for each tool, then covers the parts they share.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The stack at a glance\\n\\nAll paths below are relative to the tool\'s own public URL. Register them in **Redirect URLs** of the Casdoor application for that tool.\\n\\n| App | Protocol | Callback URL | Group/role to admin mapping | Casdoor doc |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Gitea | OIDC (OAuth2 auth source) | `/user/oauth2/<source-name>/callback` | Yes: admin group, restricted group, team map | [Gitea](/docs/integration/go/gitea) |\\n| Jenkins | OIDC (`oic-auth` plugin) | `/securityRealm/finishLogin`, logout `/OicLogout` | Yes: groups claim plus Matrix-based security | [Jenkins (OIDC)](/docs/integration/java/jenkins-oidc) |\\n| Grafana | Generic OAuth 2.0 | `/login/generic_oauth` | Yes: `role_attribute_path` | [Grafana](/docs/integration/go/grafana) |\\n| Argo CD | OIDC | `/auth/callback`, CLI `http://localhost:8085/auth/callback` | Yes: `argocd-rbac-cm` | [Argo CD](/docs/integration/go/argocd) |\\n| MinIO | OIDC | Console `/oauth_callback` | Policies from one claim (`claim_name`) | [MinIO](/docs/integration/go/minio) |\\n| Portainer | OAuth 2.0 (Custom) | The Portainer base URL | Business Edition only | [Portainer](/docs/integration/go/portainer) |\\n| Harbor | OIDC | `/c/oidc/callback` | Yes: OIDC Admin Group | none yet |\\n| GitLab | OIDC (OmniAuth) | `/users/auth/openid_connect/callback` | Premium and Ultimate: `admin_groups` | [GitLab](/docs/integration/ruby/gitlab) |\\n\\nThe discovery document every OIDC client needs is `https://<casdoor-host>/.well-known/openid-configuration`. If you have not connected an OIDC client to Casdoor before, [Connect a standard OIDC client](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client) explains the endpoints.\\n\\n## Prepare Casdoor once\\n\\nBefore touching the tools, set up three things in Casdoor.\\n\\n**Groups.** Create groups such as `devops-admins` and `devops-users` in the organization tree and add users to them. See [Groups](/docs/organization/organization-tree).\\n\\n**Token group format.** By default the `groups` claim names each group as `<organization>/<group>`. Most tools compare the claim with a plain string you type, so set **Token group format** on the **OIDC/OAuth** tab of each application to `Name (group)`. The claim then carries `devops-admins`. The options are described under [Group format](/docs/token/overview#token-group-format).\\n\\n**Token format.** Keep **Token format** at `JWT`, the default. In that format the ID token carries `groups`, `roles` and `tag`. `JWT-Empty` and `JWT-Standard` leave all three out of the token, and `JWT-Custom` carries them only if you select them in **Token fields**. The `/api/userinfo` response returns `groups` and the role names when the `profile` scope is granted, so tools that read userinfo still see them.\\n\\nRoles work too. In the `JWT` format the `roles` claim is an array of role objects, so match on `roles[*].name`. In `/api/userinfo` it is a plain array of names. See [User roles](/docs/user/roles).\\n\\n## Gitea\\n\\nIn **Site Administration**, add an authentication source of type **OAuth2** with provider **OpenID Connect**, the client ID and secret, and the Casdoor discovery URL. The name you give the source is part of the callback URL: a source named `casdoor` uses `https://gitea.example.com/user/oauth2/casdoor/callback`.\\n\\nFor group mapping, fill in **Claim name providing group names** with `groups`, then **Group Claim value for administrator users** with `devops-admins`. Gitea also has **Group Claim value for restricted users** and **Map claimed groups to Organization teams**. The same source from the CLI:\\n\\n```bash\\ngitea admin auth add-oauth \\\\\\n  --name casdoor \\\\\\n  --provider openidConnect \\\\\\n  --key <client-id> \\\\\\n  --secret <client-secret> \\\\\\n  --auto-discover-url https://door.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration \\\\\\n  --group-claim-name groups \\\\\\n  --admin-group devops-admins\\n```\\n\\n## Jenkins\\n\\nJenkins has no OIDC support of its own. Install the [OpenId Connect Authentication](https://plugins.jenkins.io/oic-auth/) plugin, choose it as the security realm, and use the well-known configuration. The login callback is `/securityRealm/finishLogin` and the logout callback is `/OicLogout`; add both to Casdoor. In JCasC:\\n\\n```yaml\\njenkins:\\n  securityRealm:\\n    oic:\\n      serverConfiguration:\\n        wellKnown:\\n          wellKnownOpenIDConfigurationUrl: https://door.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration\\n      clientId: <client-id>\\n      clientSecret: <client-secret>\\n      userNameField: name\\n      fullNameFieldName: displayName\\n      emailFieldName: email\\n      groupsFieldName: groups\\n      logoutFromOpenidProvider: true\\n      properties:\\n      - \\"pkce\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe plugin only passes the groups to Jenkins. Rights come from the authorization strategy: with Matrix-based security, each row of the table is a user or a group, so add a row for `devops-admins` and give it Overall/Administer. Choosing the plugin as the security realm turns off other login methods, so configure the plugin\'s `escapeHatch` property (a fallback username and secret) before you save. Casdoor also has its own Jenkins plugin, described in [Jenkins plugin](/docs/integration/java/jenkins-plugin), but the generic OIDC plugin is the one this post assumes.\\n\\n## Grafana\\n\\nGrafana uses generic OAuth. Its callback is `/login/generic_oauth`. Grafana\'s default `scopes` is `user:email`, so set the OIDC scopes explicitly:\\n\\n```ini\\n[auth.generic_oauth]\\nenabled = true\\nname = Casdoor\\nclient_id = <client-id>\\nclient_secret = <client-secret>\\nscopes = openid profile email\\nauth_url = https://door.example.com/login/oauth/authorize\\ntoken_url = https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/access_token\\napi_url = https://door.example.com/api/userinfo\\nuse_pkce = true\\ngroups_attribute_path = groups\\nrole_attribute_path = contains(groups[*], \'devops-admins\') && \'Admin\' || contains(groups[*], \'devops-users\') && \'Editor\' || \'Viewer\'\\nrole_attribute_strict = true\\n```\\n\\nGrafana evaluates `role_attribute_path` against the ID token first, then the userinfo response, then the access token. Valid results are `None`, `Viewer`, `Editor`, `Admin` and `GrafanaAdmin`; the last one needs `allow_assign_grafana_admin = true`. `allowed_groups` limits login to listed groups and requires `groups_attribute_path`. Team sync is a Grafana Enterprise and Grafana Cloud feature.\\n\\n## Argo CD\\n\\nArgo CD talks to Casdoor directly, without its bundled Dex. The browser callback is `/auth/callback`. `argocd login --sso` starts a local server on port 8085 by default and uses `http://localhost:8085/auth/callback`, so register that as well.\\n\\nSet `url` and `oidc.config` (with `name`, `issuer`, `clientID`, `clientSecret`, `requestedScopes`) in `argocd-cm`. Argo CD\'s default `requestedScopes` adds a `groups` scope. Casdoor does not need it, because the `JWT` ID token always carries `groups`, so our guide requests `[\\"openid\\", \\"profile\\", \\"email\\"]`. Groups are mapped in `argocd-rbac-cm`, where `scopes` defaults to `[groups]`:\\n\\n```yaml\\ndata:\\n  policy.default: role:readonly\\n  policy.csv: |\\n    g, devops-admins, role:admin\\n```\\n\\nThe full walkthrough, including the client secret in `argocd-secret`, is in the [Argo CD](/docs/integration/go/argocd) guide.\\n\\n## MinIO\\n\\nMinIO reads policies from a single claim, set with `claim_name` (default `policy`). It does not map groups. MinIO\'s own Casdoor quickstart and our guide use Casdoor\'s `tag` claim: put a MinIO policy name such as `readwrite` in the user\'s **Tag**, then:\\n\\n```bash\\nmc admin config set myminio identity_openid \\\\\\n  config_url=\\"https://door.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration\\" \\\\\\n  client_id=<client-id> client_secret=<client-secret> claim_name=\\"tag\\"\\n```\\n\\n`role_policy` is the alternative: every user who signs in through this provider gets the listed policies, and it cannot be combined with `claim_name`. The Console callback is `<console-url>/oauth_callback`. Behind a proxy, set `MINIO_BROWSER_REDIRECT_URL`; the old `redirect_uri` key is deprecated. Note that the `minio/minio` GitHub repository has been archived and its README says it is no longer maintained, so check which MinIO build you run before relying on these settings.\\n\\n## Portainer\\n\\nPortainer uses a Custom OAuth provider. Fill in **Authorization URL** (`/login/oauth/authorize`), **Access token URL** (`/api/login/oauth/access_token`) and **Resource URL** (`/api/userinfo`) on your Casdoor host, and set **Redirect URL** to the Portainer base URL. There is also a **Logout URL** field. In Portainer CE, **Automatic team membership** is marked as a Business Edition feature, and the setting that assigns admin rights to groups is only in Business Edition. On CE, users created by automatic provisioning get the standard user role and you promote admins by hand.\\n\\n## Harbor and GitLab\\n\\nHarbor shows its redirect URI at the bottom of the OIDC settings page: `<harbor-url>/c/oidc/callback`. Set **OIDC Provider Endpoint** to the Casdoor URL, **Group Claim Name** to `groups`, and **OIDC Admin Group** to `devops-admins`. Harbor reads the group claim from the ID token, which the `JWT` format provides. You can only switch Harbor from database to OIDC authentication before any local users exist.\\n\\nGitLab\'s OIDC docs have a Casdoor section and require Casdoor to be served over HTTPS. The callback is `/users/auth/openid_connect/callback`. Group-based roles (`groups_attribute`, `admin_groups`, `required_groups`, `external_groups`) need GitLab Premium or Ultimate.\\n\\n## Tools without OIDC\\n\\nFor internal dashboards with no login of their own, put them behind Apache APISIX\'s `openid-connect` plugin (`discovery`, `client_id`, `client_secret`, `redirect_uri`, `bearer_only`), as in the [APISIX guide](/docs/integration/lua/apisix), or behind nginx as described in [Nginx SSO authentication](/blog/nginx-sso-authentication).\\n\\n## One application per tool or a shared one\\n\\nA single Casdoor application with every tool\'s callback in its Redirect URLs works, but one application per tool is easier to run:\\n\\n- Each tool gets its own client secret, so a leaked Jenkins secret does not expose Grafana.\\n- **Token group format**, **Token format** and token expiry are per application, so you can give Argo CD `Name (group)` without changing another tool\'s claims.\\n- Casdoor checks `post_logout_redirect_uri` against the Redirect URLs of the application, so each list stays short and specific.\\n\\nUsers still sign in once: after the first login, Casdoor\'s session cookie lets the next tool\'s redirect complete without a password prompt.\\n\\n## Cierre de sesi\xf3n {#logout}\\n\\nCasdoor\'s end-session endpoint is `/api/logout`, listed as `end_session_endpoint` in the discovery document. It accepts `id_token_hint`, `post_logout_redirect_uri`, `client_id` and `state`, and only redirects to a URL listed in the application\'s Redirect URLs. Signing out of Casdoor ends the Casdoor session and the tokens of that session, but each tool also keeps its own session cookie. Wire up logout in each tool:\\n\\n- Jenkins: `logoutFromOpenidProvider`, with `/OicLogout` registered.\\n- Grafana: `signout_redirect_url`.\\n- Argo CD: `logoutURL` in `oidc.config`, for example `https://door.example.com/api/logout?id_token_hint={{token}}&post_logout_redirect_uri={{logoutRedirectURL}}`.\\n- Harbor: **OIDC Session Logout**.\\n- Portainer: **Logout URL**.\\n\\nThe details of the endpoint are in [RP-initiated logout](/docs/session/rp-initiated-logout).\\n\\n## Referencias {#references}\\n\\n- Gitea: [Authentication](https://docs.gitea.com/usage/authentication), [`admin auth add-oauth`](https://docs.gitea.com/administration/command-line)\\n- Jenkins: [OpenId Connect Authentication plugin](https://plugins.jenkins.io/oic-auth/), [configuration reference](https://github.com/jenkinsci/oic-auth-plugin/blob/master/docs/configuration/README.md), [Managing security](https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/security/managing-security/)\\n- Grafana: [Configure generic OAuth2 authentication](https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/setup-grafana/configure-access/configure-authentication/generic-oauth/)\\n- Argo CD: [User management](https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/user-management/), [RBAC](https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/rbac/), [`argocd login`](https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/commands/argocd_login/)\\n- MinIO: [Casdoor quickstart in the MinIO repository](https://github.com/minio/minio/blob/master/docs/sts/casdoor.md)\\n- Portainer: [OAuth authentication](https://docs.portainer.io/admin/settings/authentication/oauth)\\n- Harbor: [Configure OIDC provider authentication](https://goharbor.io/docs/main/administration/configure-authentication/oidc-auth/)\\n- GitLab: [Use OpenID Connect as an authentication provider](https://docs.gitlab.com/administration/auth/oidc/)\\n- Apache APISIX: [openid-connect plugin](https://apisix.apache.org/docs/apisix/plugins/openid-connect/)"},{"id":"dingtalk-wecom-lark-login","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/dingtalk-wecom-lark-login","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-dingtalk-wecom-lark-login.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-dingtalk-wecom-lark-login.mdx","title":"DingTalk, WeCom, Lark, Infoflow and WeChat OAuth Login with Casdoor","description":"Sign in with DingTalk, WeCom, Lark/Feishu, Baidu Infoflow and WeChat through Casdoor: app types, QR login, user IDs, email access, corporate tokens, sync.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"DingTalk","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/ding-talk"},{"inline":true,"label":"WeCom","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/we-com"},{"inline":true,"label":"Lark","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/lark"},{"inline":true,"label":"Feishu","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/feishu"},{"inline":true,"label":"WeChat","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/we-chat"},{"inline":true,"label":"Infoflow","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/infoflow"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":9.94,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"dingtalk-wecom-lark-login","title":"DingTalk, WeCom, Lark, Infoflow and WeChat OAuth Login with Casdoor","description":"Sign in with DingTalk, WeCom, Lark/Feishu, Baidu Infoflow and WeChat through Casdoor: app types, QR login, user IDs, email access, corporate tokens, sync.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","DingTalk","WeCom","Lark","Feishu","WeChat","Infoflow","OAuth","SSO"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Jenkins OIDC, Gitea OIDC and Grafana SSO with Casdoor: One Login for a Self-Hosted DevOps Stack","permalink":"/es/blog/devops-sso-gitea-jenkins-grafana"},"nextItem":{"title":"Email Provider for Casdoor Verification Codes: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, ACS Email and SES","permalink":"/es/blog/email-providers-for-verification-codes"}},"content":"Casdoor has built-in OAuth providers for DingTalk, WeCom (WeChat Work), Lark (Feishu), Baidu Infoflow and WeChat. You create an app in each vendor\'s developer console, paste its credentials into a Casdoor provider, and add the provider to your application. Casdoor runs the vendor flow (QR scan on a desktop, or sign-in inside the vendor\'s own client), fetches the corporate access token where the vendor needs one, and maps the vendor\'s user ID to a Casdoor user. Your application only talks OIDC to Casdoor and never sees a DingTalk header, a WeCom `user_ticket` or a WeChat `unionid`. For DingTalk, WeCom and Lark, a syncer can also import the whole org directory ahead of the first sign-in.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## De un vistazo {#at-a-glance}\\n\\n| Provider | App you create | Where users sign in | ID that links the Casdoor user | Email and phone | Directory syncer |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| DingTalk | Internal app (AppKey, AppSecret) | DingTalk sign-in page, `login.dingtalk.com` | `openId`; `unionid` becomes the username | From the profile, replaced by the corporate directory; treated as verified | Yes |\\n| WeCom | Self-built app (CorpID, Secret, AgentId), or third-party | Web login (QR, or quick login from WeCom desktop) or silent sign-in inside WeCom | `userid` (internal), `open_userid` (third-party) | Email only with `snsapi_privateinfo`; phone not read | Yes |\\n| Lark / Feishu | Custom app (App ID, App Secret) | Lark sign-in page, Feishu or Lark global | `open_id`; username is `user_id`, else `union_id`, else `open_id` | Yes, if the app has the contact permissions | Yes |\\n| Baidu Infoflow | Internal app (CorpID, Secret, AgentID) | Infoflow authorize page | `userid`, also the username | Email | No |\\n| WeChat | Website app on the Open Platform, plus an Official Account | QR scan on a PC; inside the WeChat browser through the Official Account | `unionid`, else `openid` | Neither | No |\\n\\nThe setup steps for each are in the docs: [DingTalk](/docs/provider/oauth/DingTalk), [WeCom](/docs/provider/oauth/weCom), [Lark](/docs/provider/oauth/lark), [Infoflow](/docs/provider/oauth/infoflow) and [WeChat](/docs/provider/oauth/Wechat). This post explains what happens behind each one.\\n\\n## What your application sees\\n\\nEvery provider in the table ends the same way. Casdoor exchanges the vendor\'s code, finds or creates the user in the organization, and then issues its own authorization code to your app. Your app redeems it at Casdoor\'s token endpoint and gets a Casdoor ID token and access token, signed with the application\'s certificate. The claims come from the Casdoor user record: name, display name, email, phone, avatar, groups.\\n\\nYour backend code is the same for all five. Any OIDC library works; see the [OAuth 2.0 and OIDC reference](/docs/how-to-connect/oauth) and, for a full walkthrough, [Spring Boot SSO with OIDC](/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc). If you do need the vendor\'s own API later, Casdoor stores the provider\'s access token on the user, as described in [OAuth providers](/docs/provider/oauth/overview#using-the-providers-access-token).\\n\\n## DingTalk\\n\\nCreate an internal app on the DingTalk Open Platform and copy its AppKey and AppSecret into the Client ID and Client secret of the provider. The sign-in page sends the user to `https://login.dingtalk.com/oauth2/auth` with `scope=openid` and `prompt=login consent`. After the user confirms, Casdoor makes four calls:\\n\\n1. `POST https://api.dingtalk.com/v1.0/oauth2/userAccessToken` turns the code into a user access token.\\n2. `GET https://api.dingtalk.com/v1.0/contact/users/me` returns the user\'s `openId`, `unionId`, nick, avatar, email and mobile. This needs the **Contact.User.Read** permission on the app.\\n3. `POST https://api.dingtalk.com/v1.0/oauth2/accessToken` with `appKey` and `appSecret` returns the corporate (app) access token.\\n4. With that token, `topapi/user/getbyunionid` maps the `unionid` to the corporate `userid`, and `topapi/v2/user/get` reads the directory record.\\n\\nIf the user is not a member of the organization that owns the app, step 4 fails with DingTalk error 60121 and the sign-in stops. If the directory lookup succeeds, the directory email and mobile replace the personal ones, `unionid` becomes the username, and the job title is kept as an extra claim.\\n\\n### The corporate access token and its header\\n\\nDingTalk has two API generations that take the same token in different places:\\n\\n```http\\nGET https://api.dingtalk.com/v1.0/contact/users/me\\nx-acs-dingtalk-access-token: <token>\\n```\\n\\n```http\\nPOST https://oapi.dingtalk.com/topapi/v2/user/get?access_token=<token>\\nContent-Type: application/json\\n\\n{\\"userid\\": \\"<userid>\\"}\\n```\\n\\nThe new API on `api.dingtalk.com` reads the token only from the `x-acs-dingtalk-access-token` header. Call it without that header (or with `Authorization: Bearer`) and it answers `AuthenticationFailed.MissingParameter`, naming the header. The older `oapi.dingtalk.com` endpoints still take an `access_token` query parameter. Casdoor uses both: the header for `users/me`, the query parameter for the two `topapi` calls, with the corporate token from `v1.0/oauth2/accessToken` in both places. DingTalk documents that token as valid for 7,200 seconds and asks callers to cache it per app. DingTalk also marks the old `gettoken` endpoint as legacy for new apps.\\n\\nCasdoor counts the email and phone that DingTalk returns as verified, so the default binding rule (email, then phone) can attach a DingTalk sign-in to an existing user. See [how Casdoor links accounts](/docs/provider/oauth/overview#automatic-account-linking).\\n\\n## WeCom (WeChat Work)\\n\\nA WeCom provider has a **Sub type** and a **Method**:\\n\\n- **Internal** with **Normal** sends the user to the WeCom web login, `login.work.weixin.qq.com/wwlogin/sso/login` with `login_type=CorpApp`. WeCom shows a QR code, or a one-click quick login when WeCom desktop is running and signed in.\\n- **Internal** with **Silent** uses `open.weixin.qq.com/connect/oauth2/authorize` with the app\'s `agentid`. This is the one to use for pages opened inside the WeCom client.\\n- **Third-party** uses `login_type=ServiceApp` and the service provider\'s secret (`get_provider_token`), then `get_login_info`, and links the user by `open_userid`.\\n\\nFor internal apps, Casdoor gets the corporate token from `cgi-bin/gettoken` with the CorpID and the app Secret, then exchanges the code for the member\'s `userid`. A visitor who is not a member comes back with an `openid` instead, and Casdoor rejects the sign-in. The token is a query parameter on every WeCom call; WeCom says it normally lasts 7,200 seconds and that `gettoken` is rate limited.\\n\\nThe email address is the part to plan for. WeCom stopped returning avatar, gender, mobile and email from the member read API (`user/get`) for self-built apps created after 20:00 on 2022-06-20. To get the email, set the provider\'s scope to `snsapi_privateinfo`. WeCom then returns a `user_ticket`, which Casdoor redeems at `getuserdetail`. WeCom requires the admin to pick the sensitive fields in the app settings and the member to consent, and it never gives these fields to third-party apps. Casdoor does not read the phone number from WeCom.\\n\\n## Lark and Feishu\\n\\nCreate a custom app on the [Feishu Open Platform](https://open.feishu.cn/) or the Lark global platform and copy the App ID and App Secret. Turn on **Use global endpoint** in the provider for Lark (`open.larksuite.com`); leave it off for Feishu (`open.feishu.cn`).\\n\\nCasdoor first gets a `tenant_access_token` from `auth/v3/tenant_access_token/internal`, then posts the code to `authen/v1/access_token` with that token in an `Authorization: Bearer` header. The response carries `open_id`, `union_id`, `user_id`, email, enterprise email and mobile. Feishu returns the sensitive ones only when the app holds the matching permission:\\n\\n| Field | Feishu permission |\\n|---|---|\\n| `user_id` | `contact:user.employee_id:readonly` |\\n| `email` | `contact:user.email:readonly` |\\n| `enterprise_email` | `contact:user.employee:readonly` |\\n| `mobile` | `contact:user.phone:readonly` |\\n\\nWithout them, sign-in still works: the username falls back from `user_id` to `union_id` to `open_id`. Casdoor uses `enterprise_email` when `email` is empty and treats both email and phone as verified. Feishu now lists the v1 `access_token` endpoint as a historical version, but it is still served.\\n\\n## Baidu Infoflow\\n\\nInfoflow (\u5982\u6D41) is Baidu\'s workplace app. Register an app in the Infoflow admin, give it address book permissions, and copy the CorpID, Secret and AgentID into the provider with the **Internal** sub type. The sign-in page goes to `https://xpc.im.baidu.com/oauth2/authorize`. Casdoor then calls `qy.im.baidu.com/api/gettoken` with the CorpID and Secret, exchanges the code and AgentID for the member\'s `userid` at `api/user/getuserinfo`, and reads name, avatar and email from `api/user/get`. The `userid` becomes both the linked ID and the username, and the email counts as verified. There is no Infoflow syncer, so users are created on first sign-in.\\n\\n## WeChat\\n\\nWeChat login has two halves, and you usually need both:\\n\\n- **Web** sub type: a website app on the WeChat Open Platform. The PC sign-in page shows a QR code from `open.weixin.qq.com/connect/qrconnect` with `scope=snsapi_login`; the user scans it with the WeChat app.\\n- **Mobile** sub type, or the second credential pair of the same provider: an Official Account. Inside the WeChat browser, a QR code is useless, so Casdoor switches to `connect/oauth2/authorize` with `snsapi_userinfo` and the Official Account\'s AppID. If no Official Account is configured, Casdoor tells the user to open the page in an external browser.\\n\\nWeChat returns no email or phone, only `openid`, `unionid`, nickname and avatar. Casdoor links the user by `unionid` when WeChat sends one, falling back to `openid`, and also stores the per-app `openid`. WeChat gives a user the same `unionid` across all apps and Official Accounts under one Open Platform account, so bind your website app and your Official Account to the same Open Platform account. Otherwise a user who scans on a PC and later signs in on a phone becomes two Casdoor users.\\n\\n## Importing the directory first\\n\\nFor DingTalk, WeCom and Lark, a [syncer](/docs/syncer/overview) can pull users and departments in on a schedule, so accounts exist before anyone signs in and departments become Casdoor groups. Each one uses a corporate token from the vendor\'s directory API: the [DingTalk syncer](/docs/syncer/DingTalk) walks departments recursively, the [WeCom syncer](/docs/syncer/WeCom) falls back to `user/list_id` and `user/get` for apps created after 2022-08-15, and the [Lark syncer](/docs/syncer/Lark) has a **Lark platform** setting to choose Feishu or Lark global.\\n\\nThe WeCom syncer writes the `userid` that the WeCom provider links by, so synced users sign in directly. The DingTalk and Lark syncers store the directory `userid` / `user_id`, while sign-in links by `openId` / `open_id`. Their first sign-in matches through the binding rule on email or phone instead, which works because both providers report those as verified.\\n\\n## QR login and account recovery\\n\\nQR login moves the password problem to the phone: if a user loses their WeChat or DingTalk account, they cannot scan. Because each of these identities is linked to a Casdoor user rather than being the user, the user can still sign in with a verification code or reset the password through the [forgot-password flow](/docs/application/signin-items-table#forgot-password-flow), as long as the application has an email or SMS provider and the user has an address on file. DingTalk and Lark fill that in from the directory; for WeChat, ask for a phone number at sign-up. To show more than one of these options on the page, see [sign-in methods](/docs/application/signin-methods).\\n\\n## Referencias {#references}\\n\\n- DingTalk: get a user token (`userAccessToken`, `expireIn` 7200): https://open.dingtalk.com/document/isvapp-server/obtain-user-token\\n- DingTalk: get the access token of an internal app: https://open.dingtalk.com/document/development/obtain-orgapp-token\\n- DingTalk: obtain user personal information: https://open.dingtalk.com/document/orgapp-server/tutorial-obtaining-user-personal-information\\n- WeCom: get access_token: https://developer.work.weixin.qq.com/document/path/91039\\n- WeCom: web login component (`wwlogin/sso/login`): https://developer.work.weixin.qq.com/document/path/98152\\n- WeCom: build the OAuth link and scopes: https://developer.work.weixin.qq.com/document/path/91022\\n- WeCom: get sensitive member info (`getuserdetail`): https://developer.work.weixin.qq.com/document/path/95833\\n- WeCom: read member (2022-06-20 field change): https://developer.work.weixin.qq.com/document/path/90196\\n- Feishu: get user_access_token (v1) and field permissions: https://open.feishu.cn/document/server-docs/authentication-management/access-token/create-2\\n- WeChat: website app login: https://developers.weixin.qq.com/doc/oplatform/Website_App/WeChat_Login/Wechat_Login.html\\n- WeChat: UnionID: https://developers.weixin.qq.com/doc/oplatform/Website_App/WeChat_Login/Authorized_Interface_Calling_UnionID.html\\n- Casdoor providers: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/idp"},{"id":"email-providers-for-verification-codes","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/email-providers-for-verification-codes","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-email-providers-for-verification-codes.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-email-providers-for-verification-codes.mdx","title":"Email Provider for Casdoor Verification Codes: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, ACS Email and SES","description":"Pick an email provider for Casdoor sign-up codes, password resets and magic links: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, Azure ACS Email, Amazon SES and plain SMTP.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Email","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/email"},{"inline":true,"label":"SMTP","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/smtp"},{"inline":true,"label":"Brevo","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/brevo"},{"inline":true,"label":"Resend","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/resend"},{"inline":true,"label":"SendGrid","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/send-grid"},{"inline":true,"label":"Azure","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/azure"},{"inline":true,"label":"Deliverability","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/deliverability"}],"readingTime":9.77,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"email-providers-for-verification-codes","title":"Email Provider for Casdoor Verification Codes: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, ACS Email and SES","description":"Pick an email provider for Casdoor sign-up codes, password resets and magic links: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, Azure ACS Email, Amazon SES and plain SMTP.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Email","SMTP","Brevo","Resend","SendGrid","Azure","Deliverability"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"DingTalk, WeCom, Lark, Infoflow and WeChat OAuth Login with Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/dingtalk-wecom-lark-login"},"nextItem":{"title":"How to Host Static Files on a CDN with Casdoor: S3, MinIO, Azure Blob and Other Storage Providers","permalink":"/es/blog/file-storage-and-cdn"}},"content":"Casdoor sends sign-up verification codes, password reset links and magic links through an Email provider. It has native API types for **Resend**, **SendGrid** and **Azure Communication Services (ACS) Email**, a `Default` SMTP type that works with any SMTP relay (your own server, Google Workspace, Microsoft 365, **Brevo**, **Amazon SES**), and a `Custom HTTP Email` type for any other HTTP API. If you want a free tier and a short setup, start with Resend or Brevo. If you already run on AWS or Azure, SES or ACS keeps billing in one place. Whichever you pick, the deliverability work is the same: a sender domain with SPF and DKIM that align with the From address.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Las opciones de un vistazo {#the-options-at-a-glance}\\n\\nFree tiers and prices were checked on 2026-11-02 on each vendor\'s own pricing page.\\n\\n| Provider | API or SMTP | Free tier (checked 2026-11-02) | Sender domain setup | Casdoor type |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Your own mail server | SMTP | You run it | You publish SPF, DKIM and DMARC, and need forward and reverse DNS for the IP | `Default` |\\n| Google Workspace (`smtp.gmail.com`) | SMTP with an app password | Part of the Workspace subscription, 2,000 messages per day | Your Workspace domain | `Default` |\\n| Microsoft 365 (`smtp.office365.com`) | SMTP AUTH | Part of the subscription | Your Microsoft 365 domain | `Default` |\\n| Brevo | SMTP relay (`smtp-relay.brevo.com`) | 300 emails per day | Brevo code (TXT), DKIM and DMARC records | `Default` |\\n| Resend | API | 3,000 emails per month, 100 per day | SPF and DKIM records generated by Resend, then DMARC | `Resend` |\\n| SendGrid | API | 60-day trial, 100 emails per day | Domain authentication: CNAME records for SPF and DKIM, TXT for DMARC | `SendGrid` |\\n| Azure Communication Services Email | API | None listed; $0.00025 per email plus $0.00012 per MB | Azure managed subdomain, or a custom domain with SPF and DKIM | `Azure ACS` |\\n| Amazon SES | SMTP interface | No SES-only quota; new AWS accounts get Free Tier credits that SES can use | Verified identity for every From address | `Default` |\\n| Any other HTTP API | HTTP | Depends on the service | Depends on the service | `Custom HTTP Email` |\\n\\nThe [Email providers overview](/docs/provider/email/overview) in the docs lists the same types and the fields each one uses.\\n\\n## SMTP: your own server, Google Workspace, Microsoft 365\\n\\nThe `Default` type takes a host, port, username and password. **SSL mode** `Auto` uses implicit TLS on port 465 and STARTTLS on other ports, which covers almost every relay below.\\n\\nWith your own server, SPF, DKIM signing, reverse DNS and IP reputation are all yours. Google requires valid forward and reverse DNS (PTR) records for the sending IP.\\n\\nGoogle Workspace documents `smtp.gmail.com` on port 465 or 587, signed in with the full Workspace address and an app password, with a limit of 2,000 messages per day.\\n\\nMicrosoft 365 is the one to avoid for new setups. Casdoor\'s SMTP provider signs in with a username and password, which is Basic authentication for SMTP AUTH. Microsoft has announced plans to retire Basic authentication for SMTP AUTH in Exchange Online, and has already turned SMTP AUTH off in tenants that weren\'t using it. Check the latest timeline in the announcement below before you build on it.\\n\\n## Brevo SMTP\\n\\nBrevo sends transactional mail through a standard SMTP relay, so it uses the `Default` type. Most setup problems come from the Brevo SMTP login:\\n\\n- The server is `smtp-relay.brevo.com`, on port 587, 465 or 2525. Port 465 needs SSL, which `Auto` handles.\\n- The SMTP login is not always your account email. Depending on when the account was created, it is either your login email or a generated address like `[ID]@smtp-brevo.com`, and it is shown on the **SMTP** page in Brevo. Changing your account email doesn\'t change it.\\n- The password is an **SMTP key**, not an API key.\\n\\nIn Casdoor, set **Type** to `Default` and put those values in **Host**, **Port**, **Username** and **Password**. The [Brevo guide](/docs/provider/email/brevo) has screenshots. For the domain, Brevo asks for its own TXT verification record, DKIM and DMARC; SPF is only needed with a dedicated IP.\\n\\n## Resend\\n\\nThe `Resend` type needs only an API key in **Secret key**, plus a **From address** on a verified domain. Leave **Host**, **Port** and **Username** empty. Casdoor formats the sender as `Name <address>` from **From name**, or from the organization\'s display name when **From name** is empty.\\n\\nTwo things to know before you test. First, the shared `onboarding@resend.dev` sender can only send to the email address of your own Resend account; any other recipient gets a 403. Second, verifying your own domain means adding the SPF and DKIM records Resend generates, and Resend recommends sending from a subdomain to keep its reputation separate. The free plan covers 3,000 emails a month with a cap of 100 per day; the Pro plan starts at $20 a month for 50,000. See the [Resend guide](/docs/provider/email/resend).\\n\\n## SendGrid\\n\\nThe `SendGrid` type uses an API key. Leave **Host** and **Endpoint** empty and Casdoor uses SendGrid\'s standard send API; fill both only if you need a different endpoint. Casdoor treats anything but `202 Accepted` as a failure and shows the error messages SendGrid returns, which helps when a sender isn\'t verified yet.\\n\\nSendGrid\'s pricing page lists a 60-day free trial at 100 emails a day, then Essentials from $19.95 a month. Domain authentication with automated security adds a CNAME for SPF and DKIM and a TXT record for DMARC. See the [SendGrid guide](/docs/provider/email/sendgrid).\\n\\n## ACS Email (Azure Communication Services)\\n\\nThe `Azure ACS` type takes the resource endpoint as **Host** and the access key as **Secret key**; Casdoor signs each request with that key. Azure gives you a one-click managed subdomain under `azurecomm.net` with authentication already set up, and Microsoft suggests using it for development and switching to a custom domain, with its SPF and DKIM records, for launch. There is no free tier on the pricing page: $0.00025 per email plus $0.00012 per MB transferred. See the [Azure ACS guide](/docs/provider/email/azureACS).\\n\\n## Amazon SES\\n\\nCasdoor has no SES API type, so use the SES SMTP interface with the `Default` type. The host is the Region\'s SMTP endpoint, such as `email-smtp.us-east-1.amazonaws.com`; SES accepts STARTTLS on 25, 587 or 2587 and implicit TLS on 465 or 2465. The SMTP username and password are SMTP credentials you generate in SES. They are not your AWS access keys, and they are different in each Region.\\n\\nNew accounts start in the SES sandbox: mail goes only to verified addresses, at most 200 messages per 24 hours and one per second. Request production access before real users sign up. SES charges $0.10 per 1,000 outbound emails at its \xe0 la carte rate, with no minimum fee.\\n\\n## Correo HTTP personalizado {#custom-http-email}\\n\\nFor a service without a native type, `Custom HTTP Email` sends `fromAddress`, `fromName`, `toAddress` (or `toAddresses` for several recipients), `subject` and `content` as a form or JSON body, with headers you define. You can rename each field to match the target API. Casdoor counts only HTTP 200 as success, so an API that answers `202 Accepted` shows up as an error; a small adapter in front fixes that. See [Send email through your own HTTP API](/docs/provider/email/customHttp).\\n\\n## Deliverability basics for auth mail\\n\\nVerification codes are useless if they land in spam. The rules that matter:\\n\\n- **SPF and DKIM.** Google requires every sender to Gmail to use SPF or DKIM. Above 5,000 messages a day it requires both, plus DMARC (a `p=none` policy is accepted). Set up all three from the start.\\n- **Alignment.** The domain in the From header must match the SPF domain or the DKIM domain, or DMARC fails. This is why each provider makes you verify the domain you send from.\\n- **A separate subdomain.** Yahoo recommends separating transactional mail from marketing mail by IP or DKIM domain, and Resend recommends subdomains. Something like `auth.example.com` keeps a bad newsletter from hurting your password resets.\\n- **TLS and spam rate.** Google requires TLS and asks you to keep the reported spam rate below 0.3%, ideally below 0.1%.\\n- **No unsubscribe header needed.** Google\'s FAQ says one-click unsubscribe applies to marketing mail, and names password reset messages as an example of transactional mail that is excluded.\\n- **Simple content.** Casdoor sends the template as one HTML body, without a separate plain-text part, for every built-in type. Keep the HTML light, put the code in plain text, and show the full link as text next to the button, as Casdoor\'s built-in magic link email does. Use your Casdoor origin directly rather than a link shortener, so the link domain matches your brand.\\n\\n## Configure and test the provider in Casdoor\\n\\n1. Go to **Identity** > **Providers**, add a provider, set **Category** to `Email` and choose the **Type** from the table above.\\n2. Set **From address** and, optionally, **From name**.\\n3. Write **Email title** and **Email content**. The content can use `%s` for the code, `%{user.friendlyName}` for the user\'s name, and `%link` inside a `<reset-link>` block for the password reset link. Magic link emails use `%link` and `%expireTime`; if your content has no `%link`, Casdoor sends its own magic link email instead. The [templates section](/docs/provider/email/overview#email-content-and-placeholders) has examples. One thing to edit: the default template says the code is valid for 5 minutes, but codes and magic links expire after `verificationCodeTimeout` in `conf/app.conf`, which defaults to 10 minutes.\\n4. Click **Test SMTP Connection** to open a connection without sending (SMTP types only), then enter an address in **Test Email** and click **Send Testing Email**. The test uses the values in the form, saved or not, with `%s` replaced by `123456` and the reset link block removed.\\n5. Add the provider to the application with a rule such as `Signup`, `Forget Password`, `Magic link` or `All`. You can use different providers for different rules. See [Add providers to an application](/docs/application/providers#provider-rules).\\n6. Set `origin` in `conf/app.conf`. Without it, unless the request comes in on a loopback host such as `localhost`, Casdoor leaves the reset link out of password reset emails and refuses to send magic links. See [Sign in with a magic link](/docs/application/signin-methods#magic-link).\\n\\nFor local development, point a `Default` provider at a fake SMTP server instead of a real account; [MailHog vs Mailpit](/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit) covers that setup.\\n\\n## Referencias {#references}\\n\\n- Casdoor source: [`email/provider.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/email/provider.go), [`object/email.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/email.go)\\n- [Resend pricing](https://resend.com/pricing) and [the resend.dev 403 error](https://resend.com/docs/knowledge-base/403-error-resend-dev-domain)\\n- [Twilio SendGrid Email API pricing](https://www.twilio.com/en-us/products/email-api/pricing) and [domain authentication](https://www.twilio.com/docs/sendgrid/ui/account-and-settings/how-to-set-up-domain-authentication)\\n- [Brevo pricing](https://www.brevo.com/pricing/) and [Send transactional emails using Brevo SMTP](https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/7924908994450)\\n- [Azure Communication Services pricing](https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/pricing/details/communication-services/) and [email domains and sender authentication](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/communication-services/concepts/email/email-domain-and-sender-authentication)\\n- [Amazon SES pricing](https://aws.amazon.com/ses/pricing/), [SES SMTP endpoints](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/smtp-connect.html) and [moving out of the sandbox](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ses/latest/dg/request-production-access.html)\\n- [Google email sender guidelines](https://support.google.com/a/answer/81126) and [FAQ](https://support.google.com/a/answer/14229414)\\n- [Yahoo sender best practices](https://senders.yahooinc.com/best-practices/)\\n- [Google Workspace: send email from a printer, scanner, or app](https://support.google.com/a/answer/176600)\\n- [Updated Exchange Online SMTP AUTH Basic authentication deprecation timeline](https://techcommunity.microsoft.com/blog/exchange/updated-exchange-online-smtp-auth-basic-authentication-deprecation-timeline/4489835)"},{"id":"file-storage-and-cdn","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/file-storage-and-cdn","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-file-storage-and-cdn-for-casdoor.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-file-storage-and-cdn-for-casdoor.mdx","title":"How to Host Static Files on a CDN with Casdoor: S3, MinIO, Azure Blob and Other Storage Providers","description":"Where Casdoor keeps avatars and uploaded files (local disk, Amazon S3, MinIO, Azure Blob, GCS, OSS, COS, Synology) and how to serve them through a CDN.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Storage","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/storage"},{"inline":true,"label":"CDN","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/cdn"},{"inline":true,"label":"Amazon S3","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/amazon-s-3"},{"inline":true,"label":"MinIO","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/min-io"},{"inline":true,"label":"Azure Blob","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/azure-blob"},{"inline":true,"label":"Object Storage","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/object-storage"}],"readingTime":9.79,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"file-storage-and-cdn","title":"How to Host Static Files on a CDN with Casdoor: S3, MinIO, Azure Blob and Other Storage Providers","description":"Where Casdoor keeps avatars and uploaded files (local disk, Amazon S3, MinIO, Azure Blob, GCS, OSS, COS, Synology) and how to serve them through a CDN.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Storage","CDN","Amazon S3","MinIO","Azure Blob","Object Storage"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Email Provider for Casdoor Verification Codes: Brevo SMTP, Resend, SendGrid, ACS Email and SES","permalink":"/es/blog/email-providers-for-verification-codes"},"nextItem":{"title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Prueba de correos de verificaci\xf3n y restablecimiento de contrase\xf1a con Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit"}},"content":"To host static files on a CDN, put them in object storage (Amazon S3, MinIO, Azure Blob Storage, Google Cloud Storage, Alibaba Cloud OSS or Tencent Cloud COS), make the CDN pull from that bucket as its origin, and hand out URLs on the CDN\'s domain. In Casdoor this is one setting: create a storage provider for the bucket and put the CDN address in its **Domain** field. Casdoor uploads avatars and other files through the provider\'s API and saves URLs that start with that domain. Casdoor\'s own frontend can come from a CDN as well, through `frontendCdnUrl` and `staticBaseUrl` in `app.conf`.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Storage providers at a glance\\n\\nA storage provider decides where Casdoor writes uploaded files: user avatars, ID card images, terms-of-use pages, and anything uploaded on the **Resources** page. You add the provider to an application, and uploads made in that application go to it. The [storage provider overview](/docs/provider/storage/overview) lists all the types. This table covers the ones people usually ask about:\\n\\n| Storage | Casdoor provider type | Does Casdoor use the S3 API? | Self-hostable | How the public URL is set |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Local file system | `Local File System` | No, plain files on disk | Yes, it is the Casdoor server | Served by Casdoor under `/files/`. **Domain** is read-only |\\n| Amazon S3 | `AWS S3` | Yes | No | **Domain**: the bucket URL or a CloudFront domain. **Endpoint (Intranet)**: a VPC endpoint |\\n| MinIO | `MinIO` | Yes, with path-style requests | Yes | **Domain**: the MinIO URL followed by the bucket name, or a CDN domain |\\n| Alibaba Cloud OSS | `Aliyun OSS` | No, the OSS SDK | No | **Domain**: a CDN domain. Left empty, it becomes `bucket.endpoint`. **Endpoint (Intranet)**: the internal endpoint |\\n| Tencent Cloud COS | `Tencent Cloud COS` | No, the COS API | No | **Domain**: a CDN domain |\\n| Azure Blob Storage | `Azure Blob` | No, the Blob API | No | **Domain**: a custom or Front Door domain. Casdoor adds the container name to the path |\\n| Google Cloud Storage | `Google Cloud Storage` | No, Google\'s Go client | No | **Domain**: left empty, it becomes `https://storage.googleapis.com`. Casdoor adds the bucket name to the path |\\n| Synology NAS | `Synology` | No, the DSM File Station API | Yes | No **Domain** field. The URL is based on **Endpoint** |\\n\\nCasdoor also supports `Qiniu Cloud Kodo`, `CUCloud OSS`, and `Casdoor`, which stores files in another Casdoor instance. The per-provider guides for [Amazon S3](/docs/provider/storage/amazon-s3), [MinIO](/docs/provider/storage/minio), [Azure Blob](/docs/provider/storage/azure), [Google Cloud Storage](/docs/provider/storage/google-cloudstorage), [Alibaba Cloud OSS](/docs/provider/storage/aliyun-oss), [Tencent Cloud COS](/docs/provider/storage/tencentCloudCOS), [Synology NAS](/docs/provider/storage/synology-nas) and the [local file system](/docs/provider/storage/localFileSystem) show which value goes in which field.\\n\\n## Endpoint vs. Domain\\n\\nThese two fields do different jobs, and most CDN problems come from mixing them up.\\n\\n- **Endpoint** is the address Casdoor uses to talk to the storage API: upload, delete, list. The CDN is not involved.\\n- **Endpoint (Intranet)** replaces Endpoint when `isCloudIntranet = true` is set in `app.conf`. That keeps upload traffic on the provider\'s internal network when Casdoor runs in the same cloud. The field appears for AWS S3, Aliyun OSS, Azure Blob and CUCloud OSS.\\n- **Domain** is the base of the URL that Casdoor saves and that browsers request. If you have a CDN, its domain goes here.\\n- **Path prefix** is put in front of every object key. With a prefix of `casdoor`, an avatar ends up under `casdoor/avatar/...`.\\n\\nCasdoor builds the file URL from the scheme and host of Domain, then the path of Domain, then the path prefix, then the file path. A Domain without a scheme gets `https://`. The path part of Domain also becomes part of the object key, so `https://cdn.example.com/casdoor/` stores files under `casdoor/` in the bucket. For Azure Blob and Google Cloud Storage, Casdoor adds the container or bucket name as the first path segment after the host. Your CDN needs to serve that path.\\n\\nIf Domain is empty, Casdoor fills it in from the provider\'s endpoint the first time it uses the provider, and saves it. Sometimes the result works, as with OSS and GCS. Sometimes it doesn\'t: for an S3 endpoint without the bucket name, or for MinIO, where path-style requests put the bucket name in the path, the generated URL misses the bucket. Set Domain yourself, upload one test avatar, and open the URL Casdoor returns before anyone else uses it.\\n\\nThe provider that Casdoor uses for avatars it copies from social logins comes from `defaultStorageProvider` in `app.conf`. It is the name of a provider owned by `admin` (see the [configuration reference](/docs/basic/configuration)):\\n\\n```ini\\ndefaultStorageProvider = provider_storage_s3\\nisCloudIntranet = false\\n```\\n\\n## Public buckets, private buckets and signed URLs\\n\\nCasdoor saves one plain, permanent URL in the user\'s `avatar` field and in the resource record. It doesn\'t create signed or expiring URLs for these files. So whatever is at Domain has to answer an anonymous `GET`. There are two ways to do that.\\n\\n**Make the objects public.** With `AWS S3` and `MinIO`, Casdoor uploads every object with the `public-read` canned ACL. With `Aliyun OSS`, the client library does the same by default. On S3 this conflicts with the defaults for new buckets: according to AWS, new buckets use the **Bucket owner enforced** setting, which disables ACLs, and an upload that sets an ACL such as `public-read` fails with `AccessControlListNotSupported`. That is why the [Amazon S3 guide](/docs/provider/storage/amazon-s3) has you turn on ACLs and turn off **Block all public access**. On OSS, an object ACL takes precedence over the bucket ACL, so a `public-read` object can be read even in a private bucket. MinIO doesn\'t use the ACL for anonymous reads. It uses a bucket policy:\\n\\n```bash\\nmc anonymous set download myminio/casdoor\\n```\\n\\nAzure needs anonymous access allowed on the storage account and the container set to the **Blob** access level. Google Cloud Storage needs the Storage Object Viewer role (`roles/storage.objectViewer`) granted to `allUsers` on the bucket.\\n\\n**Keep the bucket private and let only the CDN read it.** The CDN signs its requests to the origin, and viewers go through the CDN without a signature. Each vendor has its own version:\\n\\n- CloudFront has origin access control (OAC). AWS requires **Bucket owner enforced** for OAC, or **Bucket owner preferred** if you need ACLs.\\n- Alibaba Cloud CDN can fetch from a private OSS bucket after you authorize it.\\n- Tencent Cloud CDN has origin-pull authentication for private COS buckets.\\n- Azure Front Door Premium can reach a storage account over Private Link.\\n- Google\'s Cloud CDN guide covers private bucket access too, where the Storage Object Viewer role goes to a service account instead of `allUsers`.\\n\\nThis hides the bucket\'s own URL, but the files are still public through the CDN. With the `AWS S3` and `Aliyun OSS` types, the objects also stay readable at their bucket URLs, because of the `public-read` ACL that Casdoor sets.\\n\\nIf avatars must not be public at all, a CDN in front of object storage is the wrong tool. Keep those files on a storage type you control, and limit who can reach that host.\\n\\n## Cache headers and the avatar trap\\n\\nOn S3 and MinIO, Casdoor sets `Content-Type` from the file extension when it uploads. It doesn\'t set `Cache-Control` on any provider. That means the CDN\'s default TTL applies. On CloudFront, objects without `Cache-Control` are cached for the default TTL, which is 24 hours when you don\'t use a cache policy. To control this per object, add `Cache-Control` metadata in the bucket. For a whole path, use a cache rule on the CDN.\\n\\nAvatars need a short TTL. When a user changes their picture, Casdoor writes the new file to the same key, `avatar/<organization>/<username>.<extension>`, and saves the same URL. A long TTL means people see the old picture until it expires or until you purge or invalidate that path. Other uploads get a numbered suffix (`-1`, `-2`, ...) when the name is already taken, so a new upload gets a new URL and can be cached for a long time.\\n\\nA layout that works:\\n\\n| Path | Suggested caching |\\n|---|---|\\n| `avatar/*` | Short TTL (minutes), or purge on change |\\n| `resource/*` and other uploads | Long TTL |\\n| Hashed frontend assets (`assets/*`) | Very long TTL. The file name changes on every build |\\n\\n## Casdoor\'s own frontend on a CDN\\n\\nSeparate from user uploads, there are three ways to move Casdoor\'s own static files off the Casdoor server.\\n\\n**`frontendCdnUrl`.** Use this if you install the frontend from the `casdoor-web` npm package. Point it at an npm CDN, for example:\\n\\n```ini\\nfrontendCdnUrl = \\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/{name}@{version}\\"\\n```\\n\\nCasdoor reads the package name and version from the `package.json` in the build folder. It rewrites the script and stylesheet links in `index.html` to that CDN and keeps the local paths as a fallback. If a file fails to load, the browser loads it from Casdoor, and a cookie keeps that browser on local files for the rest of the session. A frontend you build yourself has no such `package.json`, so the setting has no effect on it.\\n\\n**The deployment script.** For a frontend you build from source, [Host the frontend files on a CDN](/docs/deployment/deploy-cdn) explains `deployment/deploy_test.go`. It uploads `web/build/assets/` through one of your storage providers and points `index.html` at the provider\'s Domain, which must end with `/`. Vite hashes these file names, so they suit the long TTL in the table above.\\n\\n**`staticBaseUrl`.** This sets where default images such as logos, provider icons and the default avatar come from. The default is `https://cdn.casbin.org`, which serves the [casbin/static](https://github.com/casbin/static) repository. On a network without internet access, serve a copy of that repository yourself and point `staticBaseUrl` at it, as described in [Host static files on an intranet](/docs/deployment/deploy-intranet).\\n\\n## Picking one\\n\\n- **One server, small user base:** `Local File System`. Mount the `files` directory on a volume if Casdoor runs in a container. Casdoor sends `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` and a sandboxing `Content-Security-Policy` with these files, so an uploaded HTML or SVG file can\'t run script on your Casdoor domain.\\n- **Self-hosted and S3-shaped:** `MinIO`. It is also the type the docs suggest for other S3-compatible stores.\\n- **Already on a cloud:** use that cloud\'s object storage and CDN, set **Endpoint (Intranet)** where Casdoor runs in the same cloud, and set Domain to the CDN.\\n\\n## Referencias {#references}\\n\\n- [Casdoor storage providers](/docs/provider/storage/overview) and the source: [`object/storage.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/storage.go), [`storage/`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/storage), [`routers/frontend_cdn.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/routers/frontend_cdn.go)\\n- AWS: [Controlling ownership of objects and disabling ACLs](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/about-object-ownership.html), [Restrict access to an Amazon S3 origin](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/private-content-restricting-access-to-s3.html), [Manage how long content stays in the cache](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/Expiration.html)\\n- MinIO: [`mc anonymous set`](https://docs.min.io/community/minio-object-store/reference/minio-mc/mc-anonymous-set.html)\\n- Microsoft: [Configure anonymous read access for containers and blobs](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/storage/blobs/anonymous-read-access-configure), [Integrate an Azure Storage account with Azure Front Door](https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/frontdoor/integrate-storage-account)\\n- Google Cloud: [Making data public](https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/access-control/making-data-public), [Set up Cloud CDN with a backend bucket](https://cloud.google.com/cdn/docs/setting-up-cdn-with-bucket)\\n- Alibaba Cloud: [Object ACL](https://www.alibabacloud.com/help/en/oss/user-guide/object-acl), [Origin fetch from private OSS buckets](https://www.alibabacloud.com/help/en/cdn/user-guide/grant-alibaba-cloud-cdn-access-permissions-on-private-oss-buckets)\\n- Tencent Cloud: [Setting CDN Acceleration for COS](https://www.tencentcloud.com/document/product/436/18670)"},{"id":"mailhog-vs-mailpit","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mailpit-vs-mailhog.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mailpit-vs-mailhog.mdx","title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Prueba de correos de verificaci\xf3n y restablecimiento de contrase\xf1a con Casdoor","description":"MailHog vs Mailpit: ambos escuchan en el puerto SMTP 1025 y en el puerto 8025 de la interfaz web. Compare mantenimiento, im\xe1genes Docker y APIs de prueba, luego apunte Casdoor a cualquiera de ellos.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Email","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/email"},{"inline":true,"label":"MailHog","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/mail-hog"},{"inline":true,"label":"Mailpit","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/mailpit"},{"inline":true,"label":"Testing","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/testing"},{"inline":true,"label":"SMTP","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/smtp"},{"inline":true,"label":"CI","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/ci"}],"readingTime":9.26,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mailhog-vs-mailpit","title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Prueba de correos de verificaci\xf3n y restablecimiento de contrase\xf1a con Casdoor","description":"MailHog vs Mailpit: ambos escuchan en el puerto SMTP 1025 y en el puerto 8025 de la interfaz web. Compare mantenimiento, im\xe1genes Docker y APIs de prueba, luego apunte Casdoor a cualquiera de ellos.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Email","MailHog","Mailpit","Testing","SMTP","CI"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"How to Host Static Files on a CDN with Casdoor: S3, MinIO, Azure Blob and Other Storage Providers","permalink":"/es/blog/file-storage-and-cdn"},"nextItem":{"title":"URIs de redirecci\xf3n OAuth MCP para Cursor, Claude y ChatGPT: Registro de clientes MCP en Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/mcp-oauth-redirect-uris"}},"content":"MailHog y Mailpit son ambos servidores SMTP falsos para desarrollo: aceptan cada mensaje, lo almacenan y lo muestran en una interfaz web en lugar de entregarlo. Ambos usan los mismos puertos predeterminados, **SMTP en 1025 y la interfaz web y API en 8025**, por lo que la direcci\xf3n SMTP es `smtp://127.0.0.1:1025` y la bandeja de entrada est\xe1 en `http://127.0.0.1:8025` para cualquiera de ellos. La diferencia es el mantenimiento: el \xfaltimo lanzamiento de MailHog, v1.0.1, sali\xf3 en agosto de 2020, mientras que Mailpit lanz\xf3 v1.33.0 el 31 de octubre de 2026. Para un nuevo proyecto, use Mailpit. Casdoor env\xeda a ambos a trav\xe9s de un proveedor SMTP est\xe1ndar, por lo que puede probar c\xf3digos de registro, correos de restablecimiento de contrase\xf1a y enlaces m\xe1gicos sin una cuenta de correo real.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## MailHog vs Mailpit de un vistazo {#mailhog-vs-mailpit-at-a-glance}\\n\\n| | MailHog | Mailpit |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| \xdaltimo lanzamiento | v1.0.1, 2020-08-11 | v1.33.0, 2026-10-31 |\\n| \xdaltimo commit en la rama predeterminada | 2022-08-02 | 2026-10-31 |\\n| Licencia | MIT | MIT |\\n| Puerto SMTP predeterminado | 1025 | 1025 |\\n| Puerto predeterminado de la interfaz web y API | 8025 | 8025 |\\n| Imagen Docker | `mailhog/mailhog`, solo amd64 | `axllent/mailpit`, amd64, arm64 y 386 |\\n| API HTTP | v1 y v2: listar, obtener, eliminar, buscar por `from`, `to` o `containing` | v1: listar, obtener, eliminar, buscar con filtros como `to:` y `subject:`, atajo para mensaje `latest` |\\n| Almacenamiento predeterminado | En memoria | Base de datos SQLite temporal, eliminada al salir |\\n| Almacenamiento persistente | MongoDB o Maildir | Archivo SQLite (`MP_DATABASE`) o rqlite |\\n| L\xedmite de mensajes | No documentado | Mantiene los 500 m\xe1s recientes por defecto (`MP_MAX_MESSAGES`) |\\n| Comprobaci\xf3n HTML, comprobaci\xf3n de enlaces, comprobaci\xf3n de spam | No | S\xed |\\n| Webhook en nuevo correo | No | S\xed (`MP_WEBHOOK_URL`) |\\n| SMTP TLS | No ofrecido | STARTTLS u SSL/TLS opcionales |\\n| Inyecci\xf3n de fallos | Jim | Chaos |\\n| Liberar un mensaje a un servidor SMTP real | S\xed | S\xed |\\n| Servidor POP3 | No | S\xed, opcional |\\n| Autenticaci\xf3n b\xe1sica para la interfaz web | S\xed | S\xed |\\n\\nLas estad\xedsticas de Docker Hub muestran que muchas personas siguen descargando MailHog. Esto refleja tutoriales antiguos y archivos de CI, no actividad actual: una issue abierta en el repositorio de MailHog titulada \\"THIS PROJECT IS DEAD!\\" tiene una respuesta de 2023 del autor diciendo que ya no tiene tiempo para mantenerlo. El README de Mailpit se describe a s\xed mismo como inspirado en MailHog y apunta a ese mismo comentario.\\n\\nLa arquitectura de la imagen importa m\xe1s de lo que parece. En un port\xe1til Apple Silicon o un ejecutor de CI ARM, `mailhog/mailhog` se ejecuta solo bajo emulaci\xf3n, mientras que `axllent/mailpit` tiene una imagen nativa arm64.\\n\\n## Ejecutar cualquiera de ellos con Docker {#run-either-one-with-docker}\\n\\nMailHog:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -d --name mailhog -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 mailhog/mailhog\\n```\\n\\nMailpit:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -d --name mailpit -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 axllent/mailpit\\n```\\n\\nMailpit mantiene los correos en una base de datos temporal que desaparece cuando se detiene el contenedor. Para conservarla entre reinicios, monte un volumen y configure `MP_DATABASE`:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -d --name mailpit \\\\\\n  -v \\"$PWD/mailpit-data:/data\\" \\\\\\n  -e MP_DATABASE=/data/mailpit.db \\\\\\n  -p 1025:1025 -p 8025:8025 \\\\\\n  axllent/mailpit\\n```\\n\\nSin Docker, tanto `brew install mailhog` como `brew install mailpit` funcionan en macOS, y Mailpit ofrece binarios est\xe1ticos para Windows, Linux y macOS en su p\xe1gina de lanzamientos.\\n\\nEn GitHub Actions, ejec\xfatelo como un contenedor de servicio junto a su trabajo de prueba:\\n\\n```yaml\\nservices:\\n  mailpit:\\n    image: axllent/mailpit\\n    ports:\\n      - 1025:1025\\n      - 8025:8025\\n```\\n\\n## Apuntar Casdoor a MailHog o Mailpit {#point-casdoor-at-mailhog-or-mailpit}\\n\\nCasdoor no tiene un tipo de proveedor separado para MailHog o Mailpit, y no lo necesita. El tipo de correo electr\xf3nico `Default` env\xeda a trav\xe9s de SMTP, y ambas herramientas son servidores SMTP.\\n\\n1. Como administrador global, agregue un proveedor en la organizaci\xf3n `admin` o `built-in`.\\n1. Establece **Category** en `Email` y **Type** en `Default`.\\n1. Establezca **Host** en la ubicaci\xf3n donde se ejecuta la herramienta y **Port** en `1025`.\\n1. Deje **Username** y **Password** vac\xedos.\\n1. Haga clic en **Test SMTP Connection**, luego en **Send Testing Email**, y revise la interfaz web en el puerto 8025.\\n\\nLas p\xe1ginas paso a paso con capturas de pantalla son [Test email with MailHog](/docs/provider/email/mailhog) y [Test email with Mailpit](/docs/provider/email/mailpit).\\n\\nAlgunos detalles deciden si esto funciona a la primera:\\n\\n**Deje el modo SSL en Auto o config\xfarelo en Disable.** En `Auto`, Casdoor usa TLS impl\xedcito solo en el puerto 465 y actualiza con STARTTLS solo si el servidor lo ofrece. Ni MailHog ni un Mailpit predeterminado ofrecen STARTTLS en el puerto 1025, por lo que la conexi\xf3n permanece sin cifrar. No elija `Enable`: eso fuerza TLS impl\xedcito, y el handshake falla contra un servidor de texto plano. La [tabla de modos SSL](/docs/provider/email/overview#ssl-mode) tiene el comportamiento completo.\\n\\n**Deje las credenciales vac\xedas.** Casdoor inicia sesi\xf3n en el servidor SMTP solo cuando se establece **Username**. Si lo rellena, la autenticaci\xf3n `PLAIN` de Go se niega a enviar la contrase\xf1a sobre una conexi\xf3n no cifrada a menos que el host sea localhost, por lo que un MailHog en `192.168.x.x` fallar\xe1 con un error de autenticaci\xf3n aunque MailHog aceptar\xeda cualquier inicio de sesi\xf3n. Mailpit, por defecto, no solicita autenticaci\xf3n en absoluto.\\n\\n**Use el host correcto.** `127.0.0.1` funciona solo cuando Casdoor y el receptor de correo se ejecutan en la misma m\xe1quina. Si ambos se ejecutan en Docker Compose, use el nombre del servicio, como `mailpit`, como host.\\n\\n**Use un proveedor global.** Un proveedor propiedad de una organizaci\xf3n ordinaria solo puede acceder a direcciones de Internet, por lo que no puede conectarse a `127.0.0.1` o una IP privada. Los proveedores en `admin` o `built-in` no est\xe1n restringidos. Consulte [Qu\xe9 direcciones puede alcanzar un proveedor](/docs/provider/overview#which-addresses-a-provider-can-reach).\\n\\n**Configure `origin` para los enlaces.** Los c\xf3digos de verificaci\xf3n funcionan inmediatamente. Los enlaces de restablecimiento de contrase\xf1a y los enlaces m\xe1gicos necesitan que Casdoor conozca su URL p\xfablica, mediante `origin` en `conf/app.conf` o una solicitud en un host de bucle invertido (loopback). De lo contrario, el enlace de restablecimiento se elimina del correo y los enlaces m\xe1gicos fallan. Consulte [Plantillas](/docs/provider/email/overview#email-content-and-placeholders).\\n\\nFinalmente, agregue el proveedor a su aplicaci\xf3n en [Add providers to an application](/docs/application/providers) para que el registro y el restablecimiento de contrase\xf1a lo usen realmente.\\n\\n## Leer el c\xf3digo de verificaci\xf3n en una prueba automatizada {#read-the-verification-code-in-an-automated-test}\\n\\nLos c\xf3digos de verificaci\xf3n de Casdoor tienen seis d\xedgitos, y la plantilla predeterminada dice \\"Here is your code: \\" seguido del c\xf3digo. Una prueba de extremo a extremo registra un usuario, pide a Casdoor que env\xede el c\xf3digo y luego lo lee desde la API del receptor de correo.\\n\\nCon Mailpit, busque por destinatario y abra la coincidencia m\xe1s reciente. `GET /api/v1/search` devuelve las coincidencias ordenadas de la m\xe1s nueva a la m\xe1s antigua, y `GET /api/v1/message/{ID}` devuelve los cuerpos `Text` y `HTML` analizados:\\n\\n```ts\\nimport { request } from \\"@playwright/test\\";\\n\\nconst MAILPIT = process.env.MAILPIT_URL ?? \\"http://127.0.0.1:8025\\";\\n\\nexport async function latestCode(to: string): Promise<string> {\\n  const api = await request.newContext({ baseURL: MAILPIT });\\n  for (let i = 0; i < 20; i++) {\\n    const res = await api.get(\\"/api/v1/search\\", {\\n      params: { query: `to:\\"${to}\\"`, limit: \\"1\\" },\\n    });\\n    const { messages } = await res.json();\\n    if (messages.length > 0) {\\n      const msg = await (await api.get(`/api/v1/message/${messages[0].ID}`)).json();\\n      const match = (msg.Text || msg.HTML).match(/code: (\\\\d{6})/);\\n      if (match) return match[1];\\n    }\\n    await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 500));\\n  }\\n  throw new Error(`no verification email for ${to}`);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nSi solo una prueba env\xeda un correo a la vez, `GET /api/v1/message/latest` omite el paso de b\xfasqueda. Antes de cada prueba, `DELETE /api/v1/messages` sin cuerpo limpia la bandeja de entrada.\\n\\nCon MailHog, la llamada m\xe1s cercana es `GET /api/v2/search?kind=to&query=<address>`. Los mensajes se devuelven en orden cronol\xf3gico inverso (del m\xe1s reciente al m\xe1s antiguo) en `items`, y el cuerpo est\xe1 en `items[0].Content.Body`:\\n\\n```ts\\nconst res = await api.get(\\"/api/v2/search\\", {\\n  params: { kind: \\"to\\", query: to, limit: \\"1\\" },\\n});\\nconst { items } = await res.json();\\nconst body: string = items[0]?.Content.Body ?? \\"\\";\\n```\\n\\n`Content.Body` es el cuerpo MIME sin procesar. Casdoor env\xeda en formato quoted-printable, por lo que `=` se convierte en `=3D` y las l\xedneas largas tienen saltos suaves que terminan en `=`. Descodif\xedquelo primero o mantenga el c\xf3digo cerca del inicio de una plantilla corta. Limpie MailHog con `DELETE /api/v1/messages`.\\n\\n## Migraci\xf3n de MailHog a Mailpit {#migrating-from-mailhog-to-mailpit}\\n\\nDebido a que los puertos coinciden, la mayor\xeda de las migraciones requieren un cambio de una sola l\xednea en `docker-compose.yml`: reemplace `mailhog/mailhog` por `axllent/mailpit`. El proveedor de Casdoor mantiene el mismo host y puerto. Lo que cambia es:\\n\\n- **Configuraci\xf3n.** MailHog lee variables `MH_*`; Mailpit lee `MP_*`. Si usaba `MH_STORAGE=maildir` o MongoDB, pase a `MP_DATABASE`.\\n- **Helpers de prueba.** Mailpit no tiene endpoints `/api/v2`. Reescriba las llamadas a `/api/v2/messages` y `/api/v2/search` para usar `/api/v1/messages` y `/api/v1/search`, y lea `messages[].ID` junto con `Text` o `HTML` en lugar de `items[].Content.Body`. La decodificaci\xf3n quoted-printable desaparece.\\n- **Sintaxis de b\xfasqueda.** `kind=to&query=a@example.com` se convierte en `query=to:\\"a@example.com\\"`.\\n- **Inyecci\xf3n de fallos.** Jim no tiene banderas en Mailpit; use Chaos.\\n- **Autenticaci\xf3n.** Si una aplicaci\xf3n insiste en iniciar sesi\xf3n sobre SMTP sin cifrar, inicie Mailpit con `MP_SMTP_AUTH_ACCEPT_ANY=1` y `MP_SMTP_AUTH_ALLOW_INSECURE=1`. Para Casdoor, es m\xe1s simple dejar las credenciales vac\xedas.\\n- **Retenci\xf3n.** Mailpit elimina por defecto los mensajes m\xe1s antiguos hasta conservar los 500 m\xe1s recientes. Aumente `MP_MAX_MESSAGES` si una ejecuci\xf3n de pruebas larga necesita m\xe1s.\\n\\n## Pasando a producci\xf3n {#going-to-production}\\n\\nMailHog y Mailpit nunca entregan correos a bandejas de entrada reales a menos que libere los mensajes manualmente, por lo que la producci\xf3n necesita un proveedor real. En Casdoor cambia el proveedor, no las plantillas. Las plantillas de correo electr\xf3nico, el marcador de posici\xf3n de c\xf3digo `%s` y el bloque `<reset-link>` permanecen iguales:\\n\\n- Cualquier servidor SMTP con el tipo `Default`, descrito en [Proveedores de email](/docs/provider/email/overview)\\n- [SendGrid](/docs/provider/email/sendgrid) y [Resend](/docs/provider/email/resend) a trav\xe9s de sus APIs\\n- [Brevo](/docs/provider/email/brevo) a trav\xe9s de su relay SMTP\\n- [Azure Communication Services](/docs/provider/email/azureACS)\\n- Su propio endpoint HTTP con [Custom HTTP Email](/docs/provider/email/customHttp)\\n\\nUna configuraci\xf3n com\xfan mantiene Mailpit en desarrollo y CI, y el proveedor real en producci\xf3n. Cada entorno apunta su aplicaci\xf3n a un proveedor diferente, por lo que el flujo de registro que prueba es el que se publica.\\n\\n## Referencias {#references}\\n\\n- Repositorio y README de MailHog: https://github.com/mailhog/MailHog\\n- Documentaci\xf3n de configuraci\xf3n y API v1/v2 de MailHog: https://github.com/mailhog/MailHog/tree/master/docs\\n- Comentario de mantenimiento de MailHog: https://github.com/mailhog/MailHog/issues/442#issuecomment-1493415258\\n- Repositorio y README de Mailpit: https://github.com/axllent/mailpit\\n- Documentaci\xf3n de Mailpit (Docker, almacenamiento, SMTP, webhook, filtros de b\xfasqueda): https://mailpit.axllent.org/docs/\\n- Referencia de la API v1 de Mailpit: https://mailpit.axllent.org/docs/api-v1/\\n- C\xf3digo del proveedor de email de Casdoor: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/email"},{"id":"mcp-oauth-redirect-uris","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/mcp-oauth-redirect-uris","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mcp-oauth-redirect-uris.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-mcp-oauth-redirect-uris.mdx","title":"URIs de redirecci\xf3n OAuth MCP para Cursor, Claude y ChatGPT: Registro de clientes MCP en Casdoor","description":"Las URLs de callback de OAuth que Cursor, Claude y ChatGPT utilizan para servidores MCP, d\xf3nde se encuentra claude_desktop_config.json y c\xf3mo registrar cada cliente en Casdoor.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"MCP","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/mcp"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"Cursor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/cursor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Claude","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/claude"},{"inline":true,"label":"ChatGPT","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/chat-gpt"},{"inline":true,"label":"Dynamic Client Registration","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/dynamic-client-registration"}],"readingTime":8.6,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"mcp-oauth-redirect-uris","title":"URIs de redirecci\xf3n OAuth MCP para Cursor, Claude y ChatGPT: Registro de clientes MCP en Casdoor","description":"Las URLs de callback de OAuth que Cursor, Claude y ChatGPT utilizan para servidores MCP, d\xf3nde se encuentra claude_desktop_config.json y c\xf3mo registrar cada cliente en Casdoor.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","MCP","OAuth","Cursor","Claude","ChatGPT","Dynamic Client Registration"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"MailHog vs Mailpit: Prueba de correos de verificaci\xf3n y restablecimiento de contrase\xf1a con Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit"},"nextItem":{"title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Protecting Any App Behind Nginx with Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/nginx-sso-authentication"}},"content":"Cada cliente MCP env\xeda su propia URI de redirecci\xf3n cuando inicia sesi\xf3n en un servidor MCP protegido por OAuth. Cursor desktop usa `http://localhost:8787/callback` (las versiones anteriores a mediados de 2026 usaban `cursor://anysphere.cursor-mcp/oauth/callback`), Cursor web y Cursor Cloud Agents usan `https://www.cursor.com/agents/mcp/oauth/callback`, claude.ai, conectores de Claude Desktop, Claude mobile y Cowork usan `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`, y ChatGPT usa `https://chatgpt.com/connector_platform_oauth_redirect` o un valor espec\xedfico por conexi\xf3n `https://chatgpt.com/connector/oauth/<callback-id>`. Si activa el registro din\xe1mico de clientes en Casdoor, no introduce ninguno de estos: los clientes se registran solos o proporcionan a Casdoor un documento de metadatos de ID de cliente. Los agrega a **Redirect URLs** solo cuando crea manualmente una aplicaci\xf3n de Casdoor para un cliente.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## URIs de redirecci\xf3n por cliente {#redirect-uris-by-client}\\n\\n| Cliente | URI de redirecci\xf3n | C\xf3mo obtiene un ID de cliente de Casdoor | Notas |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Cursor desktop (actual) | `http://localhost:8787/callback` | DCR, o `auth.CLIENT_ID` en `mcp.json` | Puerto fijo 8787 |\\n| Cursor web y Cloud Agents | `https://www.cursor.com/agents/mcp/oauth/callback` | DCR, o `auth.CLIENT_ID` | Con DCR, la aplicaci\xf3n de escritorio registra esta URI y la de localhost |\\n| Cursor desktop (versiones antiguas) | `cursor://anysphere.cursor-mcp/oauth/callback` | DCR, o `auth.CLIENT_ID` | Cursor hizo que la URI de loopback fuera la predeterminada en julio de 2026 |\\n| claude.ai, conectores de Claude Desktop, Claude mobile, Cowork | `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback` | CIMD, luego DCR, o un ID de cliente introducido en el di\xe1logo de conector personalizado | Los servidores de Anthropic realizan las solicitudes |\\n| Claude Code | `http://localhost:<port>/callback` y `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/callback` | CIMD | El puerto cambia por sesi\xf3n |\\n| Claude Desktop mediante `mcp-remote` | `http://localhost:<port>/oauth/callback` | DCR | El puerto se deriva de la URL del servidor |\\n| ChatGPT | `https://chatgpt.com/connector_platform_oauth_redirect`, o `https://chatgpt.com/connector/oauth/<callback-id>` | CIMD, DCR, o un cliente predefinido | Copie el valor exacto desde ChatGPT |\\n\\nEstos valores provienen de la documentaci\xf3n de los proveedores a fecha del 2 de noviembre de 2026 (consulte [Referencias](#references)). Los clientes cambian estos valores. Cuando falla un inicio de sesi\xf3n, lea el par\xe1metro `redirect_uri` de la solicitud de autorizaci\xf3n en la barra de direcciones del navegador y comp\xe1relo con lo que tiene Casdoor.\\n\\nLa tabla se aplica a dos configuraciones: el [servidor MCP integrado en Casdoor](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/overview) en `/api/mcp`, y su propio servidor MCP que utiliza [Casdoor como su servidor de autorizaci\xf3n](/docs/mcp-auth/overview). En ambos casos, el cliente encuentra Casdoor a trav\xe9s de los metadatos del recurso protegido ([RFC 9728](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9728)) y obtiene su ID de cliente de Casdoor.\\n\\n## Opci\xf3n 1: permitir que los clientes se registren por s\xed mismos {#option-1-let-clients-register-themselves}\\n\\nCasdoor admite el registro din\xe1mico de clientes ([RFC 7591](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7591)) en `/api/oauth/register`, y los documentos de metadatos de ID de cliente (CIMD), donde el `client_id` es una URL HTTPS de un documento JSON que enumera las URI de redireccionamiento del cliente. Un interruptor controla ambos: **Enable dynamic client registration** en la organizaci\xf3n. Est\xe1 desactivado por defecto, para todas las organizaciones incluyendo `built-in`.\\n\\nCon el interruptor activado para `built-in`, el documento de descubrimiento muestra lo que buscan los clientes:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -s https://your-casdoor.com/.well-known/openid-configuration | jq \'{\\n  registration_endpoint,\\n  client_id_metadata_document_supported,\\n  code_challenge_methods_supported,\\n  token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported,\\n  authorization_response_iss_parameter_supported\\n}\'\\n```\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \\"registration_endpoint\\": \\"https://your-casdoor.com/api/oauth/register\\",\\n  \\"client_id_metadata_document_supported\\": true,\\n  \\"code_challenge_methods_supported\\": [\\"S256\\"],\\n  \\"token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported\\": [\\"client_secret_basic\\", \\"client_secret_post\\", \\"private_key_jwt\\", \\"none\\"],\\n  \\"authorization_response_iss_parameter_supported\\": true\\n}\\n```\\n\\nCada cliente luego elige su m\xe9todo:\\n\\n- **Claude** usa CIMD solo cuando los metadatos tienen tanto `client_id_metadata_document_supported: true` como `none` en `token_endpoint_auth_methods_supported`. Casdoor tiene ambos una vez que el interruptor est\xe1 activado, por lo que Claude se identifica con una URL de documento y Casdoor crea una aplicaci\xf3n llamada `cimd_...`. De lo contrario, Claude recurre a DCR.\\n- **ChatGPT** prefiere CIMD, con el `client_id` `https://chatgpt.com/oauth/client.json` (o una variante por callback), y usa DCR cuando CIMD no est\xe1 disponible.\\n- **Cursor** se registra a trav\xe9s de DCR como un cliente p\xfablico sin secreto. La documentaci\xf3n de Cursor no menciona CIMD.\\n- **`mcp-remote`** se registra a trav\xe9s de DCR.\\n\\nLas URI de redireccionamiento provienen de la solicitud de registro o del documento de metadatos, por lo que no hay nada que escribir. Los clientes registrados se ejecutan bajo un rol restringido y sus tokens no pueden llamar a la API de gesti\xf3n. Consulte [Dynamic client registration](/docs/application/dynamic-client-registration) para conocer las reglas completas y la organizaci\xf3n en la que aterriza un cliente.\\n\\n## Opci\xf3n 2: crear una aplicaci\xf3n para el cliente {#option-2-create-an-application-for-the-client}\\n\\nSi mantiene el registro desactivado, cree una aplicaci\xf3n por cliente, para poder revocarlos por separado:\\n\\n1. En la consola de administraci\xf3n de Casdoor, ve a **Identity** > **Applications** y a\xf1ade una aplicaci\xf3n, por ejemplo `cursor-mcp`.\\n1. Agregue las URI de redireccionamiento del cliente de la tabla a **Redirect URLs**. Para Cursor, agregue las tres, porque diferentes instalaciones de Cursor env\xedan distintas.\\n1. Establezca **Category** a `Agent`. Consulte [Set up Casdoor for your MCP server](/docs/mcp-auth/setup) para \xe1mbitos y tipos de concesi\xf3n.\\n1. Proporcione el **Client ID** al cliente.\\n\\nUn secreto de cliente es opcional para estos clientes. Casdoor acepta un intercambio de c\xf3digo de autorizaci\xf3n sin secreto cuando la solicitud us\xf3 PKCE, y Cursor, Claude y ChatGPT usan todos PKCE con `S256`. Si un cliente env\xeda un secreto, debe ser el correcto.\\n\\nD\xf3nde va el ID de cliente:\\n\\n- **Cursor**: un objeto `auth` en `~/.cursor/mcp.json` o `.cursor/mcp.json`. Con un `CLIENT_ID` est\xe1tico, la aplicaci\xf3n de escritorio de Cursor env\xeda solo su URI de redireccionamiento localhost.\\n\\n  ```json\\n  {\\n    \\"mcpServers\\": {\\n      \\"casdoor\\": {\\n        \\"url\\": \\"https://your-casdoor.com/api/mcp\\",\\n        \\"auth\\": {\\n          \\"CLIENT_ID\\": \\"${env:CASDOOR_MCP_CLIENT_ID}\\",\\n          \\"scopes\\": [\\"application:read\\", \\"user:read\\"]\\n        }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  ```\\n\\n- **Claude**: el campo de ID de cliente OAuth en el di\xe1logo de conector personalizado. Registre `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`.\\n- **ChatGPT**: ChatGPT tambi\xe9n funciona con clientes OAuth predefinidos. Copie la URI de redireccionamiento desde la p\xe1gina de gesti\xf3n del servidor MCP en ChatGPT, ya que cu\xe1l de las dos formas usa depende del servidor de autorizaci\xf3n.\\n\\nGu\xedas paso a paso: [Cursor](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/connect-cursor), [Claude Desktop](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/connect-claude-desktop), [ChatGPT](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/connect-chatgpt).\\n\\n## C\xf3mo Casdoor hace coincidir las URI de redireccionamiento {#how-casdoor-matches-redirect-uris}\\n\\nCasdoor compara el `redirect_uri` de una solicitud con cada entrada en **Redirect URLs**:\\n\\n- El esquema, el host y la ruta deben coincidir. La cadena de consulta no se compara.\\n- El puerto debe coincidir, excepto en hosts de bucle invertido (loopback). Cuando ambas URI usan el mismo host de bucle invertido (`localhost`, `127.0.0.1` o `::1`), Casdoor ignora el puerto, como solicita [RFC 8252](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8252#section-7.3) para aplicaciones nativas. `localhost` y `127.0.0.1` son hosts diferentes, por lo que Claude Code necesita tanto `http://localhost/callback` como `http://127.0.0.1/callback`.\\n- Una URI de redireccionamiento en un subdominio de un host registrado tambi\xe9n coincide, por lo que `https://example.com/cb` acepta `https://app.example.com/cb`. Registre el host m\xe1s espec\xedfico posible.\\n- Los esquemas personalizados como `cursor://` coinciden con las mismas reglas. Las URI `javascript:`, `data:` y `vbscript:` siempre se rechazan.\\n\\nDebido a que el puerto de bucle invertido se ignora, un registro de `mcp-remote` o Claude Code sigue funcionando cuando el puerto local cambia entre sesiones.\\n\\n## D\xf3nde vive claude_desktop_config.json {#where-claude_desktop_configjson-lives}\\n\\nLa [documentaci\xf3n de MCP](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/connect-local-servers) proporciona estas rutas. En Claude Desktop, **Settings** > **Developer** > **Edit Config** abre el archivo y lo crea si falta.\\n\\n| Operating system | Path                                                              |\\n|---|---|\\n| macOS            | `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` |\\n| Windows          | `%APPDATA%\\\\Claude\\\\claude_desktop_config.json`                   |\\n| Linux            | `~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json`                     |\\n\\nClaude Desktop llega a un servidor MCP remoto de una de dos maneras, y usan diferentes URI de redireccionamiento:\\n\\n- **Como un conector**, agregado en la configuraci\xf3n de conectores de Claude. Los servidores de Anthropic ejecutan el flujo OAuth, la URI de redireccionamiento es `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`, y Casdoor debe ser accesible desde Internet.\\n- **A trav\xe9s de `claude_desktop_config.json`**, con `mcp-remote` como puente local. El flujo OAuth se ejecuta en su computadora, y la URI de redireccionamiento es `http://localhost:<port>/oauth/callback`:\\n\\n  ```json\\n  {\\n    \\"mcpServers\\": {\\n      \\"casdoor\\": {\\n        \\"command\\": \\"npx\\",\\n        \\"args\\": [\\"mcp-remote\\", \\"https://your-casdoor.com/api/mcp\\"]\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  ```\\n\\nSalga completamente de Claude Desktop e in\xedcielo de nuevo despu\xe9s de editar el archivo.\\n\\n## Los clientes alojados necesitan un Casdoor p\xfablico {#hosted-clients-need-a-public-casdoor}\\n\\nLos conectores de ChatGPT y Claude llaman a su servidor MCP y a Casdoor desde los servidores de sus proveedores, no desde su computadora. Casdoor debe ser accesible mediante HTTPS p\xfablico con un certificado v\xe1lido. Las solicitudes de Anthropic provienen de `160.79.104.0/21`, si filtra por IP. Cursor desktop, Claude Code y `mcp-remote` ejecutan el flujo localmente, por lo que un Casdoor en su red funciona para ellos.\\n\\nDos cosas m\xe1s que comprueban los clientes alojados, que Casdoor ya hace:\\n\\n- **Issuer in the response** ([RFC 9207](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9207)): ChatGPT rechaza una respuesta de autorizaci\xf3n sin el `iss` correcto, y usa su URI de redirecci\xf3n estable solo con servidores de autorizaci\xf3n que cumplen sus requisitos de RFC 9207. Casdoor anuncia esta compatibilidad en sus metadatos y a\xf1ade `iss` a la redirecci\xf3n.\\n- **Resource indicators** ([RFC 8707](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8707)): ChatGPT env\xeda `resource` con la URL del servidor MCP en las solicitudes de autorizaci\xf3n y token. Casdoor coloca ese valor en la reclamaci\xf3n `aud` del token de acceso, y tu servidor MCP debe verificarlo. Consulta [Autenticaci\xf3n MCP](/docs/how-to-connect/mcp/authentication) para el flujo de descubrimiento.\\n\\nLecturas relacionadas en www.casdoor.com: [OAuth para agentes de IA](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/oauth-for-ai-agents) y [servidores de autorizaci\xf3n para MCP](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/mcp-authorization-servers).\\n\\n## Referencias {#references}\\n\\n- Cursor: [Model Context Protocol (MCP)](https://cursor.com/docs/mcp), secci\xf3n \\"Static OAuth for remote servers\\"\\n- Foro de Cursor: [OAuth redirect URI changed from cursor:// to http://localhost](https://forum.cursor.com/t/oauth-redirect-uri-changed-from-cursor-to-http-localhost-for-streamable-http-mcp/165019)\\n- Anthropic: [Authentication for connectors](https://claude.com/docs/connectors/building/authentication)\\n- Model Context Protocol: [Connect to local MCP servers](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/connect-local-servers)\\n- OpenAI: [Authenticate users](https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/auth)\\n- `mcp-remote`: [README](https://github.com/geelen/mcp-remote)\\n- Casdoor: [Dynamic client registration](/docs/application/dynamic-client-registration), [Set up Casdoor for your MCP server](/docs/mcp-auth/setup)"},{"id":"nginx-sso-authentication","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/nginx-sso-authentication","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-nginx-sso-authentication.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-nginx-sso-authentication.mdx","title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Protecting Any App Behind Nginx with Casdoor","description":"A\xf1ade inicio de sesi\xf3n CAS, OIDC o auth_request delante de cualquier aplicaci\xf3n detr\xe1s de nginx con Casdoor: se comparan forward auth, OAuth2 Proxy, un m\xf3dulo de nginx, clientes CAS y MFA.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"nginx","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/nginx"},{"inline":true,"label":"CAS","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/cas"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"MFA","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/mfa"},{"inline":true,"label":"forward auth","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/forward-auth"}],"readingTime":11.57,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"nginx-sso-authentication","title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Protecting Any App Behind Nginx with Casdoor","description":"A\xf1ade inicio de sesi\xf3n CAS, OIDC o auth_request delante de cualquier aplicaci\xf3n detr\xe1s de nginx con Casdoor: se comparan forward auth, OAuth2 Proxy, un m\xf3dulo de nginx, clientes CAS y MFA.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","nginx","CAS","SSO","OIDC","MFA","forward auth"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"URIs de redirecci\xf3n OAuth MCP para Cursor, Claude y ChatGPT: Registro de clientes MCP en Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/mcp-oauth-redirect-uris"},"nextItem":{"title":"RP-Initiated Logout and SSO Logout in OpenID Connect: How Casdoor Signs Users Out","permalink":"/es/blog/oidc-rp-initiated-logout"}},"content":"nginx de c\xf3digo abierto no tiene un m\xf3dulo CAS, por lo que \\"nginx CAS authentication\\" en la pr\xe1ctica significa uno de dos tipos de configuraci\xf3n. O bien nginx pregunta a un servicio de autenticaci\xf3n sobre cada solicitud a trav\xe9s de su m\xf3dulo `auth_request`, y Casdoor responde (directamente en `/api/forward-auth`, o mediante casdoor-forward-auth u OAuth2 Proxy), o la aplicaci\xf3n detr\xe1s de nginx es en s\xed misma un cliente CAS y se comunica con los endpoints del servidor CAS de Casdoor. Si puedes recompilar nginx, el m\xf3dulo Casdoor nginx ejecuta el inicio de sesi\xf3n dentro de nginx, y si prefieres no ejecutar nginx en absoluto, el proxy de sitio de Casdoor puede situarse delante de la aplicaci\xf3n. En todos los casos, la p\xe1gina de inicio de sesi\xf3n es la de Casdoor, por lo que una sola sesi\xf3n SSO, un conjunto de permisos y una pol\xedtica de MFA cubren todas las aplicaciones.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Las opciones de un vistazo {#the-options-at-a-glance}\\n\\n| Option | Login logic runs in | What nginx needs | Protocol to Casdoor | Who gets in | Pick it when |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Casdoor forward auth | Casdoor, `/api/forward-auth` | `auth_request` | Cookie de sesi\xf3n de Casdoor en el dominio de la app | Permisos de aplicaci\xf3n, comprobados al menos una vez por minuto | nginx ya est\xe1 delante y no quieres servicios adicionales |\\n| casdoor-forward-auth | Un peque\xf1o servicio Go | `auth_request` | C\xf3digo de autorizaci\xf3n OIDC | Roles o grupos de Casdoor, por ubicaci\xf3n | Una cookie de sesi\xf3n compartida por muchos subdominios |\\n| OAuth2 Proxy | OAuth2 Proxy | `auth_request` | OIDC | `allowed_groups`, dominios de correo | Ya ejecutas OAuth2 Proxy |\\n| casdoor-nginx-module | nginx itself | A compiled module | OIDC with PKCE, tokens verified locally | Any signed-in user of the app | No extra process, plus bearer tokens for APIs |\\n| CAS client in the app | The app | Nothing, it only proxies | CAS 1.0, 2.0, 3.0 | Whatever the app does with the attributes | La aplicaci\xf3n ya soporta CAS |\\n| Casdoor site proxy | Casdoor on ports 80 and 443 | No nginx | Casdoor session | Application permissions | Nada m\xe1s est\xe1 delante de la aplicaci\xf3n |\\n\\nLas tres primeras opciones difieren solo en qui\xe9n responde a la subpetici\xf3n de nginx.\\n\\n## Por qu\xe9 no existe un \\"m\xf3dulo CAS para nginx\\" {#why-there-is-no-nginx-cas-module}\\n\\nCAS es una conversaci\xf3n entre una aplicaci\xf3n y el servidor CAS: la aplicaci\xf3n env\xeda el navegador al `/login` del servidor con una URL `service`, recibe un ticket de servicio en esa URL y valida el ticket con una llamada de servidor a servidor. Apache httpd tiene [mod_auth_cas](https://github.com/apereo/mod_auth_cas) de Apereo para hacer esto en el servidor web, pero nginx no tiene ning\xfan m\xf3dulo CAS entre sus m\xf3dulos oficiales. Para nginx, el bloque de construcci\xf3n est\xe1ndar es [`ngx_http_auth_request_module`](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_auth_request_module.html): antes de servir una solicitud, nginx env\xeda una subpetici\xf3n a una URL que t\xfa eliges. Una respuesta 2xx permite pasar la solicitud, 401 o 403 la deniega con ese c\xf3digo, y cualquier otro c\xf3digo es un error.\\n\\nPara el usuario, un inicio de sesi\xf3n CAS y uno OIDC parecen iguales: una redirecci\xf3n a la p\xe1gina de inicio de sesi\xf3n de Casdoor y vuelta. As\xed que para una aplicaci\xf3n sin soporte CAS, la respuesta pr\xe1ctica es OIDC en la capa de nginx. Informaci\xf3n general sobre el protocolo: [What is CAS](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/what-is-cas).\\n\\nEl m\xf3dulo auth_request no se compila por defecto: a\xf1ade `--with-http_auth_request_module` al compilar, o comprueba `nginx -V` en un nginx empaquetado.\\n\\n## Opci\xf3n 1: Forward auth propio de Casdoor {#option-1-casdoors-own-forward-auth}\\n\\nCasdoor puede responder \xe9l mismo a la subpetici\xf3n de nginx. A\xf1ades un **Site** en Casdoor cuyo **Domain** sea el dominio de la aplicaci\xf3n y cuyo **Casdoor app** sea la aplicaci\xf3n en la que inician sesi\xf3n los usuarios, y a\xf1ades `https://app.example.com/caswaf-handler` a los **Redirect URLs** de esa aplicaci\xf3n. El host y el puerto pueden permanecer vac\xedos, ya que el tr\xe1fico no pasa a trav\xe9s de Casdoor.\\n\\nDado que `auth_request` no puede seguir una redirecci\xf3n, Casdoor responde a una subpetici\xf3n no autenticada con `401` y pone la URL de inicio de sesi\xf3n en `Location`. nginx convierte eso en un 302:\\n\\n```nginx\\nserver {\\n    server_name app.example.com;\\n\\n    proxy_buffer_size 16k;\\n    proxy_buffers 8 16k;\\n\\n    location = /caswaf-handler {\\n        proxy_pass http://casdoor:8000/api/forward-auth;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host $http_host;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Uri $request_uri;\\n    }\\n\\n    location = /casdoor-auth {\\n        internal;\\n        proxy_pass http://casdoor:8000/api/forward-auth;\\n        proxy_pass_request_body off;\\n        proxy_set_header Content-Length \\"\\";\\n        proxy_set_header X-Original-URL $scheme://$http_host$request_uri;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Method $request_method;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\\n    }\\n\\n    location / {\\n        auth_request /casdoor-auth;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_redirect $upstream_http_location;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_user $upstream_http_x_forwarded_user;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_email $upstream_http_x_forwarded_email;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_groups $upstream_http_x_forwarded_groups;\\n        error_page 401 =302 $casdoor_redirect;\\n\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-User $casdoor_user;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Email $casdoor_email;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Groups $casdoor_groups;\\n        proxy_pass http://app:3000;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nEl backend recibe al usuario en `X-Forwarded-User`, `X-Forwarded-Email` y `X-Forwarded-Groups`. El acceso sigue un [permission](/docs/permission/permission-configuration) en la aplicaci\xf3n, comprobado de nuevo al menos una vez por minuto, por lo que un usuario deshabilitado pierde el acceso r\xe1pidamente. Las **Public paths** del sitio (webhooks, comprobaciones de estado) pasan sin necesidad de inicio de sesi\xf3n.\\n\\nConsulte [Usar su propio proxy inverso](/docs/site/overview#using-your-own-reverse-proxy-forward-auth) para la referencia y [Agregar inicio de sesi\xf3n a una aplicaci\xf3n sin OIDC](/docs/site/protect-app) para una gu\xeda paso a paso con Grafana. La aplicaci\xf3n solo debe ser accesible a trav\xe9s de nginx, o cualquiera puede enviar `X-Forwarded-User` por s\xed mismo.\\n\\n## Opci\xf3n 2: casdoor-forward-auth u OAuth2 Proxy {#option-2-casdoor-forward-auth-or-oauth2-proxy}\\n\\nSi prefiere no crear un sitio de Casdoor para cada aplicaci\xf3n, ejecute un peque\xf1o servicio que realice el flujo OIDC contra Casdoor y responda a la sub-solicitud.\\n\\n[casdoor-forward-auth](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-forward-auth) es el servicio independiente de Casdoor para esto (reemplaz\xf3 a traefik-casdoor-auth). Mantiene al usuario en una cookie firmada sin estado en el servidor. Para nginx expone `/verify`, que responde con 200 y encabezados de identidad o 401, y nginx redirige a su `/login` ante un 401:\\n\\n```nginx\\nserver {\\n    server_name app.example.com;\\n\\n    location = /_casdoor_verify {\\n        internal;\\n        proxy_pass http://casdoor-forward-auth:9999/verify?groups=built-in/dev;\\n        proxy_pass_request_body off;\\n        proxy_set_header Content-Length \\"\\";\\n    }\\n\\n    location @casdoor_login {\\n        return 302 https://auth.example.com/login?rd=$scheme://$http_host$request_uri;\\n    }\\n\\n    location / {\\n        auth_request /_casdoor_verify;\\n        error_page 401 = @casdoor_login;\\n\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_user $upstream_http_x_forwarded_user;\\n        auth_request_set $casdoor_roles $upstream_http_x_forwarded_roles;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-User $casdoor_user;\\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Roles $casdoor_roles;\\n        proxy_pass http://app:8080;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nLos par\xe1metros de consulta `roles` y `groups` en la URL de verificaci\xf3n restringen cada aplicaci\xf3n a los titulares de un rol de Casdoor o miembros de un grupo; los usuarios conectados sin ellos reciben 403. Los roles y grupos se leen en el inicio de sesi\xf3n y se mantienen durante la sesi\xf3n, por lo que los cambios se aplican despu\xe9s de que el usuario vuelva a iniciar sesi\xf3n. Establecer `COOKIE_DOMAIN=example.com` permite que un solo inicio de sesi\xf3n cubra todos los subdominios. La [gu\xeda de Traefik](/docs/integration/go/traefik) cubre el mismo servicio para Traefik.\\n\\n[OAuth2 Proxy](https://oauth2-proxy.github.io/oauth2-proxy/configuration/integrations/nginx) funciona de la misma manera con `provider = \\"oidc\\"` y `oidc_issuer_url` configurado con la URL de su Casdoor. Su endpoint `/oauth2/auth` devuelve 202 o 401, los encabezados de usuario y correo electr\xf3nico necesitan `--set-xauthrequest`, y el manejo de encabezados reenviados en su ejemplo de nginx requiere `--reverse-proxy`. Dos configuraciones de Casdoor son importantes: use el formato de token `JWT-Standard`, o los usuarios con un correo electr\xf3nico no verificado ser\xe1n rechazados, y mantenga el scope `profile` para que se devuelvan los grupos y roles. Consulte [OAuth2 Proxy con Casdoor](/docs/integration/go/oauth2-proxy) y la [gu\xeda de la edici\xf3n comunitaria de nginx](/docs/integration/C++/NginxCommunityVersion).\\n\\n## Opci\xf3n 3: el m\xf3dulo de nginx de Casdoor {#option-3-the-casdoor-nginx-module}\\n\\n[casdoor-nginx-module](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-nginx-module) realiza el inicio de sesi\xf3n dentro de nginx, sin un proceso separado. Los navegadores sin sesi\xf3n van a la p\xe1gina de inicio de sesi\xf3n de Casdoor; el m\xf3dulo intercambia el c\xf3digo con PKCE, verifica la firma del token de acceso, `exp`, `aud` y tipo contra el certificado de Casdoor, y mantiene al usuario en una cookie firmada. Los clientes API pueden enviar `Authorization: Bearer` con un token de acceso de Casdoor, verificado localmente sin llamar a Casdoor.\\n\\nRequiere nginx 1.22 o superior y OpenSSL 1.1.1 o superior. Puede compilarlo como un m\xf3dulo din\xe1mico contra el nginx que ya ejecuta:\\n\\n```bash\\n./configure --with-compat --with-http_ssl_module --add-dynamic-module=/path/to/casdoor-nginx-module\\nmake modules\\ncp objs/ngx_http_casdoor_module.so /etc/nginx/modules/\\n```\\n\\n```nginx\\nload_module modules/ngx_http_casdoor_module.so;\\n\\nevents {}\\n\\nhttp {\\n    server {\\n        listen 443 ssl;\\n        server_name app.example.com;\\n        ssl_certificate     /etc/nginx/app.crt;\\n        ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/app.key;\\n\\n        casdoor_endpoint        https://door.example.com;\\n        casdoor_client_id       <client-id>;\\n        casdoor_client_secret   <client-secret>;\\n        casdoor_certificate     /etc/nginx/casdoor.pem;\\n        casdoor_redirect_uri    https://app.example.com/casdoor/callback;\\n        casdoor_token_location  /_casdoor/token;\\n\\n        location = /_casdoor/token {\\n            internal;\\n            proxy_pass https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/access_token;\\n            proxy_ssl_server_name on;\\n        }\\n\\n        location = /casdoor/callback {\\n            casdoor_callback;\\n        }\\n\\n        location / {\\n            casdoor on;\\n            proxy_set_header X-User $casdoor_user;\\n            proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nReemplace los marcadores de posici\xf3n con los valores de la aplicaci\xf3n. La sesi\xf3n no se vuelve a verificar con Casdoor hasta que expire, por lo que deshabilitar a un usuario surte efecto cuando termina la sesi\xf3n; `casdoor_session_timeout` acorta ese periodo. El m\xf3dulo no tiene comprobaciones de roles o grupos. En NGINX Plus, consulte [la gu\xeda de NGINX Plus](/docs/integration/C++/Nginx) en su lugar.\\n\\n## Opci\xf3n 4: la aplicaci\xf3n es un cliente CAS {#option-4-the-app-is-a-cas-client}\\n\\nSi la aplicaci\xf3n ya habla CAS, como una aplicaci\xf3n Java con el cliente Apereo CAS, nginx solo act\xfaa como proxy y Casdoor es el servidor CAS. Cada aplicaci\xf3n de Casdoor tiene endpoints CAS bajo `/cas/<organization>/<application>`: `/login` y `/logout`, `/validate` para CAS 1.0, `/serviceValidate`, `/proxyValidate` y `/proxy` para 2.0, `/p3/serviceValidate` y `/p3/proxyValidate` para 3.0, y `/samlValidate` para validaci\xf3n SAML 1.1.\\n\\nLa aplicaci\xf3n env\xeda el navegador a `/login?service=...`, Casdoor redirige de vuelta con `ticket=ST-...`, y la aplicaci\xf3n lo valida:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl \\"https://door.example.com/cas/my-org/my-app/p3/serviceValidate?ticket=ST-1234567890&service=https%3A%2F%2Fapp.example.com%2F\\"\\n```\\n\\nUna respuesta exitosa es un `cas:serviceResponse` con `cas:user` y atributos como correo electr\xf3nico, nombre para mostrar y `memberOf`; agregue `format=json` para JSON. Cosas que suelen confundir:\\n\\n- Cada URL `service` debe estar listada en los **Redirect URLs** de la aplicaci\xf3n, y el `service` en la validaci\xf3n debe coincidir exactamente con aquel para el que se emiti\xf3 el ticket.\\n- Los tickets funcionan una sola vez y caducan despu\xe9s de 5 minutos.\\n- Los tickets se guardan en memoria, por lo que con varias r\xe9plicas de Casdoor, la validaci\xf3n debe llegar a la r\xe9plica que emiti\xf3 el ticket (sesiones adherentes).\\n- Detr\xe1s de nginx, pase `Host` y `X-Forwarded-Proto` para que la aplicaci\xf3n construya la misma URL `service` que vio el navegador.\\n\\nLa configuraci\xf3n, incluidos los filtros del cliente Java para cada versi\xf3n de CAS, est\xe1 en [Conectar un cliente CAS](/docs/how-to-connect/cas).\\n\\n## Opci\xf3n 5: dejar que Casdoor sea el proxy inverso {#option-5-let-casdoor-be-the-reverse-proxy}\\n\\nCuando nada m\xe1s est\xe1 delante de la aplicaci\xf3n, Casdoor puede servirla directamente: su proxy de sitio escucha en `gatewayHttpPort` y `gatewayHttpsPort` (80 y 443 por defecto) una vez que existe un sitio. Configure el **Host** del sitio con el backend, elija un certificado, establezca **Casdoor app**, y apunte el DNS a Casdoor. El inicio de sesi\xf3n, los permisos y los encabezados se comportan como en la opci\xf3n 1. Consulte [Sitios](/docs/site/overview).\\n\\n## Agregar MFA {#adding-mfa}\\n\\nMFA no necesita ning\xfan cambio en nginx ni en la aplicaci\xf3n. Casdoor solicita el segundo factor en su propia p\xe1gina de inicio de sesi\xf3n antes de emitir un c\xf3digo de autorizaci\xf3n OAuth o un ticket de servicio CAS, por lo que todas las opciones anteriores lo obtienen. Los usuarios pueden usar una aplicaci\xf3n TOTP, SMS, correo electr\xf3nico o RADIUS, con c\xf3digos de recuperaci\xf3n, y los administradores pueden requerir MFA para una organizaci\xf3n con [Elementos de MFA](/docs/organization/mfa-items). Esto tambi\xe9n responde a \\"CAS 2FA\\": con Casdoor como servidor CAS, el segundo factor es parte del inicio de sesi\xf3n CAS.\\n\\nMFA se aplica en el inicio de sesi\xf3n, no por solicitud: una sesi\xf3n que nginx o un servicio forward-auth ya mantiene dura hasta que expira, y el **Tiempo de recordatorio de MFA** de la organizaci\xf3n permite a los usuarios omitir el segundo factor en un dispositivo confiable durante un tiempo. Consulte la [gu\xeda de MFA](/docs/user/multi-factor-authentication) y [Qu\xe9 es MFA](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/what-is-mfa).\\n\\nUna vez conectado a Casdoor, los usuarios acceden a otras aplicaciones que los env\xedan a Casdoor sin una nueva solicitud de contrase\xf1a; con **Auto signin**, Casdoor tambi\xe9n omite el selector de cuenta. Consulte [inicio de sesi\xf3n \xfanico](/docs/session/single-sign-on) y [Cierre de sesi\xf3n \xfanico](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/single-logout).\\n\\n## Cu\xe1l elegir {#which-one-to-pick}\\n\\n- Sin inicio de sesi\xf3n en la aplicaci\xf3n y nginx ya delante: autenticaci\xf3n hacia adelante de Casdoor.\\n- Un inicio de sesi\xf3n en varios subdominios, o comprobaciones de roles por ubicaci\xf3n: casdoor-forward-auth (o OAuth2 Proxy si ya lo ejecuta).\\n- Usted compila nginx usted mismo y tambi\xe9n protege las API con tokens bearer: casdoor-nginx-module.\\n- La aplicaci\xf3n admite CAS: endpoints CAS de Casdoor, nginx como proxy simple.\\n- A\xfan no hay proxy: proxy de sitio de Casdoor.\\n\\n## Referencias {#references}\\n\\n- nginx: [ngx_http_auth_request_module](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_auth_request_module.html)\\n- OAuth2 Proxy: [integraci\xf3n con nginx](https://oauth2-proxy.github.io/oauth2-proxy/configuration/integrations/nginx)\\n- Apereo: [Especificaci\xf3n del protocolo CAS](https://apereo.github.io/cas/7.1.x/protocol/CAS-Protocol-Specification.html), [mod_auth_cas](https://github.com/apereo/mod_auth_cas)\\n- Casdoor: [casdoor-forward-auth](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-forward-auth), [casdoor-nginx-module](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-nginx-module)\\n- Documentaci\xf3n de Casdoor: [Sites](/docs/site/overview), [Conectar un cliente CAS](/docs/how-to-connect/cas), [MFA](/docs/user/multi-factor-authentication)"},{"id":"oidc-rp-initiated-logout","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/oidc-rp-initiated-logout","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-oidc-rp-initiated-logout.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-oidc-rp-initiated-logout.mdx","title":"RP-Initiated Logout and SSO Logout in OpenID Connect: How Casdoor Signs Users Out","description":"How OIDC RP-initiated logout, back-channel logout and token revocation work in Casdoor, what each one clears, and how single sign-out spans several apps.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"Logout","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/logout"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Session","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/session"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/o-auth"}],"readingTime":10.53,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"oidc-rp-initiated-logout","title":"RP-Initiated Logout and SSO Logout in OpenID Connect: How Casdoor Signs Users Out","description":"How OIDC RP-initiated logout, back-channel logout and token revocation work in Casdoor, what each one clears, and how single sign-out spans several apps.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","OIDC","Logout","SSO","Session","OAuth"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Nginx CAS Authentication and SSO: Protecting Any App Behind Nginx with Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/nginx-sso-authentication"},"nextItem":{"title":"RADIUS Multi-Factor Authentication for VPN and Wi-Fi: TOTP 2FA with the Casdoor RADIUS Server","permalink":"/es/blog/radius-mfa-vpn-wifi"}},"content":"Logging a user out of an OpenID Connect app takes more than deleting your own session cookie. The user is still signed in at the identity provider, so the next \\"Sign in\\" click goes straight through without a password, and the other apps that share that identity provider session stay signed in too. A proper logout has three parts: clear your app\'s local session, send the browser to the provider\'s `end_session_endpoint` (RP-initiated logout) so the provider session ends, and let the provider tell the other apps through back-channel logout. With Casdoor, the end-session endpoint is `/api/logout`, back-channel logout is configured per application, and refresh tokens can be revoked at `/api/login/oauth/revoke`.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Logout mechanisms at a glance\\n\\n| Mechanism | Who triggers it | What it clears | Casdoor support |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Local app logout | Your app | Your app\'s session cookie and stored tokens only | Your code |\\n| RP-initiated logout | Your app, by redirecting the browser | The Casdoor browser session and the token of the `id_token_hint` | Yes, `GET` or `POST /api/logout`, announced as `end_session_endpoint` |\\n| Back-channel logout | Casdoor, server to server | The sessions of other apps, each app ends its own | Yes, per application **Backchannel logout URL** |\\n| Front-channel logout | The provider, through hidden iframes | The sessions of other apps, through the browser | No, Casdoor doesn\'t announce `frontchannel_logout_supported` |\\n| Token revocation (RFC 7009) | Your app, server or client | One access or refresh token, and the tokens that share its refresh token | Yes, `POST /api/login/oauth/revoke`, announced as `revocation_endpoint` |\\n| Casdoor SSO logout | Your app, with the user\'s token or cookie | All sessions and tokens of the user, or the current session only | Yes, `/api/sso-logout`, Casdoor-specific |\\n| SAML single logout | Casdoor or a SAML service provider | Sessions in SAML apps | Yes, signed `LogoutRequest` |\\n\\n## Local session vs. identity provider session\\n\\nThere are at least two sessions involved in every OIDC sign-in, and often three:\\n\\n1. **Your app\'s session.** Usually a cookie that maps to the user and the tokens you got from the token endpoint.\\n2. **The Casdoor session.** A cookie on the Casdoor domain. As long as it is valid, Casdoor signs the user in to any app of the organization without a password, after a one-click account picker or silently with automatic sign-in. That is what makes [single sign-on](/docs/session/single-sign-on) work.\\n3. **An upstream identity provider session**, when the user signed in to Casdoor through Google, GitHub, an enterprise IdP and so on.\\n\\nAuth0 describes the same three layers in its logout documentation (application, Auth0 and identity provider session layers), and the model applies to any OIDC provider.\\n\\n\\"Localized application session termination\\" ends only the first one: your app forgets the user, the Casdoor session lives on. That is what happens when you just clear a cookie. \\"Centralized single sign-out\\" ends the Casdoor session and tells every app of the user to end its own. Most users who click \\"Sign out\\" expect the second, especially on a shared computer.\\n\\nFor the third layer, Casdoor calls the logout URL of a Custom OAuth provider when the application signs users in through one and the provider has a logout URL set. It doesn\'t sign the user out of Google or GitHub.\\n\\n## RP-initiated logout with the end-session endpoint\\n\\n[OpenID Connect RP-Initiated Logout 1.0](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-rpinitiated-1_0.html) defines how a relying party (your app) asks the provider to end its session. The provider publishes the endpoint as `end_session_endpoint` in its discovery document, and the request carries these parameters:\\n\\n- `id_token_hint`: the ID token the provider issued to your app. The spec marks it RECOMMENDED. Casdoor uses it to find and expire that exact token.\\n- `post_logout_redirect_uri`: where the browser goes afterwards. The spec requires it to be registered in advance. Casdoor checks it against the application\'s **Redirect URLs** and refuses to redirect to anything else.\\n- `state`: an opaque value that Casdoor appends to the redirect, so your app can tell its own logout round trip from a forged one.\\n- `client_id`: identifies the app when you can\'t send an ID token.\\n\\nThe spec also defines `logout_hint` and `ui_locales`. Casdoor doesn\'t read them.\\n\\nCasdoor\'s discovery document at `/.well-known/openid-configuration` announces `end_session_endpoint` as `https://<your-casdoor>/api/logout`, so OIDC libraries that support RP-initiated logout pick it up without configuration. The endpoint accepts `GET` and `POST`, as the spec requires.\\n\\nWhat Casdoor does with the request, briefly (the [RP-initiated logout reference](/docs/session/rp-initiated-logout) has every branch):\\n\\n- With `id_token_hint`, Casdoor looks up the token, sends back-channel logout tokens to the user\'s applications, expires the token (its refresh token stops working too), clears the browser\'s Casdoor session and redirects to `post_logout_redirect_uri` with `state`.\\n- Without `id_token_hint`, Casdoor signs out the current browser session and expires the tokens issued in it. If the browser has no session left, it still redirects when it knows the app, from the session or from `client_id`.\\n- Without `post_logout_redirect_uri`, the endpoint returns a JSON response instead of a redirect.\\n\\n### Example: sign-out button in a browser app\\n\\nA single-page app that signed in with the authorization code flow and PKCE keeps the ID token after the token exchange. The sign-out handler clears local state first, then hands the browser to Casdoor:\\n\\n```js\\nconst ISSUER = \\"https://door.example.com\\";\\nconst CLIENT_ID = \\"your-client-id\\";\\n\\nasync function signOut() {\\n  const idToken = sessionStorage.getItem(\\"id_token\\");\\n\\n  const discovery = await fetch(`${ISSUER}/.well-known/openid-configuration`)\\n    .then((r) => r.json());\\n\\n  // 1. Forget the user locally, whatever happens next\\n  sessionStorage.clear();\\n\\n  // 2. Remember a state value to check when Casdoor sends the browser back\\n  const state = crypto.randomUUID();\\n  sessionStorage.setItem(\\"logout_state\\", state);\\n\\n  // 3. End the Casdoor session\\n  const url = new URL(discovery.end_session_endpoint);\\n  if (idToken) {\\n    url.searchParams.set(\\"id_token_hint\\", idToken);\\n  } else {\\n    url.searchParams.set(\\"client_id\\", CLIENT_ID);\\n  }\\n  url.searchParams.set(\\"post_logout_redirect_uri\\", \\"https://app.example.com/signed-out\\");\\n  url.searchParams.set(\\"state\\", state);\\n  window.location.assign(url.toString());\\n}\\n\\n// On https://app.example.com/signed-out\\nconst returned = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get(\\"state\\");\\nif (returned !== sessionStorage.getItem(\\"logout_state\\")) {\\n  console.warn(\\"Unexpected logout redirect\\");\\n}\\nsessionStorage.removeItem(\\"logout_state\\");\\n```\\n\\nAdd `https://app.example.com/signed-out` to the application\'s **Redirect URLs** in Casdoor, or Casdoor returns an error instead of redirecting. A server-rendered app does the same from its logout route: destroy the server session, then answer with a 302 to the end-session URL. For Spring Security, see the [Spring Boot SSO post](/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc).\\n\\n## Back-channel logout: telling the other apps\\n\\nEnding the Casdoor session stops new silent sign-ins, but app B still has its own session cookie from an hour ago. Something has to tell app B. [OpenID Connect Back-Channel Logout 1.0](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-backchannel-1_0.html) does this server to server: the provider sends a `POST` with a form parameter `logout_token`, a signed JWT that names the user, to each app\'s registered back-channel URI.\\n\\nIn Casdoor, set **Backchannel logout URL** on the application\'s edit page. When a user signs out through `/api/logout`, Casdoor sends a logout token to every application that holds an active token for that user and has this URL set. The token is signed with the application\'s certificate, the same key as its ID tokens, and contains:\\n\\n- `iss` and `aud`: the same issuer and client ID as the app\'s ID tokens\\n- `sub`: the user\'s ID, the same value as `sub` in the ID token\\n- `iat` and `jti`\\n- `events` with the member `http://schemas.openid.net/event/backchannel-logout`\\n- `sid` only for some logouts, such as [SSO logout](/docs/session/single-sign-out), and not for `/api/logout`\\n\\nCasdoor\'s ID tokens don\'t carry a `sid` claim, so key your local sessions by `sub` and end all of them for that user. Casdoor\'s logout token also has no `exp` claim, which the final spec lists as required, so check freshness with `iat` and don\'t make `exp` mandatory in your validator. Casdoor sends each token once, with a 30-second timeout, and doesn\'t retry. For applications outside the `built-in` organization, it refuses to call loopback and link-local addresses, so `http://localhost` won\'t receive logout tokens during development.\\n\\n### Example: a back-channel logout receiver\\n\\nThis Express handler follows the validation steps of the spec, using the [`jose`](https://github.com/panva/jose) library:\\n\\n```js\\nimport express from \\"express\\";\\nimport { createRemoteJWKSet, jwtVerify } from \\"jose\\";\\n\\n// Take both values from the discovery document of your Casdoor\\nconst ISSUER = \\"https://door.example.com\\";\\nconst JWKS = createRemoteJWKSet(new URL(`${ISSUER}/.well-known/jwks`));\\nconst CLIENT_ID = process.env.CASDOOR_CLIENT_ID;\\nconst EVENT = \\"http://schemas.openid.net/event/backchannel-logout\\";\\n\\nconst seenJti = new Map();\\nconst app = express();\\n\\napp.post(\\n  \\"/oidc/backchannel-logout\\",\\n  express.urlencoded({ extended: false }),\\n  async (req, res) => {\\n    res.set(\\"Cache-Control\\", \\"no-store\\");\\n    try {\\n      const { payload } = await jwtVerify(req.body.logout_token, JWKS, {\\n        issuer: ISSUER,\\n        audience: CLIENT_ID,\\n        maxTokenAge: \\"2m\\",\\n      });\\n\\n      if (!payload.events || typeof payload.events[EVENT] !== \\"object\\") {\\n        throw new Error(\\"not a logout token\\");\\n      }\\n      if (!payload.sub && !payload.sid) {\\n        throw new Error(\\"no sub or sid\\");\\n      }\\n      if (\\"nonce\\" in payload) {\\n        throw new Error(\\"nonce is not allowed\\");\\n      }\\n      if (seenJti.has(payload.jti)) {\\n        throw new Error(\\"replayed token\\");\\n      }\\n      seenJti.set(payload.jti, Date.now());\\n\\n      // Your own helper: delete every session row that belongs to this user\\n      await destroySessionsForUser(payload.sub);\\n      res.sendStatus(200);\\n    } catch (err) {\\n      res.status(400).json({ error: \\"invalid_request\\", error_description: err.message });\\n    }\\n  }\\n);\\n```\\n\\n`jwtVerify` checks the signature, `iss`, `aud`, and with `maxTokenAge` the `iat` claim. To find sessions by user, store `sub` from the ID token in each session record at sign-in. Prune `seenJti` periodically, or keep it in Redis.\\n\\n## Revoking refresh tokens\\n\\nA refresh token outlives the session that created it. If your app keeps one, a logout that never touches it leaves a credential that can mint new access tokens for days.\\n\\nThe end-session call with `id_token_hint` already expires that token, refresh token included. Revoke explicitly when the logout doesn\'t go through the browser, for example in a CLI, a desktop or mobile app, or when the user leaves only your app and keeps the Casdoor session. Casdoor implements [RFC 7009](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7009) at the `revocation_endpoint` of its discovery document:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -X POST https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/revoke \\\\\\n  -u \\"$CLIENT_ID:$CLIENT_SECRET\\" \\\\\\n  -d token=\\"$REFRESH_TOKEN\\" \\\\\\n  -d token_type_hint=refresh_token\\n```\\n\\nA public client that used PKCE can send `client_id` in the body without a secret. Revoking a refresh token also expires the access tokens issued with it. After that, the refresh endpoint answers `invalid_grant` and introspection returns `\\"active\\": false`; [When Casdoor revokes tokens](/docs/token/overview#when-casdoor-revokes-tokens) lists every event that does this.\\n\\nOne limit applies to every provider that issues JWT access tokens: an API that only checks the signature and `exp` keeps accepting a revoked access token until it expires. Keep access tokens short-lived, or have the API call [token introspection](/docs/how-to-connect/oauth#how-to-verify-access-token) when it must notice a logout at once.\\n\\n## Single sign-out across several apps\\n\\nWith the pieces above, single sign-out works like this when the user clicks \\"Sign out\\" in app A:\\n\\n1. App A clears its session and redirects to `/api/logout` with `id_token_hint`.\\n2. Casdoor sends logout tokens to the back-channel URLs of the user\'s other apps, expires the token, and ends the browser\'s Casdoor session.\\n3. Apps B and C receive the logout token and delete their sessions for that `sub`.\\n4. The browser lands on app A\'s signed-out page. Opening app B now shows the Casdoor sign-in page.\\n\\nAn app without a back-channel URL stays signed in until its own session or access token expires. If you can\'t add a receiver to an app, keep its sessions short or make it validate the token on each request.\\n\\nFor \\"sign me out everywhere\\" after a lost laptop, use Casdoor\'s [SSO logout endpoint](/docs/session/single-sign-out) with `logoutAll=true`: it expires every token and deletes every session of the user, and also sends signed notifications through notification providers to apps that don\'t speak OIDC. Administrators can end single sessions on the [Sessions page](/docs/session/management). For SAML apps, see [SAML single logout](/docs/how-to-connect/saml/overview#single-logout). Background reading: [single logout](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/single-logout) and [SAML single logout](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/saml-single-logout) on www.casdoor.com.\\n\\n## Referencias {#references}\\n\\n- [OpenID Connect RP-Initiated Logout 1.0](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-rpinitiated-1_0.html)\\n- [OpenID Connect Back-Channel Logout 1.0](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-backchannel-1_0.html)\\n- [OpenID Connect Front-Channel Logout 1.0](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-frontchannel-1_0.html)\\n- [RFC 7009: OAuth 2.0 Token Revocation](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7009)\\n- [Auth0: Logout](https://auth0.com/docs/authenticate/login/logout)\\n- [Casdoor: RP-initiated logout](/docs/session/rp-initiated-logout)\\n- [Casdoor: Sign users out of all applications](/docs/session/single-sign-out)\\n- [Casdoor: Connect a standard OIDC client](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client)"},{"id":"radius-mfa-vpn-wifi","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/radius-mfa-vpn-wifi","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-radius-mfa-vpn-wifi.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-radius-mfa-vpn-wifi.mdx","title":"RADIUS Multi-Factor Authentication for VPN and Wi-Fi: TOTP 2FA with the Casdoor RADIUS Server","description":"Add TOTP 2FA to VPN logins over RADIUS with Casdoor: why only PAP works, how Access-Challenge asks for the code, which VPN clients support it, and Wi-Fi limits.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"RADIUS","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/radius"},{"inline":true,"label":"MFA","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/mfa"},{"inline":true,"label":"2FA","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/2-fa"},{"inline":true,"label":"TOTP","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/totp"},{"inline":true,"label":"VPN","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/vpn"},{"inline":true,"label":"Wi-Fi","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/wi-fi"},{"inline":true,"label":"802.1X","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/802-1-x"}],"readingTime":10.04,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"radius-mfa-vpn-wifi","title":"RADIUS Multi-Factor Authentication for VPN and Wi-Fi: TOTP 2FA with the Casdoor RADIUS Server","description":"Add TOTP 2FA to VPN logins over RADIUS with Casdoor: why only PAP works, how Access-Challenge asks for the code, which VPN clients support it, and Wi-Fi limits.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","RADIUS","MFA","2FA","TOTP","VPN","Wi-Fi","802.1X"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"RP-Initiated Logout and SSO Logout in OpenID Connect: How Casdoor Signs Users Out","permalink":"/es/blog/oidc-rp-initiated-logout"},"nextItem":{"title":"SSO de Spring Boot con OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server y Spring Cloud Gateway con Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc"}},"content":"To put multi-factor authentication in front of a VPN with RADIUS, the VPN gateway sends the username and password to the RADIUS server with PAP, the server checks the password and answers with an `Access-Challenge`, the client shows a prompt, and the user types the 2FA code from an authenticator app into a second request. Casdoor ships a RADIUS server that does exactly this for users who have TOTP set up, so a gateway that can show an `Access-Challenge` prompt (FortiGate SSL VPN is one whose vendor documents it) gets a password plus a 2FA code without any extra software. Wi-Fi with WPA2/WPA3-Enterprise is different: 802.1X runs over EAP, which has no place for a typed code, so Casdoor has a switch to skip the code for those clients.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nIf you want the background on RADIUS itself and how it compares with LDAP for network devices, read [RADIUS vs LDAP](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/radius-vs-ldap) first. This post is about the MFA part: what goes over the wire, which clients can carry a second factor, and how to turn it on in Casdoor.\\n\\n## Which clients can do RADIUS MFA with Casdoor\\n\\nThe table lists what each vendor documents. \\"Challenge support\\" means the client shows the `Access-Challenge` prompt to the user and sends the answer back. Where the vendor page we checked doesn\'t say, the table says so instead of guessing.\\n\\n| Client | RADIUS auth method to use with Casdoor | Access-Challenge support | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| FortiGate SSL VPN | PAP (set the method to PAP, or leave Default, which tries PAP first) | Yes, per Fortinet | Fortinet says SSL VPN forwards the challenge to the client and waits for the answer |\\n| FortiGate IPsec VPN (including FortiClient) | PAP | Not forwarded by default | Fortinet\'s fix requires MS-CHAP on the RADIUS server, which Casdoor doesn\'t speak; use SSL VPN or skip MFA |\\n| Cisco ASA (AnyConnect / Secure Client) | PAP, the ASA default for all connection types | Not stated in the ASA RADIUS configuration guide | Don\'t enable `password-management` on the tunnel group: it switches the ASA to MS-CHAPv2 |\\n| OpenVPN Access Server | PAP (it also offers CHAP and MS-CHAPv2) | Not stated on the RADIUS settings page | Accounting is a separate toggle, default port 1813 |\\n| pfSense (RADIUS auth server, used by its OpenVPN) | PAP (also MD5-CHAP, MS-CHAPv1, MS-CHAPv2) | Not stated in the Netgate docs | Netgate\'s OTP examples put the code inside the password field, which Casdoor doesn\'t accept |\\n| WireGuard | None | Not applicable | Peers authenticate with public keys only, so there is no login for RADIUS to check |\\n| Wi-Fi, WPA2/WPA3-Enterprise (802.1X) | EAP (PEAP, EAP-TTLS and so on) | No typed-code prompt | Casdoor doesn\'t implement EAP; see the Wi-Fi section |\\n\\nWhere a cell says \\"not stated\\", test with the real VPN client before you roll it out, and check that the prompt appears and the code goes through.\\n\\n## Why only PAP can carry the password Casdoor checks\\n\\nRADIUS has several ways to send a password, and they decide what the server is able to verify.\\n\\nWith **PAP**, the client puts the password into the `User-Password` attribute, hidden with an MD5 hash of the shared secret and the request authenticator (RFC 2865, section 5.2). The server reverses that with the same secret, gets the cleartext password, and can check it against a stored hash or an LDAP bind. Casdoor reads `User-Password` and runs the same password check as its sign-in page.\\n\\nWith **CHAP**, the client never sends the password. It sends a hash of the password and a random challenge. RFC 2865 says plainly that CHAP needs the user\'s password in cleartext on the server to recompute that hash. **MS-CHAPv2** has the same shape with an NT hash. An identity provider that stores passwords as one-way hashes (Casdoor\'s built-in organization uses bcrypt) can\'t verify either one. The Casdoor RADIUS server only reads `User-Password`, so a CHAP or MS-CHAPv2 request reaches it with no password and is rejected.\\n\\nBecause the PAP hiding is only MD5 with the shared secret, use a long random secret, keep RADIUS on a private network or a VPN between the gateway and Casdoor, and turn on the `Message-Authenticator` attribute on the client. Casdoor verifies `Message-Authenticator` when a request carries it and signs its reply in that case, which is the mitigation for Blast-RADIUS (CVE-2024-3596). The details are in the [RADIUS server docs](/docs/radius/overview#message-authenticator).\\n\\n## Access-Challenge vs appending the code to the password\\n\\nThere are two common ways to get a 2FA code through RADIUS.\\n\\n1. **Access-Challenge.** The server checks the password, then replies with `Access-Challenge`, a `State` attribute and a `Reply-Message`. The client shows the message, the user types the code, and the client sends a new `Access-Request` with the code in `User-Password` and the same `State`. RFC 2865 section 4.4 also says what happens on a client without challenge support: it MUST treat the `Access-Challenge` as an `Access-Reject`.\\n2. **Code appended to the password.** The user types `password123456` into the one password field, and the server splits off the last six digits. This works with any client, which is why products built for older gateways offer it.\\n\\nCasdoor implements only the first. Here is the flow in its RADIUS server (`radius/server.go`):\\n\\n- The first `Access-Request` must carry the correct password. A user with MFA turned on gets `Access-Challenge`, a random `State`, and the `Reply-Message` `please enter OTP`.\\n- The `State` is valid for 120 seconds and can be used once. It is tied to the organization and username of the first request.\\n- The second `Access-Request` must carry that `State` and the six-digit TOTP code from the user\'s authenticator app in `User-Password`. Casdoor checks the code against the user\'s TOTP secret and answers `Access-Accept` or `Access-Reject`.\\n- Wrong codes count toward the failed sign-in limit of the user\'s application (5 attempts and a 15-minute freeze by default), separately from wrong passwords.\\n\\nTwo consequences follow. A client that can only do \\"password plus code in one field\\" will fail, because Casdoor checks the whole string as the password. And only TOTP works over RADIUS: SMS and email codes need Casdoor to send a message after the password step, which the RADIUS server doesn\'t do. A user whose only MFA method is SMS or email gets the challenge and then a reject, so have RADIUS users set up an authenticator app.\\n\\n## Wi-Fi: 802.1X and WPA-Enterprise\\n\\nOn Wi-Fi with WPA2-Enterprise or WPA3-Enterprise, the access point doesn\'t collect a password itself. It relays an EAP conversation between the device (the supplicant) and the RADIUS server, and RADIUS carries the EAP packets in `EAP-Message` attributes. In that setup `Access-Challenge` is already in use: every round trip of the EAP exchange is an `Access-Request` and `Access-Challenge` pair (RFC 3579). RFC 3579 also says a message carrying `EAP-Message` must not include `Reply-Message`, so there is no field for \\"please enter OTP\\" and no dialog on the phone or laptop to type a code into.\\n\\nTwo practical points for Casdoor:\\n\\n- Casdoor\'s RADIUS server doesn\'t implement EAP. An access point can\'t talk to it directly for WPA-Enterprise. You need an EAP server in front that terminates the TLS tunnel and forwards a plain PAP request. FreeRADIUS can do this with EAP-TTLS and PAP as the inner method, proxying the inner request to another RADIUS server, and the devices must be set to TTLS with PAP inside. PEAP with MS-CHAPv2, the default on many devices, won\'t work against Casdoor for the reason in the PAP section.\\n- For those users, turn on **Skip MFA for RADIUS** on the organization. Casdoor then accepts users who have MFA after the password check alone. MFA on the web sign-in page is not affected.\\n\\n## Ports, shared secret and accounting\\n\\nRFC 2865 assigns UDP port 1812 to RADIUS authentication, and RFC 2866 assigns 1813 to accounting. Casdoor listens on one UDP port, `radiusServerPort`, and handles both `Access-Request` and `Accounting-Request` there. So point the gateway\'s accounting at the same port as authentication (1812 by default), not at 1813, or accounting packets will go nowhere. If Casdoor runs in a container, publish the port as UDP, for example `-p 1812:1812/udp`.\\n\\nThe shared secret is one value for all clients, `radiusSecret`. Every gateway that talks to Casdoor uses it.\\n\\nFor accounting, Casdoor records `Start`, `Interim-Update` and `Stop` messages in its database (table `radius_accounting`), with the session ID, session time, NAS address, framed IP and byte and packet counters. It only does that for organizations with RADIUS turned on.\\n\\n## Configurar Casdoor {#configure-casdoor}\\n\\n**1. Set the RADIUS options.** In `conf/app.conf`, or as environment variables with the same names:\\n\\n```ini\\nradiusServerPort = 1812\\nradiusDefaultOrganization = \\"my-company\\"\\nradiusSecret = \\"a-long-random-string-shared-with-the-gateway\\"\\n```\\n\\n`radiusDefaultOrganization` is the organization Casdoor uses when a request has no `Class` attribute. If your gateway can\'t add a `Class` attribute to its requests, this setting decides which organization\'s users sign in over RADIUS. All keys are listed in the [configuration reference](/docs/basic/configuration).\\n\\n**2. Turn on RADIUS for the organization.** On the organization edit page, turn on **Enable RADIUS**. It is off by default and only global administrators can change it. While it is off, Casdoor rejects every `Access-Request` for that organization and ignores its accounting.\\n\\n**3. Decide on MFA for RADIUS.** Leave **Skip MFA for RADIUS** off for VPN gateways that support `Access-Challenge`. Turn it on for an organization whose RADIUS clients can\'t answer a challenge, such as WPA-Enterprise Wi-Fi.\\n\\n**4. Get users onto TOTP.** Users set up an authenticator app from their profile page, as described in [multi-factor authentication](/docs/user/multi-factor-authentication). To make it mandatory, add the TOTP item with the **Required** rule under the organization\'s [MFA items](/docs/organization/mfa-items). Note the order: a user who must still set up required MFA, or must change the password first, is rejected over RADIUS until they finish that on the Casdoor sign-in page.\\n\\n**5. Test from the command line.** FreeRADIUS\'s `radclient` sends PAP requests and prints the reply:\\n\\n```bash\\necho \'User-Name = \\"alice\\", User-Password = \\"her-password\\"\' \\\\\\n  | radclient -x casdoor.example.com:1812 auth a-long-random-string-shared-with-the-gateway\\n```\\n\\nFor a user with TOTP you should see `Access-Challenge` with a `State` and the `Reply-Message`. Send the code back with that `State` within 120 seconds:\\n\\n```bash\\necho \'User-Name = \\"alice\\", User-Password = \\"123456\\", State = 0x...\' \\\\\\n  | radclient -x casdoor.example.com:1812 auth a-long-random-string-shared-with-the-gateway\\n```\\n\\nPaste the `State` value from the first reply in place of `0x...`. An `Access-Accept` means the setup is right, and the gateway is next.\\n\\nIf you also want Casdoor to use an outside RADIUS server as a second factor for its own web sign-in, that is the opposite direction and is covered by the [RADIUS MFA provider](/docs/provider/mfa/radius).\\n\\n## Referencias {#references}\\n\\n- [RFC 2865: Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS)](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2865)\\n- [RFC 2866: RADIUS Accounting](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2866)\\n- [RFC 3579: RADIUS Support for EAP](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3579)\\n- [Fortinet: Difference in RADIUS Access-Challenge handling between SSL VPN and IPsec VPN](https://community.fortinet.com/t5/FortiGate/Technical-Tip-Difference-in-RADIUS-Access-Challenge-for/ta-p/406069)\\n- [FortiOS 7.6.5: Configuring a RADIUS server](https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/7.6.5/administration-guide/759080/configuring-a-radius-server)\\n- [Cisco ASA 9.22: RADIUS servers for AAA](https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/security/asa/asa922/configuration/general/asa-922-general-config/aaa-radius.html)\\n- [OpenVPN Access Server: Authentication](https://openvpn.net/as-docs/authentication-78640/)\\n- [pfSense: RADIUS authentication servers](https://docs.netgate.com/pfsense/en/latest/usermanager/radius.html)\\n- [WireGuard](https://www.wireguard.com/)\\n- [FreeRADIUS `inner-tunnel` virtual server](https://github.com/FreeRADIUS/freeradius-server/blob/v3.2.x/raddb/sites-available/inner-tunnel)\\n- [Casdoor RADIUS server](/docs/radius/overview)"},{"id":"spring-boot-sso-oidc","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-spring-boot-sso-oidc.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-spring-boot-sso-oidc.mdx","title":"SSO de Spring Boot con OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server y Spring Cloud Gateway con Casdoor","description":"Agregue inicio de sesi\xf3n \xfanico a Spring Boot con OpenID Connect y Casdoor: aplicaciones web oauth2Login, servidores de recursos JWT y Spring Cloud Gateway TokenRelay, con YAML.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Spring Boot","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/spring-boot"},{"inline":true,"label":"Spring Security","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/spring-security"},{"inline":true,"label":"Spring Cloud Gateway","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/spring-cloud-gateway"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth 2.0","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/o-auth-2-0"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"JWT","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/jwt"}],"readingTime":13.19,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"spring-boot-sso-oidc","title":"SSO de Spring Boot con OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server y Spring Cloud Gateway con Casdoor","description":"Agregue inicio de sesi\xf3n \xfanico a Spring Boot con OpenID Connect y Casdoor: aplicaciones web oauth2Login, servidores de recursos JWT y Spring Cloud Gateway TokenRelay, con YAML.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Spring Boot","Spring Security","Spring Cloud Gateway","OIDC","OAuth 2.0","SSO","JWT"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"RADIUS Multi-Factor Authentication for VPN and Wi-Fi: TOTP 2FA with the Casdoor RADIUS Server","permalink":"/es/blog/radius-mfa-vpn-wifi"},"nextItem":{"title":"DolphinScheduler, IoTDB and Other Apache Data Platforms: SSO with Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/sso-for-apache-data-platforms"}},"content":"Para agregar inicio de sesi\xf3n \xfanico a Spring Boot, registre cada aplicaci\xf3n como una aplicaci\xf3n en un proveedor de OpenID Connect como Casdoor, apunte Spring Security a la URL del emisor del proveedor y elija el m\xf3dulo de Spring que coincida con la aplicaci\xf3n: `oauth2Login()` para una aplicaci\xf3n web renderizada en el servidor, `oauth2ResourceServer().jwt()` para una API REST que recibe tokens bearer, y Spring Cloud Gateway con el filtro `TokenRelay` cuando una puerta de enlace inicia sesi\xf3n a los usuarios para un conjunto de microservicios. Los usuarios obtienen SSO porque todas las aplicaciones los env\xedan al mismo Casdoor, que recuerda la sesi\xf3n: la segunda aplicaci\xf3n redirige, Casdoor ya conoce al usuario y este vuelve directamente sin un formulario de inicio de sesi\xf3n. Esta publicaci\xf3n configura las tres formas contra un solo Casdoor, cada una con un `application.yml` funcional y un `SecurityFilterChain` personalizado.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Las tres formas de un vistazo {#the-three-shapes-at-a-glance}\\n\\n| Forma | Dependencia de Spring | Qui\xe9n mantiene el inicio de sesi\xf3n | Endpoints de Casdoor utilizados | Cadena de filtros |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Aplicaci\xf3n web | `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client` | La aplicaci\xf3n (sesi\xf3n HTTP) | discovery, authorize, token, userinfo, JWKS, logout | `SecurityFilterChain` con `oauth2Login()` |\\n| API REST | `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server` | Nadie, cada solicitud lleva un token bearer | JWKS (y discovery si usa `issuer-uri`) | `SecurityFilterChain` con `oauth2ResourceServer()` |\\n| Puerta de enlace + microservicios | `spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux` + `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client` | La puerta de enlace (sesi\xf3n WebFlux) | Igual que la aplicaci\xf3n web; los servicios detr\xe1s usan JWKS | `SecurityWebFilterChain` con `oauth2Login()`, `TokenRelay` en rutas |\\n\\nSuposiciones de versi\xf3n: Spring Boot 3.5 con Spring Security 6.5, Java 17 y Spring Cloud 2025.0 (Spring Cloud Gateway 4.3). En Spring Boot 4, los dos starters de Security se llaman `spring-boot-starter-security-oauth2-client` y `spring-boot-starter-security-oauth2-resource-server`. Gateway 4.3 y 5.0 utilizan ambos el prefijo de propiedad `spring.cloud.gateway.server.webflux.*` utilizado a continuaci\xf3n; el prefijo m\xe1s antiguo `spring.cloud.gateway.*` y el artefacto `spring-cloud-starter-gateway` est\xe1n obsoletos desde Spring Cloud 2025.0.\\n\\n## Qu\xe9 ofrece Casdoor a Spring {#what-casdoor-gives-spring}\\n\\nEn la consola de administraci\xf3n de Casdoor, cree una aplicaci\xf3n (o reutilice una) y copie su **Client ID** y **Client secret**. Agregue todas las devoluciones de llamada que usar\xe1n las aplicaciones Spring a **Redirect URLs**. El valor predeterminado de Spring Security es `{baseUrl}/login/oauth2/code/{registrationId}`, por lo que con el ID de registro `casdoor` y una aplicaci\xf3n en el puerto 8080, la URL es `http://localhost:8080/login/oauth2/code/casdoor`. Si utiliza cierre de sesi\xf3n OIDC, agregue tambi\xe9n la p\xe1gina donde aterrizar\xe1n los usuarios despu\xe9s, por ejemplo `http://localhost:8080/`; Casdoor rechaza un `post_logout_redirect_uri` que no est\xe9 en la lista.\\n\\nCasdoor publica un documento de descubrimiento est\xe1ndar en `/.well-known/openid-configuration`, por lo que Spring solo necesita el emisor. Estos son los endpoints que anuncia, tomados del c\xf3digo fuente de Casdoor:\\n\\n| Campo de metadatos | Ruta de Casdoor |\\n|---|---|\\n| `issuer` | El origen del backend, p. ej. `https://door.example.com` (sin barra final) |\\n| `authorization_endpoint` | `/login/oauth/authorize` |\\n| `token_endpoint` | `/api/login/oauth/access_token` |\\n| `userinfo_endpoint` | `/api/userinfo` |\\n| `jwks_uri` | `/.well-known/jwks` |\\n| `end_session_endpoint` | `/api/logout` |\\n\\nLos \xe1mbitos admitidos son `openid`, `email`, `profile`, `address`, `phone` y `offline_access`, adem\xe1s de cualquier \xe1mbito personalizado definido en la aplicaci\xf3n. Los tokens se firman con RS256 de forma predeterminada y llevan el nombre del certificado como `kid`. Cada aplicaci\xf3n tambi\xe9n tiene su propio documento de descubrimiento en `/.well-known/<application>/openid-configuration`; consulte [Conectar un cliente OIDC est\xe1ndar](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client) para obtener detalles y la lista completa de [endpoints OAuth 2.0](/docs/how-to-connect/oauth).\\n\\nDos cosas en los tokens de Casdoor importan para Spring:\\n\\n- **El emisor debe coincidir exactamente.** Spring compara el `issuer` en el documento de descubrimiento con el `issuer-uri` que configure, y luego con la reclamaci\xf3n `iss` de cada token. Casdoor deriva el emisor de la configuraci\xf3n `origin` en `app.conf`, o del encabezado Host cuando `origin` est\xe1 vac\xedo. Si sus aplicaciones Spring llegan a Casdoor a trav\xe9s de un nombre de host interno (digamos `http://casdoor:8000` dentro de Docker) mientras que los navegadores usan uno p\xfablico, establezca `origin` en la URL p\xfablica para que todos los tokens reciban el mismo `iss`. Consulte [Configuraci\xf3n](/docs/basic/configuration).\\n- **Los roles vienen en dos formas.** `/api/userinfo` devuelve `roles` como una lista de nombres de roles cuando se otorg\xf3 el \xe1mbito `profile`. El token de acceso en el formato de token `JWT` [predeterminado](/docs/token/overview) contiene `roles` como una lista de objetos de rol, cada uno con un campo `name`. El c\xf3digo siguiente maneja ambos. Los tokens de acceso de Casdoor tambi\xe9n llevan `scope` como una cadena separada por espacios, que Spring convierte en autoridades `SCOPE_...` de forma predeterminada.\\n\\nSi desea informaci\xf3n general sobre el protocolo, consulte [\xbfQu\xe9 es OpenID Connect?](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/what-is-openid-connect) y [Token de ID vs. token de acceso](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/id-token-vs-access-token).\\n\\n## 1. Aplicaci\xf3n web Spring Boot con oauth2Login() {#1-spring-boot-web-app-with-oauth2login}\\n\\nAgregue `spring-boot-starter-web`, `spring-boot-starter-security` y `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client`. Luego configure el proveedor mediante el emisor:\\n\\n```yaml title=\\"application.yml\\"\\nserver:\\n  port: 8080\\n\\nspring:\\n  security:\\n    oauth2:\\n      client:\\n        registration:\\n          casdoor:\\n            client-id: <client-id>\\n            client-secret: <client-secret>\\n            authorization-grant-type: authorization_code\\n            scope: openid,profile,email\\n        provider:\\n          casdoor:\\n            issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n            user-name-attribute: preferred_username\\n```\\n\\nAl iniciarse, Spring Boot obtiene `https://door.example.com/.well-known/openid-configuration` y completa las URI de autorizaci\xf3n, token, userinfo y JWKS. Luego, Spring valida la firma del token de ID, el emisor, la audiencia y el nonce, llama a `/api/userinfo` porque `profile` y `email` est\xe1n en el \xe1mbito (scope), y fusiona las reclamaciones (claims). `preferred_username` es el nombre de usuario de Casdoor; `name` en userinfo es el nombre para mostrar, y el valor predeterminado de `sub` es el UUID del usuario.\\n\\nSin su propia configuraci\xf3n, Spring Boot requiere inicio de sesi\xf3n para cada URL. El siguiente `SecurityFilterChain` personalizado abre la p\xe1gina de inicio, asigna los roles de Casdoor a las autoridades `ROLE_` y cierra la sesi\xf3n tanto en Casdoor como en la aplicaci\xf3n:\\n\\n```java title=\\"SecurityConfig.java\\"\\n@Configuration\\n@EnableWebSecurity\\npublic class SecurityConfig {\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http,\\n            ClientRegistrationRepository clientRegistrations) throws Exception {\\n        OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler oidcLogout =\\n                new OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler(clientRegistrations);\\n        oidcLogout.setPostLogoutRedirectUri(\\"{baseUrl}/\\");\\n\\n        http\\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/\\", \\"/public/**\\", \\"/error\\").permitAll()\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/admin/**\\").hasRole(\\"admin\\")\\n                .anyRequest().authenticated())\\n            .oauth2Login(login -> login\\n                .userInfoEndpoint(userInfo -> userInfo\\n                    .userAuthoritiesMapper(casdoorRoles())))\\n            .logout(logout -> logout\\n                .logoutSuccessHandler(oidcLogout));\\n        return http.build();\\n    }\\n\\n    private GrantedAuthoritiesMapper casdoorRoles() {\\n        return authorities -> {\\n            Set<GrantedAuthority> mapped = new HashSet<>(authorities);\\n            for (GrantedAuthority authority : authorities) {\\n                if (authority instanceof OidcUserAuthority oidc && oidc.getUserInfo() != null) {\\n                    List<String> roles = oidc.getUserInfo().getClaimAsStringList(\\"roles\\");\\n                    if (roles != null) {\\n                        roles.forEach(role -> mapped.add(new SimpleGrantedAuthority(\\"ROLE_\\" + role)));\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n            return mapped;\\n        };\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\n`hasRole(\\"admin\\")` ahora coincide con los usuarios que tienen el rol `admin` de Casdoor. `OidcClientInitiatedLogoutSuccessHandler` lee `end_session_endpoint` del documento de descubrimiento y redirige al `/api/logout` de Casdoor con `id_token_hint` y `post_logout_redirect_uri`, por lo que tambi\xe9n se termina la sesi\xf3n de Casdoor. Sin esto, `/logout` solo limpia la sesi\xf3n de Spring y el siguiente inicio de sesi\xf3n ser\xe1 silencioso.\\n\\nEn un controlador, el usuario conectado es un `OidcUser`:\\n\\n```java\\n@GetMapping(\\"/me\\")\\nMap<String, Object> me(@AuthenticationPrincipal OidcUser user) {\\n    return Map.of(\\"name\\", user.getName(), \\"email\\", user.getEmail(),\\n            \\"authorities\\", user.getAuthorities().toString());\\n}\\n```\\n\\nLa p\xe1gina anterior paso a paso para esta configuraci\xf3n est\xe1 disponible en [Spring Security OAuth](/docs/integration/java/spring-security/spring-security-oauth).\\n\\n## 2. API de Spring Boot como servidor de recursos OAuth2 {#2-spring-boot-api-as-an-oauth2-resource-server}\\n\\nUna API REST no debe redirigir a nadie. Acepta `Authorization: Bearer <access token>`, verifica la firma JWT contra el JWKS de Casdoor y revisa las reclamaciones. Agregue `spring-boot-starter-web`, `spring-boot-starter-security` y `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server`:\\n\\n```yaml title=\\"application.yml\\"\\nserver:\\n  port: 9000\\n\\nspring:\\n  security:\\n    oauth2:\\n      resourceserver:\\n        jwt:\\n          issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n          jwk-set-uri: https://door.example.com/.well-known/jwks\\n          audiences: <client-id>\\n```\\n\\nCon solo este archivo, Spring Boot crea un `JwtDecoder` que verifica la firma, la expiraci\xf3n, `iss` y `aud`. Establecer `jwk-set-uri` junto con `issuer-uri` significa que las claves se obtienen directamente en lugar de a trav\xe9s del descubrimiento, y el emisor sigue siendo validado. La audiencia es el ID de cliente de la aplicaci\xf3n de Casdoor que emiti\xf3 el token: si una aplicaci\xf3n de una sola p\xe1gina o la puerta de enlace de la secci\xf3n 3 obtienen el token, utilice el ID de cliente de esa aplicaci\xf3n.\\n\\nHay una comprobaci\xf3n espec\xedfica de Casdoor que vale la pena agregar. Casdoor firma los tokens de ID y los tokens de acceso con la misma clave y otorga a ambos el ID de cliente como audiencia, por lo que los validadores predeterminados tambi\xe9n aceptar\xedan un token de ID enviado como token bearer. Cada token de Casdoor tiene una reclamaci\xf3n `tokenType` (`access-token`, `id-token` o `refresh-token`), lo que hace que la comprobaci\xf3n sea simple. Definir un bean `JwtDecoder` reemplaza el de Boot, por lo que el bean vuelve a agregar las comprobaciones de emisor y audiencia:\\n\\n```java title=\\"ApiSecurityConfig.java\\"\\n@Configuration\\n@EnableWebSecurity\\npublic class ApiSecurityConfig {\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    JwtDecoder jwtDecoder(\\n            @Value(\\"${spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.issuer-uri}\\") String issuer,\\n            @Value(\\"${spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.jwk-set-uri}\\") String jwkSetUri,\\n            @Value(\\"${spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.audiences}\\") String clientId) {\\n        NimbusJwtDecoder decoder = NimbusJwtDecoder.withJwkSetUri(jwkSetUri).build();\\n        decoder.setJwtValidator(new DelegatingOAuth2TokenValidator<>(\\n                JwtValidators.createDefaultWithIssuer(issuer),\\n                new JwtClaimValidator<List<String>>(JwtClaimNames.AUD,\\n                        aud -> aud != null && aud.contains(clientId)),\\n                new JwtClaimValidator<String>(\\"tokenType\\", \\"access-token\\"::equals)));\\n        return decoder;\\n    }\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    SecurityFilterChain apiFilterChain(HttpSecurity http, JwtDecoder jwtDecoder) throws Exception {\\n        http\\n            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/actuator/health\\").permitAll()\\n                .requestMatchers(\\"/api/admin/**\\").hasRole(\\"admin\\")\\n                .anyRequest().authenticated())\\n            .oauth2ResourceServer(server -> server\\n                .jwt(jwt -> jwt\\n                    .decoder(jwtDecoder)\\n                    .jwtAuthenticationConverter(casdoorJwtConverter())))\\n            .sessionManagement(session -> session\\n                .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))\\n            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable());\\n        return http.build();\\n    }\\n\\n    private JwtAuthenticationConverter casdoorJwtConverter() {\\n        JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter scopes = new JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter();\\n        JwtAuthenticationConverter converter = new JwtAuthenticationConverter();\\n        converter.setPrincipalClaimName(\\"name\\");\\n        converter.setJwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter(jwt -> {\\n            Collection<GrantedAuthority> authorities = new ArrayList<>(scopes.convert(jwt));\\n            if (jwt.getClaims().get(\\"roles\\") instanceof Collection<?> roles) {\\n                for (Object role : roles) {\\n                    Object name = role instanceof Map<?, ?> map ? map.get(\\"name\\") : role;\\n                    if (name instanceof String roleName && !roleName.isEmpty()) {\\n                        authorities.add(new SimpleGrantedAuthority(\\"ROLE_\\" + roleName));\\n                    }\\n                }\\n            }\\n            return authorities;\\n        });\\n        return converter;\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nEl conversor mantiene las autoridades `SCOPE_`, agrega autoridades `ROLE_` desde la reclamaci\xf3n `roles`, ya sea que contenga nombres u objetos, y utiliza la reclamaci\xf3n `name` (el nombre de usuario de Casdoor) como principal. Si la aplicaci\xf3n utiliza el formato de token `JWT-Standard`, use `preferred_username` como reclamaci\xf3n principal en su lugar. Deshabilitar CSRF y sesiones es correcto aqu\xed porque la API nunca ve una cookie del navegador; no copie esas dos l\xedneas en la aplicaci\xf3n web.\\n\\nPara probarlo sin un navegador, obtenga un token de m\xe1quina con la concesi\xf3n de credenciales de cliente y llame a la API:\\n\\n```shell\\nTOKEN=$(curl -s -u \'<client-id>:<client-secret>\' \\\\\\n  -d grant_type=client_credentials \\\\\\n  https://door.example.com/api/login/oauth/access_token | jq -r .access_token)\\n\\ncurl -H \\"Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN\\" http://localhost:9000/api/hello\\n```\\n\\nUn token de credenciales de cliente representa a la aplicaci\xf3n, no a un usuario, por lo que no tiene roles. Para m\xe1s detalles sobre qu\xe9 reclamaciones verificar y por qu\xe9, consulte [Validaci\xf3n JWT](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/jwt-validation) y [\xc1mbitos OAuth](https://www.casdoor.com/blog/oauth-scopes).\\n\\n## 3. Spring Cloud Gateway con TokenRelay {#3-spring-cloud-gateway-with-tokenrelay}\\n\\nEn microservicios, un dise\xf1o com\xfan es permitir que la puerta de enlace gestione el inicio de sesi\xf3n y mantener los servicios como servidores de recursos. El navegador solo habla con la puerta de enlace y mantiene una cookie de sesi\xf3n; la puerta de enlace almacena el token de acceso del usuario y el filtro `TokenRelay` lo agrega como `Authorization: Bearer ...` a cada solicitud proxy. Los servicios de la secci\xf3n 2 lo validan normalmente.\\n\\nAgregue `spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux` y `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-client`, con el BOM de Spring Cloud 2025.0:\\n\\n```yaml title=\\"application.yml\\"\\nserver:\\n  port: 8080\\n\\nspring:\\n  application:\\n    name: gateway\\n  security:\\n    oauth2:\\n      client:\\n        registration:\\n          casdoor:\\n            client-id: <client-id>\\n            client-secret: <client-secret>\\n            authorization-grant-type: authorization_code\\n            scope: openid,profile,email\\n        provider:\\n          casdoor:\\n            issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n            user-name-attribute: preferred_username\\n  cloud:\\n    gateway:\\n      server:\\n        webflux:\\n          routes:\\n            - id: orders\\n              uri: http://localhost:9000\\n              predicates:\\n                - Path=/api/orders/**\\n              filters:\\n                - TokenRelay=\\n            - id: users\\n              uri: http://localhost:9001\\n              predicates:\\n                - Path=/api/users/**\\n              filters:\\n                - TokenRelay=\\n```\\n\\n`TokenRelay=` sin argumento retransmite el token del usuario conectado. La f\xe1brica de filtros `TokenRelay` solo se crea cuando se configura `spring.security.oauth2.client.*`, y el almac\xe9n predeterminado de clientes autorizados est\xe1 en memoria, por lo que una puerta de enlace con varias r\xe9plicas necesita un almac\xe9n compartido o sesiones adherentes (sticky sessions). Los servicios detr\xe1s de ella establecen `audiences` en el ID de cliente de la puerta de enlace.\\n\\nSpring Cloud Gateway se ejecuta en WebFlux, por lo que su configuraci\xf3n de seguridad es una `SecurityWebFilterChain` construida desde `ServerHttpSecurity`, no la `SecurityFilterChain` de servlets:\\n\\n```java title=\\"GatewaySecurityConfig.java\\"\\n@Configuration\\n@EnableWebFluxSecurity\\npublic class GatewaySecurityConfig {\\n\\n    @Bean\\n    SecurityWebFilterChain gatewayFilterChain(ServerHttpSecurity http,\\n            ReactiveClientRegistrationRepository clientRegistrations) {\\n        OidcClientInitiatedServerLogoutSuccessHandler oidcLogout =\\n                new OidcClientInitiatedServerLogoutSuccessHandler(clientRegistrations);\\n        oidcLogout.setPostLogoutRedirectUri(\\"{baseUrl}/\\");\\n\\n        http\\n            .authorizeExchange(exchanges -> exchanges\\n                .pathMatchers(\\"/\\", \\"/actuator/health\\").permitAll()\\n                .anyExchange().authenticated())\\n            .oauth2Login(Customizer.withDefaults())\\n            .logout(logout -> logout.logoutSuccessHandler(oidcLogout));\\n        return http.build();\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nMantenga las decisiones de autorizaci\xf3n en los servicios, donde los roles est\xe1n en el token; la puerta de enlace solo asegura que haya un usuario conectado. La protecci\xf3n CSRF permanece activada en la puerta de enlace porque es basada en cookies.\\n\\nLas p\xe1ginas existentes de [Spring Cloud Gateway](/docs/integration/java/spring-cloud-gateway) y [Spring Cloud](/docs/integration/java/spring-cloud) muestran un dise\xf1o diferente, donde un `GlobalFilter` personalizado verifica un usuario de Casdoor almacenado en la sesi\xf3n de la puerta de enlace.\\n\\n## Alternativa: el starter de Casdoor para Spring Boot {#alternative-the-casdoor-spring-boot-starter}\\n\\nSi prefiere no escribir YAML, [casdoor-spring-boot-starter](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-spring-boot-starter) 2.x (Spring Boot 3 y 4, Java 17+) acepta `casdoor.endpoint`, `casdoor.client-id`, `casdoor.client-secret`, `casdoor.organization-name` y `casdoor.application-name`, y establece las propiedades `spring.security.oauth2.*` anteriores seg\xfan cu\xe1l starter de Security est\xe9 en la ruta de clase. Registra el cliente como `casdoor`, mapea los roles de Casdoor a autoridades `ROLE_` tanto para el inicio de sesi\xf3n OAuth2 como para el servidor de recursos de servlets, y expone los servicios del [SDK de Java de Casdoor](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-java-sdk) como beans. Sus propiedades tienen la menor precedencia, por lo que a\xfan puede sobrescribir cualquiera de ellas. Consulte [Spring Boot](/docs/integration/java/spring-boot) para la configuraci\xf3n.\\n\\n## Errores comunes {#common-errors}\\n\\n- **\\"The Issuer ... provided in the configuration metadata did not match the requested issuer\\"**: el `issuer-uri` difiere de lo que informa Casdoor. Elimine cualquier barra final y establezca `origin` en Casdoor si los nombres de host difieren.\\n- **`redirect_uri` rechazado por Casdoor**: la URL de devoluci\xf3n de llamada no est\xe1 en las **Redirect URLs** de la aplicaci\xf3n. Verifique el esquema y el puerto, especialmente detr\xe1s de un proxy inverso.\\n- **\\"Missing attribute \'preferred_username\'\\"**: no se solicit\xf3 el \xe1mbito `profile`, o los campos de token de la aplicaci\xf3n omiten `Name`.\\n- **Error 401 con `invalid_token` en la API**: el token proviene de otra aplicaci\xf3n (`aud` incorrecto), ha expirado o es un ID token.\\n\\n## Referencias {#references}\\n\\n- Documento de descubrimiento y endpoints de Casdoor: [`object/wellknown_oidc_discovery.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/wellknown_oidc_discovery.go)\\n- Claims del token de Casdoor: [`object/token_jwt.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/token_jwt.go)\\n- [Referencia de Spring Boot 3.5: Spring Security, cliente OAuth2 y servidor de recursos](https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/3.5/reference/web/spring-security.html)\\n- [Spring Security: cierre de sesi\xf3n iniciado por el cliente OIDC](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/oauth2/login/logout.html)\\n- [Spring Security: JWT para servidor de recursos OAuth 2.0](https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/oauth2/resource-server/jwt.html)\\n- [Spring Cloud Gateway: f\xe1brica de filtros TokenRelay GatewayFilter](https://docs.spring.io/spring-cloud-gateway/reference/spring-cloud-gateway-server-webflux/gatewayfilter-factories/tokenrelay-factory.html)\\n- [Spring Cloud Gateway: starter `spring-cloud-starter-gateway-server-webflux`](https://docs.spring.io/spring-cloud-gateway/reference/spring-cloud-gateway-server-webflux/starter.html)\\n- [Notas de la versi\xf3n de Spring Cloud 2025.0](https://github.com/spring-cloud/spring-cloud-release/wiki/Spring-Cloud-2025.0-Release-Notes)\\n- [casdoor-spring-boot-starter](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-spring-boot-starter)"},{"id":"sso-for-apache-data-platforms","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/sso-for-apache-data-platforms","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-sso-for-apache-data-platforms.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-sso-for-apache-data-platforms.mdx","title":"DolphinScheduler, IoTDB and Other Apache Data Platforms: SSO with Casdoor","description":"Which Apache data projects support Casdoor SSO, since which version and with which config keys: DolphinScheduler, IoTDB, ShenYu, Pulsar Manager, ElasticJob UI.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"DolphinScheduler","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/dolphin-scheduler"},{"inline":true,"label":"IoTDB","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/io-tdb"},{"inline":true,"label":"ShenYu","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/shen-yu"},{"inline":true,"label":"Pulsar","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/pulsar"},{"inline":true,"label":"ShardingSphere","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/sharding-sphere"},{"inline":true,"label":"RuoYi","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/ruo-yi"}],"readingTime":8.91,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"sso-for-apache-data-platforms","title":"DolphinScheduler, IoTDB and Other Apache Data Platforms: SSO with Casdoor","description":"Which Apache data projects support Casdoor SSO, since which version and with which config keys: DolphinScheduler, IoTDB, ShenYu, Pulsar Manager, ElasticJob UI.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","OIDC","DolphinScheduler","IoTDB","ShenYu","Pulsar","ShardingSphere","RuoYi"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"SSO de Spring Boot con OpenID Connect: Spring Security, Resource Server y Spring Cloud Gateway con Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc"},"nextItem":{"title":"Steam OAuth and Telegram OAuth with Casdoor: Adding Non-Standard Social Logins Through OIDC","permalink":"/es/blog/steam-telegram-login"}},"content":"Several Apache data and middleware projects ship a Casdoor login of their own: Apache DolphinScheduler has an authentication type called `CASDOOR_SSO` (since 3.2.0) and a generic OIDC mode (since 3.4.0), Apache ShenYu has a Casdoor plugin (since 2.5.1), Apache Pulsar Manager has a \\"Log in with Casdoor\\" button (since 0.3.0), and Apache APISIX has the `authz-casdoor` plugin (since 2.14.0). Apache IoTDB is the exception: the database itself authenticates with its own users and passwords, and only the now archived IoTDB Web Workbench talks to Casdoor. The table below lists every project, the version, and where the settings live; the second half of the post sets up user authentication in IoTDB step by step.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Which Apache projects support Casdoor\\n\\nAll facts in this table come from each project\'s source tree and release tags, checked on 2026-11-02.\\n\\n| Project | How it connects to Casdoor | First release | Settings |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| Apache DolphinScheduler | Built-in `CASDOOR_SSO` authentication type (Casdoor Java SDK, OAuth 2.0 code flow) | 3.2.0 | `security.authentication.type`, `casdoor.*` in `dolphinscheduler-api/src/main/resources/application.yaml` |\\n| Apache DolphinScheduler | Generic OIDC login (Spring Security) | 3.4.0 | `security.authentication.oidc.*` in the same file |\\n| Apache IoTDB Web Workbench | Casdoor login in the web console | no tagged release, repository archived | `casdoor.*` in `backend/src/main/resources/application.properties`, `VUE_APP_CASDOOR` in `frontend/.env` |\\n| Apache IoTDB (server) | Native users and roles; an `OpenIdAuthorizer` existed up to 2.0.8 and was removed | n/a | see the IoTDB section below |\\n| Apache ShenYu | Casdoor plugin on the gateway | 2.5.1 | plugin config `endpoint`, `client_id`, `client_secrect`, `certificate`, `organization-name`, `application-name` |\\n| Apache Pulsar Manager | \\"Log in with Casdoor\\" button next to the password form | 0.3.0 | `casdoor.*` in `src/main/resources/application.properties`; front-end config in `front-end/src/main.js` since 0.4.0 |\\n| Apache ShardingSphere ElasticJob UI | \\"Login with Casdoor\\" button | master only, after 3.0.2 | `casdoor.*` in the backend `application.properties` |\\n| Apache APISIX | `authz-casdoor` plugin, or `openid-connect` | 2.14.0 | `endpoint_addr`, `client_id`, `client_secret`, `callback_url` |\\n| RuoYi-Cloud (not an Apache project) | Code changes with the Casdoor Spring Boot SDK | n/a | your own login controller |\\n\\nEvery SDK-based integration in the table follows the same pattern. You create an application in Casdoor, copy its client ID, client secret and the public certificate of its token cert, and paste them into the project\'s config together with the organization and application names. The project redirects the browser to Casdoor, receives an authorization code, exchanges it for a JWT, and verifies the JWT with the certificate. The certificate is the one under **Certs** in the Casdoor admin console; see [certificates](/docs/cert/overview) and the **Token cert** field in the [application settings reference](/docs/application/terminology).\\n\\n## Apache DolphinScheduler\\n\\nDolphinScheduler gives you two ways in.\\n\\n**`CASDOOR_SSO`** has been there since 3.2.0. Set the type and fill the top-level `casdoor` block of `dolphinscheduler-api/src/main/resources/application.yaml`:\\n\\n```yaml\\nsecurity:\\n  authentication:\\n    type: CASDOOR_SSO\\n    casdoor:\\n      user:\\n        admin: \\"alice\\"   # this Casdoor user becomes a DolphinScheduler admin\\ncasdoor:\\n  endpoint: https://door.example.com\\n  client-id: <client id>\\n  client-secret: <client secret>\\n  certificate: |-\\n    -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\\n    ...\\n    -----END CERTIFICATE-----\\n  organization-name: my-org\\n  application-name: app-dolphinscheduler\\n  redirect-url: http://localhost:5173/login\\n```\\n\\n`redirect-url` is the DolphinScheduler UI login page, and it must also be in the **Redirect URLs** of the Casdoor application. On the first login, DolphinScheduler creates a local user with the Casdoor user name and email. The user named in `security.authentication.casdoor.user.admin` gets the admin type; everyone else becomes a general user. Our [DolphinScheduler guide](/docs/integration/java/dolphinscheduler) has the screenshots.\\n\\n**OIDC** arrived in 3.4.0. It is generic, so Casdoor works as an ordinary issuer. Spring Security\'s discovery requires the configured issuer to match the `issuer` in the discovery document, and Casdoor\'s issuer is the origin of its backend, so `issuer-uri` is just the Casdoor URL:\\n\\n```yaml\\napi:\\n  base-url: https://ds.example.com/dolphinscheduler\\n  ui-url: https://ds.example.com\\nsecurity:\\n  authentication:\\n    type: OIDC\\n    oidc:\\n      enable: true\\n      providers:\\n        casdoor:\\n          display-name: \\"Login with Casdoor\\"\\n          issuer-uri: https://door.example.com\\n          client-id: <client id>\\n          client-secret: <client secret>\\n          scope: openid, profile, email\\n          user-name-attribute: preferred_username\\n          groups-claim: groups\\n      user:\\n        auto-create: true\\n        default-tenant-code: \\"default\\"\\n        default-queue: \\"default\\"\\n        admin-group-mapping:\\n          - my-org/ds-admins\\n```\\n\\nThe redirect URL to register in Casdoor is `https://ds.example.com/dolphinscheduler/login/oauth2/code/casdoor`, that is, `api.base-url` plus `/login/oauth2/code/` plus the provider key. With the `profile` scope, Casdoor\'s UserInfo endpoint returns `preferred_username` and `groups`. By default groups arrive as `my-org/ds-admins`. Set **Token group format** to `Name (group)` in the application if you would rather write `ds-admins` in `admin-group-mapping`. If `auto-create` is `false` (the upstream default), only users that already exist in DolphinScheduler can sign in. The [standard OIDC client guide](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client) lists what the discovery document and UserInfo return.\\n\\n## Apache ShenYu\\n\\nThe Casdoor plugin has shipped with the ShenYu gateway since 2.5.1. You turn it on in the ShenYu admin console and fill in the plugin data. Two details come from the source rather than the docs. First, the secret key is spelled `client_secrect`. Second, the certificate field is a single line, so you write `\\\\n` where each line break goes. The plugin accepts a JWT from the `Authorization` header, or a `code` and `state` on the query string. It then passes the user downstream in the headers `name`, `id` and `organization`. Requests that carry neither are rejected with a token error, not redirected. On ShenYu\'s master branch (October 2026) the plugin moved out of the main repository into the ShenYu plugin store. The plugin name, fields and headers stayed the same, and you add the starter `shenyu-spring-boot-starter-plugin-casdoor` yourself. See [Using Casdoor in ShenYu](/docs/integration/java/shenyu).\\n\\n## Apache Pulsar Manager and ShardingSphere ElasticJob UI\\n\\nPulsar Manager added Casdoor in 0.3.0. The back end reads `casdoor.endpoint`, `casdoor.clientId`, `casdoor.clientSecret`, `casdoor.certificate`, `casdoor.organizationName` and `casdoor.applicationName` from `src/main/resources/application.properties`. Since 0.4.0 the front end uses `casdoor-vue-sdk`, configured in `front-end/src/main.js` with `serverUrl`, `clientId`, `organizationName`, `appName` and `redirectPath`. A first-time Casdoor user gets a local Pulsar Manager account. See [Pulsar Manager](/docs/integration/java/Pulsar-manager).\\n\\nElasticJob UI, the console of Apache ShardingSphere ElasticJob, merged Casdoor login in November 2022. That was after its last release, 3.0.2, so you need to build master. The keys use dashes: `casdoor.endpoint`, `casdoor.client-id`, `casdoor.client-secret`, `casdoor.certificate`, `casdoor.organization-name`, `casdoor.application-name`. See [ShardingSphere](/docs/integration/java/ShardingSphere).\\n\\nFor Apache APISIX, both plugins are covered in [APISIX](/docs/integration/lua/apisix).\\n\\n## Step by step user authentication setup in Apache IoTDB\\n\\nIoTDB authenticates every session (CLI, JDBC, Session API) with a user name and password stored by the database. Since there is no Casdoor login on the server side, the setup is IoTDB\'s native user model. The statements below are for the tree model. The table model of IoTDB 2.x has its own syntax in the [table model guide](https://iotdb.apache.org/UserGuide/latest-Table/User-Manual/Authority-Management_apache.html).\\n\\n**Step 1. Change the root password.** `root` is the only administrator and cannot be deleted. Log in with the CLI and set a strong password:\\n\\n```sql\\nALTER USER root SET PASSWORD \'Str0ng_Passw0rd!\';\\n```\\n\\n**Step 2. Create users.** User names and passwords are 4 to 32 characters long. They can contain letters, digits and `!@#$%^&*()_+-=`, and from V2.0.7 a password cannot equal the user name. TimechoDB, Timecho\'s commercial IoTDB distribution, requires 12 to 32 characters with upper and lower case, a digit and a symbol, and it has a different default root password. Change it the same way.\\n\\n```sql\\nCREATE USER ln_reader \'Read_Passw0rd!\';\\nCREATE USER ops_admin \'Ops_Passw0rd!\';\\n```\\n\\n**Step 3. Group privileges in roles.** New users have no privileges. Series privileges (`READ_DATA`, `WRITE_DATA`, `READ_SCHEMA`, `WRITE_SCHEMA`, or the aliases `READ`, `WRITE`, `ALL`) are granted on path patterns:\\n\\n```sql\\nCREATE ROLE ln_readers;\\nGRANT READ ON root.ln.** TO ROLE ln_readers;\\nGRANT ROLE ln_readers TO ln_reader;\\n```\\n\\n**Step 4. Delegate user management.** This is where versions differ. From V2.0.7, global privileges are `SYSTEM` and `SECURITY`. Before that, they were split into `MANAGE_USER`, `MANAGE_ROLE`, `MANAGE_DATABASE` and others. Global privileges are always granted on `root.**`:\\n\\n```sql\\n-- IoTDB V2.0.7 and later, TimechoDB\\nGRANT SECURITY ON root.** TO USER ops_admin;\\n-- before V2.0.7\\nGRANT MANAGE_USER, MANAGE_ROLE ON root.** TO USER ops_admin;\\n```\\n\\n**Step 5. Connect and verify.**\\n\\n```bash\\nsbin/start-cli.sh -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6667 -u ln_reader -pw \'Read_Passw0rd!\'\\n```\\n\\nThen run `LIST PRIVILEGES OF USER ln_reader;`. A user\'s effective privileges are the union of their own and those of their roles. To take a privilege away, revoke it from both the user and every role that grants it.\\n\\n### What about OIDC in IoTDB?\\n\\nOlder IoTDB versions had `authorizer_provider_class=org.apache.iotdb.commons.auth.authorizer.OpenIdAuthorizer` with `openID_url`. The client passed an access token as the user name with an empty password. That authorizer was built around Keycloak. It treats a user as an administrator only if the token\'s `realm_access.roles` contains `iotdb_admin`, and every privilege check reduces to that test. Its token parser also sets a near-infinite clock skew, which in practice ignores token expiry. Casdoor does not emit `realm_access`, so this was never a fit. IoTDB removed it in March 2026 ([apache/iotdb#17320](https://github.com/apache/iotdb/pull/17320)), and it is absent from 2.0.10 and 2.0.11.\\n\\nIf you want Casdoor accounts in front of IoTDB, the option that exists is the [IoTDB Web Workbench](/docs/integration/java/iotdb) integration. Its repository is archived on GitHub, so treat it as a starting point rather than a maintained product. The database connection itself still uses an IoTDB user from the steps above.\\n\\n## RuoYi\\n\\nRuoYi-Cloud has no Casdoor switch. Our [RuoYi guide](/docs/integration/java/RuoYi) replaces its password login with the Casdoor authorization code flow through the Casdoor Spring Boot SDK. It syncs the existing RuoYi users into Casdoor with a syncer, using bcrypt so their passwords keep working. For a plain Spring Boot app, start with [Spring Boot SSO with OIDC](/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc).\\n\\n## Referencias {#references}\\n\\n- Apache DolphinScheduler, [authentication types](https://github.com/apache/dolphinscheduler/blob/dev/docs/docs/en/guide/security/authentication-type.md) and [`application.yaml`](https://github.com/apache/dolphinscheduler/blob/dev/dolphinscheduler-api/src/main/resources/application.yaml)\\n- Apache IoTDB, [Authority Management (from V2.0.7)](https://iotdb.apache.org/UserGuide/latest/User-Manual/Authority-Management-Upgrade_apache.html), [Authority Management (before V2.0.7)](https://iotdb.apache.org/UserGuide/latest/User-Manual/Authority-Management_apache.html), [CLI](https://iotdb.apache.org/UserGuide/latest/Tools-System/CLI_apache.html)\\n- Timecho, [TimechoDB Authority Management](https://www.timecho.com/docs/UserGuide/latest/User-Manual/Authority-Management-Upgrade_timecho.html)\\n- [apache/iotdb-web-workbench](https://github.com/apache/iotdb-web-workbench)\\n- Apache ShenYu, [Casdoor plugin](https://shenyu.apache.org/docs/plugin-center/security/casdoor/) and [plugin store cutover](https://github.com/apache/shenyu/blob/master/docs/plugin-store-casdoor.md)\\n- [apache/pulsar-manager](https://github.com/apache/pulsar-manager) and [apache/shardingsphere-elasticjob-ui](https://github.com/apache/shardingsphere-elasticjob-ui)\\n- Apache APISIX, [authz-casdoor](https://apisix.apache.org/docs/apisix/plugins/authz-casdoor/)"},{"id":"steam-telegram-login","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/steam-telegram-login","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-steam-telegram-login.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-steam-telegram-login.mdx","title":"Steam OAuth and Telegram OAuth with Casdoor: Adding Non-Standard Social Logins Through OIDC","description":"Steam uses OpenID 2.0 and Telegram signs widget data with HMAC. Here is how Casdoor adds Steam, Telegram, X and Facebook login and hands your app plain OIDC.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"OIDC","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/oidc"},{"inline":true,"label":"Steam","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/steam"},{"inline":true,"label":"Telegram","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/telegram"},{"inline":true,"label":"Social Login","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/social-login"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":9.21,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"steam-telegram-login","title":"Steam OAuth and Telegram OAuth with Casdoor: Adding Non-Standard Social Logins Through OIDC","description":"Steam uses OpenID 2.0 and Telegram signs widget data with HMAC. Here is how Casdoor adds Steam, Telegram, X and Facebook login and hands your app plain OIDC.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","OAuth","OIDC","Steam","Telegram","Social Login","SSO"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"DolphinScheduler, IoTDB and Other Apache Data Platforms: SSO with Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/sso-for-apache-data-platforms"},"nextItem":{"title":"Dummy Payment Gateway for Testing: Casdoor\'s Dummy Provider, Stripe Test Mode and PayPal Sandbox","permalink":"/es/blog/test-payments-without-a-gateway"}},"content":"There is no \\"Steam OAuth\\": Steam signs users in with OpenID 2.0 and gives you a 64-bit SteamID, nothing more. Telegram\'s classic Login Widget isn\'t OAuth either: it hands your page a set of user fields plus an HMAC-SHA256 hash that you check with your bot token. Telegram now also runs a real OpenID Connect provider at `oauth.telegram.org`. If you put Casdoor between your app and these providers, Casdoor does the protocol-specific checks and your app only ever speaks OpenID Connect to Casdoor, the same way it would for Google or GitHub.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The providers at a glance\\n\\n| Provider | Protocol | What identifies the user | Email available? | Casdoor provider type |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| Steam | OpenID 2.0 at `https://steamcommunity.com/openid/login`, plus the Steam Web API for the profile | SteamID64, taken from the claimed ID `https://steamcommunity.com/openid/id/<steamid>` | No | `Steam` |\\n| Telegram (Login Widget) | Signed widget data, HMAC-SHA256 keyed with the SHA-256 of the bot token | Telegram user `id` | No | `Telegram` |\\n| Telegram (OpenID Connect) | OIDC authorization code flow with PKCE at `https://oauth.telegram.org` | `sub` claim of the ID token | No email claim; phone with the `phone` scope | `Custom` |\\n| X (Twitter) | OAuth 2.0 authorization code with PKCE | X user ID from `/2/users/me` | Not with Casdoor\'s default scopes | `Twitter` |\\n| Facebook | OAuth 2.0 authorization code | App-scoped user ID | Only if the profile has a valid primary email | `Facebook` |\\n\\nThe rest of this post goes through what each protocol actually returns and what Casdoor does with it.\\n\\n## Steam: OpenID 2.0, no OAuth\\n\\nSteam\'s developer pages describe Steam as an OpenID provider: your site sends the user to Steam, and Steam sends back a claimed ID of the form `https://steamcommunity.com/openid/id/<steamid>`. The number at the end is the user\'s 64-bit SteamID. There is no authorization code, no access token, no scopes, and no email: neither the OpenID response nor the Web API profile contains one.\\n\\nThat is why searches for \\"steam oauth\\" lead nowhere useful: the protocol is OpenID 2.0, the predecessor of OpenID Connect, so an OAuth 2.0 or OIDC client library can\'t talk to Steam directly.\\n\\nWhat Casdoor does for a Steam sign-in:\\n\\n1. The Casdoor sign-in page builds an OpenID 2.0 `checkid_setup` request to `https://steamcommunity.com/openid/login`, with `openid.realm` set to the Casdoor origin and `openid.return_to` set to Casdoor\'s `/callback` page.\\n2. When Steam redirects back, Casdoor posts the signed fields back to Steam with `openid.mode=check_authentication`. Only if Steam answers `is_valid:true` does it continue. It also checks that `openid.claimed_id` matches the `steamcommunity.com/openid/id/<digits>` pattern and extracts the SteamID from it.\\n3. With the Steam Web API key that you enter as the provider\'s **Client secret**, Casdoor calls `ISteamUser/GetPlayerSummaries` to get the persona name and the avatar.\\n\\nThe resulting Casdoor user has the SteamID64 as its username, the Steam persona name as its display name and the Steam avatar. The email field stays empty. Casdoor deliberately drops the placeholder text that the underlying library puts there. If your app needs an email address, collect it after sign-up.\\n\\nSetup takes two minutes: register a Web API key for your Casdoor domain on Steam\'s key page, create a provider of type `Steam` and paste the key as the client secret. The [Steam provider guide](/docs/provider/oauth/Steam) has the steps.\\n\\n## Telegram: the Login Widget and its HMAC check\\n\\nThe classic Telegram login is a JavaScript widget. You create a bot with @BotFather, link your domain to it with `/setdomain`, and embed the widget. When the user confirms in Telegram, the widget either redirects to the URL in `data-auth-url` or calls a JavaScript function, passing `id`, `first_name`, `last_name`, `username`, `photo_url`, `auth_date` and `hash`.\\n\\nNothing in that payload is a token. Anyone can type those fields into a URL, so the server must check the `hash`. Telegram\'s documented check works like this:\\n\\n```text\\ndata_check_string = all received fields except hash,\\n                    as key=value, sorted by key, joined with \\"\\\\n\\"\\nsecret_key        = SHA256(bot_token)\\nvalid             = hex(HMAC_SHA256(data_check_string, secret_key)) == hash\\n```\\n\\nTelegram also tells you to check `auth_date` so that old data can\'t be replayed, but it leaves the window up to you.\\n\\nIn Casdoor, the `Telegram` provider type takes the bot username (without `@`) as the **Client ID** and the bot token as the **Client secret**. When a user clicks the Telegram button, Casdoor opens its own `/telegram-login` page, which loads `telegram-widget.js` from telegram.org with your bot username and points `data-auth-url` back at Casdoor\'s `/callback`. The callback collects the fields and the Casdoor backend then:\\n\\n- rebuilds the data-check string from every field except `hash`, sorts it, and compares the HMAC-SHA256 against `hash` using `SHA256(bot token)` as the key;\\n- rejects the data if `auth_date` is more than 24 hours old;\\n- uses the numeric Telegram `id` as the identity, the `username` as the username, the first and last name as the display name, and `photo_url` as the avatar.\\n\\nTelegram usernames are optional. When a user has none, Casdoor generates `telegram_<id>`, which stays the same at every sign-in. The widget never sends an email address or a phone number, so a Telegram-only user in Casdoor has neither. See the [Telegram provider guide](/docs/provider/oauth/telegram) for the BotFather steps.\\n\\n## Telegram now offers OpenID Connect too\\n\\nTelegram\'s \\"Log In With Telegram\\" page now documents a standard OpenID Connect flow, and the old iframe widget documentation has moved to an archive page. The facts from Telegram\'s docs:\\n\\n- Discovery document: `https://oauth.telegram.org/.well-known/openid-configuration`. Authorization endpoint `https://oauth.telegram.org/auth`, token endpoint `https://oauth.telegram.org/token`, keys at `https://oauth.telegram.org/.well-known/jwks.json`.\\n- In @BotFather you open your bot\'s **Login Widget** settings, register the allowed URLs (origins and exact redirect URIs), and get a client ID and client secret.\\n- Authorization code flow, PKCE with `S256` recommended. The token request uses HTTP Basic authentication with the client ID and secret.\\n- Scopes: `openid` is required; `profile` adds the name, `preferred_username` and `picture`; `phone` adds `phone_number` with the user\'s consent. There is no email claim.\\n- There is no UserInfo endpoint. All user data is in the ID token, whose `iss` is `https://oauth.telegram.org` and whose `aud` is your bot ID.\\n\\nYou can connect this flow with a Casdoor [custom OAuth provider](/docs/provider/oauth/CustomProvider). Set the **Auth URL** and **Token URL** to the endpoints above, set **Scope** to `openid profile` (add `phone` if you need it), turn on **Enable PKCE**, and leave **UserInfo URL** empty. Register Casdoor\'s `https://<your-casdoor-host>/callback` as an allowed URL in BotFather. With no UserInfo URL, Casdoor takes the user from the ID token in the token response: it requires `sub`, rejects an expired token, and checks that `aud` contains the client ID. Without a user mapping, `sub` becomes the user ID, `preferred_username` the username, `name` the display name and `picture` the avatar, and `phone_number` fills the phone. The Casdoor sign-in page recognizes a custom provider whose auth URL points at `oauth.telegram.org` and keeps the OAuth `state` short for it.\\n\\nNote that a Telegram user signed in through the widget and the same person signed in through OIDC are not automatically the same Casdoor user. The widget identity is the numeric `id`, while the OIDC identity is `sub`, and Telegram\'s own examples show different values for the two. Pick one method per application.\\n\\n## X (Twitter) and Facebook\\n\\nThese two are ordinary OAuth 2.0, but each has a detail worth knowing.\\n\\n**X** requires a PKCE challenge on its OAuth 2.0 authorize URL. Casdoor\'s `Twitter` type generates a new code verifier for every sign-in, sends the `S256` challenge, and passes the verifier in the token exchange, so there is nothing to configure beyond the client ID and secret. Casdoor requests the `users.read tweet.read` scopes and reads the user from `/2/users/me`, which gives the user ID, the handle and the display name. It doesn\'t request an email field, so plan for X users without an email address. The [Twitter provider guide](/docs/provider/oauth/Twitter) covers the app settings.\\n\\n**Facebook** returns an app-scoped user ID: the same person has a different ID in each Facebook app, so moving to a new Facebook app means your users won\'t match their old records unless you link them by email. Casdoor asks for `email,public_profile` and reads `id`, `name`, `picture` and `email` from the Graph API. Facebook omits `email` when the profile has no valid address. See the [Facebook provider guide](/docs/provider/oauth/facebook).\\n\\n## How your app sees all of this: plain OIDC\\n\\nYour application never talks to Steam, Telegram, X or Facebook. It is an OIDC client of Casdoor, configured once with the Casdoor issuer, `https://<your-casdoor-host>/.well-known/openid-configuration`. You add the social providers to the Casdoor application on its **Providers** tab, and their buttons appear on the Casdoor sign-in page.\\n\\nAfter any of these sign-ins, Casdoor finds or creates a Casdoor user and finishes the normal authorization code flow with your app. The ID token your app receives is signed by Casdoor, and its `sub` is the Casdoor user ID, not the SteamID or the Telegram ID. So your app needs no OpenID 2.0 library, no HMAC code, no PKCE quirks per provider, and no special case for users without an email. The original provider ID is stored on the Casdoor user, for example in the `steam` or `telegram` field, if you ever need it.\\n\\nA few things to plan for:\\n\\n- **Account linking.** Casdoor links a social sign-in to an existing user by verified email, verified phone, or username, in the order the application\'s binding rule sets. Steam and widget-based Telegram provide neither email nor phone, so they create a separate user unless the user links the provider from an account that is already signed in. See [how Casdoor links accounts](/docs/provider/oauth/overview#automatic-account-linking).\\n- **Extra profile fields.** Providers that pass on raw data, such as the Goth-based Steam type and custom providers, can fill more user fields through [user mapping](/docs/provider/oauth/user-mapping).\\n- **The client side.** Any standard library works against Casdoor. [Connect a standard OIDC client](/docs/how-to-connect/oidc-client) lists the endpoints, and the [Spring Boot SSO with OIDC](/blog/spring-boot-sso-oidc) post shows a complete app.\\n\\n## Referencias {#references}\\n\\n- Steam Web API and OpenID: [steamcommunity.com/dev](https://steamcommunity.com/dev)\\n- Steamworks, User Authentication and Ownership: [partner.steamgames.com/doc/features/auth](https://partner.steamgames.com/doc/features/auth)\\n- Telegram, Log In With Telegram (OpenID Connect): [core.telegram.org/bots/telegram-login](https://core.telegram.org/bots/telegram-login)\\n- Telegram, Login Widget (legacy): [core.telegram.org/widgets/login-legacy](https://core.telegram.org/widgets/login-legacy)\\n- X, OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code Flow with PKCE: [docs.x.com](https://docs.x.com/fundamentals/authentication/oauth-2-0/authorization-code)\\n- Meta, Graph API User reference: [developers.facebook.com/docs/graph-api/reference/user](https://developers.facebook.com/docs/graph-api/reference/user/)\\n- Casdoor source: [`idp/telegram.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/idp/telegram.go), [`idp/goth.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/idp/goth.go), [`idp/custom.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/idp/custom.go)"},{"id":"test-payments-without-a-gateway","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/test-payments-without-a-gateway","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-test-payments-without-a-gateway.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-11-02-test-payments-without-a-gateway.mdx","title":"Dummy Payment Gateway for Testing: Casdoor\'s Dummy Provider, Stripe Test Mode and PayPal Sandbox","description":"A dummy payment gateway completes checkout without moving money. How the Casdoor Dummy provider works, and when to switch to Stripe test mode or PayPal sandbox.","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Payment","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/payment"},{"inline":true,"label":"Testing","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/testing"},{"inline":true,"label":"Stripe","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/stripe"},{"inline":true,"label":"PayPal","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/pay-pal"},{"inline":true,"label":"Subscription","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/subscription"}],"readingTime":8.82,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"test-payments-without-a-gateway","title":"Dummy Payment Gateway for Testing: Casdoor\'s Dummy Provider, Stripe Test Mode and PayPal Sandbox","description":"A dummy payment gateway completes checkout without moving money. How the Casdoor Dummy provider works, and when to switch to Stripe test mode or PayPal sandbox.","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Payment","Testing","Stripe","PayPal","Subscription"],"date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Steam OAuth and Telegram OAuth with Casdoor: Adding Non-Standard Social Logins Through OIDC","permalink":"/es/blog/steam-telegram-login"},"nextItem":{"title":"Casdoor v4: una nueva consola, un Marketplace y OAuth para servidores MCP","permalink":"/es/blog/casdoor-v4"}},"content":"A dummy payment gateway is a payment provider that accepts every checkout as paid without contacting a bank or card network, so you can test orders, subscriptions and fulfillment with no money and no gateway account. Casdoor ships one: the [Dummy payment provider](/docs/provider/payment/dummy). Add it to a product, buy the product, and Casdoor records the payment and the order as `Paid`, starts the subscription and writes the transaction, all without leaving your server. Use Dummy in development, and switch to a vendor sandbox (Stripe test mode, PayPal sandbox, Paddle sandbox) in staging, where you also need declined cards, 3-D Secure and real redirects.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Dummy, Balance and vendor sandboxes at a glance\\n\\n| Provider | Test environment | How Casdoor picks test or live | Casdoor provider type |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| Dummy | None needed, every payment succeeds | Always simulated | `Dummy` |\\n| Balance | Internal, funded by a recharge product | Always internal | `Balance` |\\n| Stripe | Sandbox with `sk_test_` / `pk_test_` keys | By the keys you enter | `Stripe` |\\n| PayPal | Sandbox apps and sandbox accounts | `runmode` other than `prod` uses the sandbox | `PayPal` |\\n| Paddle | Separate sandbox account at `sandbox-vendors.paddle.com` | `runmode` other than `prod` uses `sandbox-api.paddle.com` | `Paddle` |\\n| Adyen | Test environment (`checkout-test.adyen.com`) | `runmode` other than `prod` uses the test environment | `Adyen` |\\n| AirWallex | Separate sandbox account | `runmode` other than `prod` calls `api-demo.airwallex.com` | `AirWallex` |\\n| Lemon Squeezy | Store test mode with test-mode API keys | By the API key you enter | `Lemon Squeezy` |\\n| FastSpring | Test storefront with `.test.` in the host | By the **Host** you enter | `FastSpring` |\\n| Polar | Sandbox at `sandbox.polar.sh` | Not supported, Casdoor always calls `api.polar.sh` | `Polar` |\\n\\n`runmode` is the option in `conf/app.conf` (or the `runmode` environment variable) described in the [configuration reference](/docs/basic/configuration). Its default is `dev`, so a fresh Casdoor talks to the sandboxes of PayPal, Paddle, Adyen and AirWallex until you set it to `prod`.\\n\\n## What a dummy transaction means\\n\\nA dummy transaction runs through the same records as a real purchase (order, payment, subscription, ledger entry) but moves no money. It is different from a sandbox transaction. A sandbox still calls the vendor\'s API, shows the vendor\'s checkout page and sends webhooks; only the money is fake. A dummy transaction never leaves your system.\\n\\nThat difference decides what each one can test. A dummy gateway is good for your own logic: does the plan grant the right role, does the order show up, does the coupon count as used, does the user land on the right page. A sandbox is good for the integration: credentials, redirects, card declines, authentication challenges and webhook delivery. You need both, at different stages.\\n\\n## How the Casdoor Dummy provider works\\n\\nThe Dummy provider has no settings and no payment page. Here is the full flow on Casdoor master:\\n\\n1. When the user pays, the provider\'s `Pay` call returns the Casdoor result page as the payment URL. It packs the price and currency into the order ID instead of creating anything at a vendor.\\n1. Casdoor stores the payment in state `Created` and sends the browser straight to the result page. There is no card form, fake or otherwise.\\n1. The result page sees a `Created` payment and calls `POST /api/notify-payment` about a second later.\\n1. The provider\'s `Notify` call always answers `Paid`, with the price it packed earlier, so the price check passes.\\n1. Casdoor then runs the normal success path: the payment and the order become `Paid`, the subscriptions created for the payment are updated, a `Purchase` transaction is written, product stock is reduced and a coupon on the order is marked as used. If the product is a recharge product, a `Recharge` transaction also adds the amount to the user\'s balance.\\n\\nSo yes, Dummy marks the order as paid right away, and the user sees the success page with no step in between.\\n\\nTwo limits follow from that design. Dummy can\'t fail: there is no way to make it return `Canceled`, `Timeout` or `Error`, so failure paths need a sandbox. And Dummy can\'t renew: Casdoor only allows auto renewal for Balance and Stripe, so a subscription bought with Dummy always has auto renewal off.\\n\\nOn a new database, Casdoor creates a built-in Dummy provider named `provider_payment_dummy` in the `admin` organization, next to a built-in Balance provider. You can also add your own: in **Identity** > **Providers**, set **Category** to `Payment` and **Type** to `Dummy`.\\n\\n### Keep Dummy out of production\\n\\n`/api/notify-payment` is open to anonymous callers, because real gateways call it without a Casdoor session. For a real provider that is safe, since `Notify` checks the payment with the vendor. Dummy checks nothing and reports `Paid`. Any product that lists Dummy in its **Payment providers** shows it at checkout, and anyone who picks it gets the product for free. Remove Dummy from every product before you go live, and do not put it on products in an organization that real users can reach.\\n\\n## Balance: the internal wallet you can fund with Dummy\\n\\nThe [Balance provider](/docs/provider/payment/Balance) lets users pay from their account balance. Casdoor checks the balance first, deducts the price, and marks the payment and order `Paid` in the same request. Balance is a real production feature, not a test tool, but it pairs well with Dummy: put Dummy on a recharge product, \\"top up\\" a test user, and the balance is credited with no money involved.\\n\\nThis matters for subscriptions, because Balance is one of the two providers that support auto renewal. With Dummy plus Balance you can test the whole renewal loop on a laptop with no gateway account.\\n\\n## Vendor sandboxes for staging\\n\\n**Stripe.** Stripe separates test and live data by keys: sandbox keys start with `pk_test_` and `sk_test_`, live keys with `pk_live_` and `sk_live_`, and objects in one mode are not visible in the other. Casdoor uses whatever keys you put in the [Stripe provider](/docs/provider/payment/stripe); `runmode` does not change anything for Stripe. Stripe\'s test cards cover the paths Dummy can\'t: `4242 4242 4242 4242` succeeds, `4000 0000 0000 0002` is declined, and `4000 0000 0000 3220` requires 3-D Secure. Use any future expiry and any CVC. Stripe\'s terms forbid testing in live mode with real card details.\\n\\n**PayPal.** The PayPal sandbox is a separate environment with its own apps and test accounts. PayPal creates a default personal and a default business sandbox account when you first sign in to the developer dashboard; you pay with the personal one. Create the app in sandbox mode, put its credentials in the [PayPal provider](/docs/provider/payment/paypal), and keep `runmode` at `dev`.\\n\\n**Paddle.** The sandbox is a separate account at `sandbox-vendors.paddle.com` with its own keys and products; nothing is shared with live. Paddle\'s test cards include `4242 4242 4242 4242` (succeeds) and `4000 0000 0000 0002` (declined).\\n\\n**Lemon Squeezy** has a test mode per store, and API keys created in test mode only see test data. **FastSpring** test orders go through a test storefront: insert `.test.` into the store host, for example `yourstore.test.onfastspring.com`, and enter that as the provider\'s **Host**. **Polar** has a sandbox with its own tokens, but the Casdoor Polar provider does not select it, so a Polar provider in Casdoor always works against live Polar. **Alipay** in Casdoor is also hard-wired to production.\\n\\n## A test plan for Casdoor products, plans and subscriptions\\n\\n### Development, with Dummy\\n\\n1. Create a [product](/docs/products/product) and add Dummy to its **Payment providers**.\\n1. Create a [plan](/docs/pricing/plan) for that product and a pricing page that lists it (see the [pricing overview](/docs/pricing/overview)).\\n1. As a test user, open the pricing page and buy the plan. Check that the payment and the order are `Paid`, the subscription is `Active`, and a `Purchase` transaction appears on the user.\\n1. Sign in as the user and check that the plan\'s role took effect in your application.\\n1. If the plan has **Is exclusive** on, buy it again and expect the error that the user already has an active subscription.\\n1. Apply a coupon at checkout and check that its usage is recorded.\\n1. Create a recharge product with Dummy, top up the test user, then buy the plan with Balance and **Auto renew** on.\\n1. To test renewal without waiting a month, edit the subscription\'s **End time** on its edit page so it ends within 24 hours. Casdoor checks subscriptions once an hour; expect **Renewal state** `Renewed` and a new subscription for the next period. Then buy the plan again with a balance too low for the next period, move its end time the same way, and expect `Failed` with the reason in **Renewal message**. The [subscription docs](/docs/pricing/subscription#auto-renewal) describe the exact rules.\\n\\n### Staging, with a sandbox\\n\\n1. Run staging with `runmode = dev` and sandbox or test credentials only.\\n1. Replace Dummy with the provider you will use in production, configured with its sandbox keys.\\n1. Repeat the purchase and check that the browser goes to the vendor\'s checkout and comes back to the Casdoor result page.\\n1. With Stripe, pay once with the decline card and once with the 3-D Secure card, and check the payment and order states for each.\\n1. With Stripe and **Auto renew** on, Casdoor saves the card at checkout and charges it itself at renewal, so the end-time trick above works in the sandbox too.\\n\\n### Going live\\n\\nRemove Dummy from all products, set `runmode = prod` if you use PayPal, Paddle, Adyen or AirWallex, and swap in live keys. Check the [payment records](/docs/products/payment) of the first real orders.\\n\\nIf you also test sign-up email in the same environment, a local mail catcher keeps those messages off real inboxes; see [MailHog vs Mailpit](/blog/mailhog-vs-mailpit).\\n\\n## Referencias {#references}\\n\\n- Casdoor source: [`pp/dummy.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/pp/dummy.go), [`pp/balance.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/pp/balance.go), [`object/order_pay.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/order_pay.go), [`object/payment.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/payment.go), [`object/subscription_renew.go`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/blob/master/object/subscription_renew.go)\\n- Casdoor docs: [Payment providers](/docs/provider/payment/overview), [Dummy](/docs/provider/payment/dummy), [Balance](/docs/provider/payment/Balance), [Subscription](/docs/pricing/subscription)\\n- Stripe: [Testing](https://docs.stripe.com/testing), [API keys](https://docs.stripe.com/keys)\\n- PayPal: [Sandbox](https://developer.paypal.com/tools/sandbox/), [Sandbox accounts](https://developer.paypal.com/tools/sandbox/accounts/)\\n- Paddle: [Sandbox](https://developer.paddle.com/build/tools/sandbox)\\n- Lemon Squeezy: [Test mode](https://docs.lemonsqueezy.com/help/getting-started/test-mode)\\n- FastSpring: [Test orders](https://developer.fastspring.com/docs/test-orders)\\n- Polar: [Sandbox](https://polar.sh/docs/integrate/sandbox)\\n- Adyen: [Live endpoints](https://docs.adyen.com/development-resources/live-endpoints)"},{"id":"casdoor-v4","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/casdoor-v4","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-30-casdoor-v4.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-30-casdoor-v4.mdx","title":"Casdoor v4: una nueva consola, un Marketplace y OAuth para servidores MCP","description":"Casdoor v4.0 se lanz\xf3 el 1 de septiembre. Dos meses y 25 versiones despu\xe9s estamos en la v4.25, por lo que es un buen momento para revisar los cambios. En resumen: la consola de administraci\xf3n fue reconstruida, el auto-hospedaje se volvi\xf3 mucho menos tedioso, ahora existe un Marketplace para integraciones y temas de inicio de sesi\xf3n, y Casdoor puede actuar como servidor de autorizaci\xf3n para servidores MCP y agentes de IA.","date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Release","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/release"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/o-auth"},{"inline":true,"label":"MCP","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/mcp"},{"inline":true,"label":"Self-hosting","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/self-hosting"}],"readingTime":5.54,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-v4","title":"Casdoor v4: una nueva consola, un Marketplace y OAuth para servidores MCP","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Release","IAM","SSO","OAuth","MCP","Self-hosting"],"date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Dummy Payment Gateway for Testing: Casdoor\'s Dummy Provider, Stripe Test Mode and PayPal Sandbox","permalink":"/es/blog/test-payments-without-a-gateway"},"nextItem":{"title":"Ejecutar Casdoor en Kubernetes: Chart de Helm oficial y configuraci\xf3n como c\xf3digo","permalink":"/es/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes"}},"content":"Casdoor v4.0 se lanz\xf3 el 1 de septiembre. Dos meses y 25 versiones despu\xe9s estamos en la v4.25, por lo que es un buen momento para revisar los cambios. En resumen: la consola de administraci\xf3n fue reconstruida, el auto-hospedaje se volvi\xf3 mucho menos tedioso, ahora existe un Marketplace para integraciones y temas de inicio de sesi\xf3n, y Casdoor puede actuar como servidor de autorizaci\xf3n para servidores MCP y agentes de IA.\\n\\nSi no lo has usado antes: Casdoor es una plataforma open-source de gesti\xf3n de identidad y acceso (IAM) escrita en Go. Es un proveedor de identidad completo, no una biblioteca ni un proxy de inicio de sesi\xf3n. Los usuarios residen en Casdoor, Casdoor emite los tokens y tus aplicaciones se comunican con \xe9l mediante OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect, SAML 2.0, CAS, LDAP o SCIM. La autorizaci\xf3n es gestionada por [Apache Casbin](https://casbin.apache.org), por lo que los modelos ACL, RBAC y ABAC funcionan perfectamente. Est\xe1 bajo licencia Apache-2.0 y aparece listado en el CNCF Landscape.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Una consola y p\xe1ginas de inicio de sesi\xf3n redise\xf1adas {#a-rebuilt-console-and-sign-in-pages}\\n\\nLa v4.0 reemplaz\xf3 la consola de administraci\xf3n de Ant Design por una nueva construida sobre shadcn/ui, Tailwind y Vite (ahora con React 19). Incluye una paleta de comandos, selecci\xf3n masiva de filas, validaci\xf3n de formularios en l\xednea, formularios de dos columnas y tablas donde puedes ocultar columnas. Cambiar de idioma ya no recarga la p\xe1gina.\\n\\nLas p\xe1ginas de inicio de sesi\xf3n, registro y restablecimiento de contrase\xf1a tambi\xe9n fueron redise\xf1adas. El modo oscuro funciona correctamente y las p\xe1ginas son usables en tel\xe9fonos m\xf3viles.\\n\\nSi est\xe1s actualizando desde la v3, lee primero la gu\xeda de actualizaci\xf3n enlazada desde el README. Lo principal que debes comprobar es la ruta de la compilaci\xf3n del frontend si sirves archivos est\xe1ticos a trav\xe9s de un CDN o proxy inverso.\\n\\n## M\xe1s f\xe1cil de auto-hospedar {#easier-to-self-host}\\n\\nGran parte del trabajo de la v4 provino de issues e hilos del foro de personas que ejecutan Casdoor por su cuenta:\\n\\n- **Sin contrase\xf1a predeterminada.** En la primera visita, Casdoor te pide establecer la contrase\xf1a de `admin`, o puedes configurarla mediante `initAdminPassword`.\\n- **Despliegue en subruta.** Establece `basePath` para servir Casdoor en `https://example.com/auth/` en lugar de su propio host.\\n- **Forward auth.** Casdoor tiene un endpoint de forward auth para Traefik, Caddy y Nginx, por lo que puedes colocar detr\xe1s aplicaciones que no tienen su propio inicio de sesi\xf3n, con rutas p\xfablicas y reglas por sitio.\\n- **Etiquetas Docker.** Las im\xe1genes ahora tambi\xe9n est\xe1n etiquetadas como `X`, `X.Y` y `X.Y.Z`, por lo que puedes seguir una versi\xf3n mayor o menor.\\n- **Helm.** El chart se movi\xf3 al repositorio principal, se publica en GHCR y [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) con cada versi\xf3n, y puede mantener organizaciones, aplicaciones y usuarios como configuraci\xf3n. Consulta [Ejecutando Casdoor en Kubernetes](/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes).\\n- **Servicio de Windows.** El binario puede ejecutarse como un servicio de Windows.\\n- **Actualizaciones.** La p\xe1gina de informaci\xf3n del sistema muestra la \xfaltima versi\xf3n y puede actualizar un binario oficial con un solo clic.\\n- **Operaciones.** Comprobaciones de salud de contenedores, contadores Prometheus para \xe9xito y fallo de inicio de sesi\xf3n, registros de auditor\xeda enviados a Syslog y soporte para Redis Cluster.\\n\\n## Marketplace\\n\\nEl nuevo [Marketplace](https://marketplace.casdoor.com) permite a un administrador instalar integraciones desde dentro de Casdoor: plantillas de aplicaciones, proveedores de identidad y temas de inicio de sesi\xf3n. Instalar uno completa la configuraci\xf3n por ti, y desinstalarlo restaura lo que hab\xeda antes.\\n\\nHay m\xe1s de 20 temas de inicio de sesi\xf3n hasta ahora. Puedes previsualizar un tema en tu propia p\xe1gina de inicio de sesi\xf3n antes de instalarlo. Los administradores de organizaci\xf3n tambi\xe9n pueden personalizar las p\xe1ginas de inicio de sesi\xf3n con sus propias fuentes, im\xe1genes y HTML, dentro de una lista blanca que bloquea formularios y scripts. La demo de solo lectura en [door.casdoor.com](https://door.casdoor.com) recorre los temas si quieres verlos.\\n\\n## OAuth para servidores MCP y agentes de IA {#oauth-for-mcp-servers-and-ai-agents}\\n\\nLa especificaci\xf3n MCP solicita que los servidores MCP remotos utilicen OAuth, y los clientes MCP generalmente no saben de antemano qu\xe9 servidor de autorizaci\xf3n encontrar\xe1n. Casdoor ahora cubre las piezas necesarias para esto:\\n\\n- Flujo de c\xf3digo de autorizaci\xf3n OAuth 2.1 con PKCE, y una p\xe1gina de consentimiento donde los usuarios pueden otorgar solo algunas de las scopes solicitadas\\n- Registro Din\xe1mico de Clientes (RFC 7591) y gesti\xf3n de clientes (RFC 7592)\\n- Documentos de Metadatos de ID de Cliente: un cliente MCP puede usar una URL como su `client_id` sin registrarse primero\\n- Autenticaci\xf3n de cliente `private_key_jwt` para clientes registrados din\xe1micamente\\n- Indicadores de Recurso (RFC 8707) y Metadatos de Recursos Protegidos (RFC 9728)\\n- Intercambio de Tokens (RFC 8693), incluyendo ID-JAGs para autorizaci\xf3n gestionada por empresas, para que el IdP de una organizaci\xf3n decida qu\xe9 agentes pueden alcanzar qu\xe9 servidores MCP\\n\\nApunta los metadatos de recurso protegido de tu servidor MCP a Casdoor, y el inicio de sesi\xf3n, consentimiento, emisi\xf3n y validaci\xf3n de tokens se manejan por ti. Casdoor tambi\xe9n ejecuta su propio servidor MCP, por lo que un asistente como Claude Code o Cursor puede gestionar usuarios, aplicaciones y permisos a trav\xe9s de \xe9l.\\n\\n## Otro trabajo en protocolos {#other-protocol-work}\\n\\n- Cierre de Sesi\xf3n \xdanico (SLO) de SAML y SSO iniciado por el IdP\\n- `prompt=none` de OIDC, mejor conformidad para el flujo h\xedbrido, `prompt` y `max_age`, y el par\xe1metro `iss` de la RFC 9207\\n- Revocaci\xf3n de tokens (RFC 7009), DPoP y el flujo de autorizaci\xf3n de dispositivos\\n- Inicio de sesi\xf3n con enlace m\xe1gico, comprobaciones de historial de contrase\xf1as y passkeys compartidas entre subdominios hermanos\\n- Inicio de sesi\xf3n exclusivo con un n\xfamero m\xe1ximo de sesiones por usuario\\n\\n## Pru\xe9balo {#try-it}\\n\\nLa forma m\xe1s r\xe1pida de echar un vistazo:\\n\\n```shell\\ndocker run -p 8000:8000 casbin/casdoor-all-in-one\\n```\\n\\nLuego abre http://localhost:8000. La imagen incluye SQLite y datos de ejemplo, as\xed que \xfasala para probar cosas, no para producci\xf3n. La [gu\xeda de instalaci\xf3n](/docs/basic/server-installation) cubre despliegues reales.\\n\\nHay dos demos en l\xednea: [demo.casdoor.com](https://demo.casdoor.com) es editable y se reinicia cada cinco minutos, y [door.casdoor.com](https://door.casdoor.com) es de solo lectura.\\n\\nSi prefieres no ejecutarlo t\xfa mismo, [Casdoor Cloud](https://www.casdoor.com/pricing) te ofrece una instancia dedicada y gestionada a un precio fijo sin tarifas por usuario. Las nuevas cuentas reciben $10 de cr\xe9dito para probarlo.\\n\\n## Enlaces {#links}\\n\\n- GitHub: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor\\n- Notas de la versi\xf3n: https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/releases\\n- Docs: https://casdoor.ai/docs/overview\\n- Discord: https://discord.gg/5rPsrAzK7S\\n\\nSe aceptan informes de errores y solicitudes de nuevas funciones en GitHub. Si Casdoor te resulta \xfatil, una estrella ayuda a que otras personas lo encuentren."},{"id":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-10-07-casdoor-on-kubernetes.mdx","title":"Ejecutar Casdoor en Kubernetes: Chart de Helm oficial y configuraci\xf3n como c\xf3digo","description":"Casdoor tiene un chart de Helm oficial. Vive en el repositorio principal bajo manifests/casdoor, se publica con cada versi\xf3n de Casdoor en GHCR y est\xe1 listado en Artifact Hub. A partir de Casdoor v4.18.0, el chart tambi\xe9n puede contener sus organizaciones, aplicaciones, usuarios y proveedores como configuraci\xf3n declarativa: c\xe1mbielos en el archivo de valores, ejecute helm upgrade, y los pods en ejecuci\xf3n aplican el cambio sin reiniciar.","date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"Kubernetes","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/kubernetes"},{"inline":true,"label":"Helm","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/helm"},{"inline":true,"label":"GitOps","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/git-ops"},{"inline":true,"label":"Configuration as Code","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/configuration-as-code"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/sso"}],"readingTime":6.02,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-on-kubernetes","title":"Ejecutar Casdoor en Kubernetes: Chart de Helm oficial y configuraci\xf3n como c\xf3digo","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","Kubernetes","Helm","GitOps","Configuration as Code","IAM","SSO"],"date":"2026-10-07T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor v4: una nueva consola, un Marketplace y OAuth para servidores MCP","permalink":"/es/blog/casdoor-v4"},"nextItem":{"title":"Casdoor se une al CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","permalink":"/es/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"}},"content":"Casdoor tiene un chart de Helm oficial. Vive en el repositorio principal bajo [`manifests/casdoor`](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor), se publica con cada versi\xf3n de Casdoor en GHCR y est\xe1 listado en [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor). A partir de Casdoor v4.18.0, el chart tambi\xe9n puede contener sus organizaciones, aplicaciones, usuarios y proveedores como configuraci\xf3n declarativa: c\xe1mbielos en el archivo de valores, ejecute `helm upgrade`, y los pods en ejecuci\xf3n aplican el cambio sin reiniciar.\\n\\nEsta publicaci\xf3n recorre una implementaci\xf3n en Kubernetes desde el primer `helm install` hasta una configuraci\xf3n que puede mantenerse en Git.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Instalaci\xf3n {#install}\\n\\nNecesita Helm v3.8+ y un cl\xfaster:\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor\\n```\\n\\nLa versi\xf3n del chart es la versi\xf3n de Casdoor, por lo que `--version 4.18.0` instala Casdoor 4.18.0. Sin un Ingress o Gateway, el servicio solo es accesible dentro del cl\xfaster. Para probarlo, reenv\xede un puerto:\\n\\n```shell\\nkubectl port-forward svc/casdoor 8000:8000\\n```\\n\\nLuego abra `http://localhost:8000`. En la primera visita, Casdoor le pide que establezca la contrase\xf1a de `built-in/admin`; luego inicie sesi\xf3n con ella. Vea [Iniciar sesi\xf3n en la consola de administraci\xf3n](/docs/basic/server-installation#sign-in).\\n\\n## Una configuraci\xf3n de producci\xf3n {#a-production-setup}\\n\\nLos valores predeterminados est\xe1n pensados para una prueba r\xe1pida: Casdoor almacena sus datos en SQLite dentro del contenedor, por lo que se pierden cuando se reemplaza el pod. Para una implementaci\xf3n real, coloque la configuraci\xf3n siguiente en un archivo `values.yaml` e instale con `helm install casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor -f values.yaml`.\\n\\n### Base de datos externa {#external-database}\\n\\n```yaml\\ndatabase:\\n  driver: postgres # or mysql, cockroachdb\\n  host: postgres.db.svc.cluster.local\\n  user: casdoor\\n  password: change-me\\n  databaseName: casdoor\\n  sslMode: require\\n```\\n\\nPara mantener la contrase\xf1a de la base de datos fuera del archivo de valores, coloque todo el `app.conf` en un Secret y establezca `configFromSecret` en su nombre.\\n\\n### Exponer Casdoor {#exposing-casdoor}\\n\\nEl chart soporta tanto un Ingress cl\xe1sico como la API de Gateway. Con un controlador de Ingress y cert-manager:\\n\\n```yaml\\ningress:\\n  enabled: true\\n  className: nginx\\n  annotations:\\n    cert-manager.io/cluster-issuer: letsencrypt-prod\\n  hosts:\\n    - host: door.example.com\\n      paths:\\n        - path: /\\n          pathType: Prefix\\n  tls:\\n    - secretName: casdoor-tls\\n      hosts:\\n        - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nCon la API de Gateway (Istio, Envoy Gateway, Cilium, Kong, NGINX Gateway Fabric y otros), adjunte un `HTTPRoute` a un Gateway existente:\\n\\n```yaml\\ngatewayApi:\\n  enabled: true\\n  parentRefs:\\n    - name: my-gateway\\n      namespace: gateway-system\\n  hostnames:\\n    - door.example.com\\n```\\n\\nEl chart tambi\xe9n puede crear el Gateway mismo y a\xf1adir una redirecci\xf3n de HTTP a HTTPS, vea la [gu\xeda de Helm](/docs/basic/try-with-helm#exposing-casdoor).\\n\\n### M\xe1s de una r\xe9plica {#more-than-one-replica}\\n\\nCasdoor mantiene las sesiones de inicio de sesi\xf3n en el disco local por defecto. Antes de ejecutar m\xe1s de una r\xe9plica, apunte todas ellas al mismo Redis, para que un usuario permanezca conectado independientemente del pod que atienda la solicitud. Cada ajuste de `app.conf` puede pasarse como variable de entorno, por lo que el endpoint puede provenir de un Secret:\\n\\n```yaml\\nreplicaCount: 3\\n\\nenvFromSecret:\\n  - name: redisEndpoint\\n    secretName: casdoor-redis\\n    key: endpoint # host:port, or host:port,db,password\\n```\\n\\nEl mismo Redis se usa para el estado del flujo de dispositivo OAuth. Una vez que Redis est\xe9 en su lugar, `autoscaling.enabled: true` a\xf1ade un HorizontalPodAutoscaler.\\n\\n## Configuraci\xf3n como c\xf3digo {#configuration-as-code}\\n\\nHacer clic en organizaciones y aplicaciones juntos en la interfaz web est\xe1 bien para una primera prueba, pero la mayor\xeda de los equipos quieren que la configuraci\xf3n de su proveedor de identidad sea revisada y versionada como el resto de su infraestructura. Los valores de `initData` hacen eso:\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  data:\\n    organizations:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: acme\\n        displayName: Acme\\n        passwordType: bcrypt\\n    applications:\\n      - owner: admin\\n        name: app-acme-portal\\n        organization: acme\\n        displayName: Acme Portal\\n        redirectUris:\\n          - https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\n    users:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: alice\\n        displayName: Alice\\n        password: change-me\\n        signupApplication: app-acme-portal\\n    roles:\\n      - owner: acme\\n        name: admins\\n        displayName: Admins\\n        users: [acme/alice]\\n        isEnabled: true\\n```\\n\\nEl chart almacena los datos en un Secret y los monta en los pods. Casdoor los aplica al iniciar, luego los verifica cada 30 segundos (`initData.watchInterval`). Cuando cambie los datos y ejecute `helm upgrade`, kubelet actualiza el Secret montado y los pods en ejecuci\xf3n lo aplican sin reiniciar.\\n\\nAlgunas reglas hacen que esto sea seguro de usar junto con la interfaz web:\\n\\n- **Solo se gestionan los campos que escribe.** Un objeto que ya existe obtiene los campos establecidos en el archivo; sus otros campos conservan los valores editados en la UI. Puede gestionar solo las URIs de redirecci\xf3n de una aplicaci\xf3n y dejar el resto a la consola de administraci\xf3n.\\n- **Las contrase\xf1as son contrase\xf1as iniciales.** La `password` de un usuario solo se utiliza cuando se crea el usuario, por lo que los usuarios pueden cambiarla despu\xe9s.\\n- **No se elimina nada.** Eliminar un objeto del archivo no lo elimina de Casdoor.\\n- **Los errores no derriban Casdoor.** Si un objeto falla al aplicarse, el error se registra y Casdoor sigue sirviendo; los datos se reintentan en cada verificaci\xf3n hasta que se apliquen.\\n\\nEl formato del archivo es el que Casdoor siempre ha usado para la [inicializaci\xf3n de datos](/docs/deployment/data-initialization): organizaciones, aplicaciones, usuarios, proveedores, certificados, roles, permisos, grupos, servidores LDAP, webhooks, planes, precios y m\xe1s, con los mismos nombres de campos que la API REST. Una forma r\xe1pida de empezar es exportar una instancia existente con `casdoor -export` y conservar los objetos que desea gestionar.\\n\\nPara mantener los datos fuera del archivo de valores, por ejemplo cuando son gestionados por Sealed Secrets o External Secrets, cree el Secret usted mismo:\\n\\n```yaml\\ninitData:\\n  enabled: true\\n  existingSecret: casdoor-init-data\\n  existingSecretKey: init_data.yaml\\n```\\n\\nLa misma caracter\xedstica funciona sin Kubernetes. Monte un archivo JSON o YAML y establezca:\\n\\n```ini\\ninitDataFile = /init-data/init_data.yaml\\ninitDataMerge = true\\ninitDataWatchInterval = 30\\n```\\n\\n## O use Terraform {#or-use-terraform}\\n\\nSi prefiere `terraform plan`, importaciones de objetos existentes y detecci\xf3n de desviaciones, el [proveedor de Terraform](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/casdoor/casdoor) oficial gestiona los mismos objetos a trav\xe9s de la API de Casdoor:\\n\\n```terraform\\nresource \\"casdoor_application\\" \\"portal\\" {\\n  name          = \\"app-acme-portal\\"\\n  organization  = \\"acme\\"\\n  redirect_uris = [\\"https://portal.acme.example.com/callback\\"]\\n}\\n```\\n\\nUse los valores de Helm cuando la configuraci\xf3n deba incluirse con la implementaci\xf3n, y Terraform cuando Casdoor sea uno de los muchos sistemas que ya gestiona con \xe9l. Vea la [gu\xeda de Terraform](/docs/deployment/terraform).\\n\\n## Procedente del antiguo chart {#coming-from-the-old-chart}\\n\\nHasta la versi\xf3n v4.15.0, el chart se publicaba en `oci://registry-1.docker.io/casbin/casdoor-helm-charts`, y el repositorio independiente `casdoor/casdoor-helm` est\xe1 ahora archivado. El chart de Docker Hub sigue recibiendo cada versi\xf3n, pero las nuevas instalaciones deben usar el de GHCR. Migrar una instalaci\xf3n existente es una actualizaci\xf3n regular y los nombres de los recursos permanecen iguales:\\n\\n```shell\\nhelm upgrade casdoor oci://ghcr.io/casdoor/helm-charts/casdoor --version <version>\\n```\\n\\n## Enlaces {#links}\\n\\n- Chart de Helm: [Artifact Hub](https://artifacthub.io/packages/helm/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [c\xf3digo fuente](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor/tree/master/manifests/casdoor)\\n- Gu\xedas: [Prueba con Helm](/docs/basic/try-with-helm) \xb7 [Inicializaci\xf3n de datos y configuraci\xf3n como c\xf3digo](/docs/deployment/data-initialization#configuration-as-code) \xb7 [Terraform](/docs/deployment/terraform)\\n- \xbfNo quieres ejecutarlo t\xfa mismo? [Casdoor Cloud](https://www.casdoor.com/pricing?utm_source=casdoor.ai&utm_medium=blog&utm_content=kubernetes) te ofrece una instancia dedicada de Casdoor que nosotros alojamos y mantenemos actualizada por ti."},{"id":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2026-02-22-casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape.mdx","title":"Casdoor se une al CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","description":"El 22 de febrero de 2026, Casdoor se uni\xf3 al CNCF Cloud Native Landscape, bajo la categor\xeda Security & Compliance en Provisioning. El Landscape re\xfane proyectos y productos cloud-native ampliamente utilizados; la inclusi\xf3n ayuda a usuarios y empresas a descubrir y evaluar soluciones para construir sistemas cloud-native.","date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"CNCF","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/cncf"},{"inline":true,"label":"Cloud Native","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/cloud-native"},{"inline":true,"label":"Landscape","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/landscape"},{"inline":true,"label":"IAM","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/iam"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Security","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/security"}],"readingTime":2.19,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Yang Luo","title":"Casdoor Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/hsluoyz","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/hsluoyz","key":"hsluoyz","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape","title":"Casdoor se une al CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","authors":["hsluoyz"],"tags":["Casdoor","CNCF","Cloud Native","Landscape","IAM","SSO","Security"],"date":"2026-02-22T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Ejecutar Casdoor en Kubernetes: Chart de Helm oficial y configuraci\xf3n como c\xf3digo","permalink":"/es/blog/casdoor-on-kubernetes"},"nextItem":{"title":"Mejorando la seguridad de la aplicaci\xf3n y la experiencia del usuario con Casdoor Face Authentication SSO","permalink":"/es/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"El 22 de febrero de 2026, **Casdoor** se uni\xf3 al [CNCF Cloud Native Landscape](https://landscape.cncf.io/), bajo la categor\xeda **Security & Compliance** en Provisioning. El Landscape re\xfane proyectos y productos cloud-native ampliamente utilizados; la inclusi\xf3n ayuda a usuarios y empresas a descubrir y evaluar soluciones para construir sistemas cloud-native.\\n\\n[https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?item=provisioning--security-compliance--casdoor)\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n<img src=\\"/img/blog/cncf-landscape-casdoor.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor on CNCF Landscape - Security and Compliance section\\" style={{maxWidth: \'100%\', height: \'auto\', border: \'1px solid #eee\', borderRadius: \'8px\'}} />\\n\\n_Casdoor on the CNCF Cloud Native Landscape (Security & Compliance)_\\n\\n## Sobre Casdoor {#about-casdoor}\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/casdoor-logo_1185x256.png\\" alt=\\"Casdoor\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nCasdoor es una plataforma de c\xf3digo abierto, centrada en la interfaz de usuario, de Identity and Access Management (IAM) / Single Sign-On (SSO) con una UI web. Admite OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML y CAS, as\xed como muchos inicios de sesi\xf3n sociales (Google, GitHub, WeChat, etc.), y le ayuda a construir la gesti\xf3n de usuarios y el SSO en unos pocos pasos. Casdoor est\xe1 desarrollado por [Casbin](https://casbin.org) en Golang y React. Utiliza una arquitectura de separaci\xf3n front-end/back-end, soporta alta concurrencia y es mantenido activamente por la comunidad. El proyecto ha recibido m\xe1s de 13k estrellas en GitHub.\\n\\n## Acerca de CNCF y el Cloud Native Landscape {#about-cncf-and-the-cloud-native-landscape}\\n\\n<div style={{display: \'flex\', alignItems: \'center\', gap: \'1.5rem\', flexWrap: \'wrap\', marginBottom: \'1rem\'}}>\\n\\n<img src=\\"https://www.cncf.io/wp-content/uploads/2023/04/cncf-main-site-logo.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF - Cloud Native Computing Foundation\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n<img src=\\"https://landscape.cncf.io/images/logo_header.svg\\" alt=\\"CNCF Cloud Native Landscape\\" style={{height: \'80px\', width: \'auto\'}} />\\n\\n</div>\\n\\nLa **Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF)** es un proyecto de la Linux Foundation que promueve las tecnolog\xedas nativas de la nube. Su visi\xf3n es hacer que lo nativo de la nube sea ubicuo. La CNCF mantiene el **Cloud Native Landscape** ([https://landscape.cncf.io](https://landscape.cncf.io)), que clasifica y rastrea proyectos y productos en el ecosistema nativo de la nube y sirve como mapa de referencia para desarrolladores y empresas.\\n\\n## Mirando hacia el futuro {#looking-ahead}\\n\\nUnirse al CNCF Landscape refleja el progreso en la madurez y comunidad de Casdoor. Seguiremos mejorando Casdoor y su integraci\xf3n con flujos de trabajo nativos de la nube, y damos la bienvenida a m\xe1s usuarios y colaboradores a unirse a la comunidad.\\n\\nEnlaces relacionados:\\n\\n- [Casdoor](https://casdoor.org) \xb7 [GitHub](https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor) \xb7 [Documentaci\xf3n](https://casdoor.org/docs) \xb7 [CNCF Landscape \u2013 Casdoor](https://landscape.cncf.io/?selected=casdoor)"},{"id":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2023-12-12-face-authentication-casdoor-sso.mdx","title":"Mejorando la seguridad de la aplicaci\xf3n y la experiencia del usuario con Casdoor Face Authentication SSO","description":"En una era donde la gesti\xf3n de la identidad digital es crucial, soluciones de Single Sign-On (SSO) como Casdoor est\xe1n revolucionando la forma en que los usuarios interact\xfaan con m\xfaltiples aplicaciones. El SSO de Casdoor simplifica el proceso de inicio de sesi\xf3n al permitir a los usuarios autenticarse solo una vez para acceder a m\xfaltiples servicios. Esto no solo mejora la comodidad del usuario, sino que tambi\xe9n aumenta el compromiso y la satisfacci\xf3n.","date":"2023-12-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Single-Sign-On","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/single-sign-on"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face Recogniztion","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/face-recogniztion"},{"inline":true,"label":"FaceID","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/face-id"},{"inline":true,"label":"Face","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/face"},{"inline":true,"label":"Machine Learning","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/machine-learning"}],"readingTime":2.02,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"James Lawson","title":"Product Manager","url":"https://casdoor.org","imageURL":"https://cdn.casbin.com/casdoor/static/admin.png","key":"James Lawson","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"face-authentication-casdoor-sso","title":"Mejorando la seguridad de la aplicaci\xf3n y la experiencia del usuario con Casdoor Face Authentication SSO","authors":["James Lawson"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Single-Sign-On","Face Recogniztion","FaceID","Face","Machine Learning"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Casdoor se une al CNCF Cloud Native Landscape","permalink":"/es/blog/casdoor-joins-cncf-landscape"},"nextItem":{"title":"Bienvenido","permalink":"/es/blog/welcome"}},"content":"En una era donde la gesti\xf3n de la identidad digital es crucial, soluciones de Single Sign-On (SSO) como Casdoor est\xe1n revolucionando la forma en que los usuarios interact\xfaan con m\xfaltiples aplicaciones. El SSO de Casdoor simplifica el proceso de inicio de sesi\xf3n al permitir a los usuarios autenticarse solo una vez para acceder a m\xfaltiples servicios. Esto no solo mejora la comodidad del usuario, sino que tambi\xe9n aumenta el compromiso y la satisfacci\xf3n.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nPara las empresas, el SSO de Casdoor agiliza la gesti\xf3n de credenciales de usuario, mejorando la seguridad y simplificando la incorporaci\xf3n de nuevos usuarios. Contrarresta el riesgo asociado con contrase\xf1as d\xe9biles o recicladas centralizando la gesti\xf3n de usuarios.\\n\\nSin embargo, ante la creciente amenaza en l\xednea, integrar medidas de seguridad robustas en sistemas SSO como Casdoor es vital. Aqu\xed es donde la tecnolog\xeda de autenticaci\xf3n facial entra en juego, agregando una capa extra de protecci\xf3n mientras se mantienen interacciones amigables con el usuario.\\n\\nLa autenticaci\xf3n facial emplea caracter\xedsticas faciales \xfanicas para confirmar las identidades de los usuarios. Compara im\xe1genes en vivo con plantillas faciales almacenadas, ofreciendo una forma segura y sencilla de autenticar a los usuarios. Este m\xe9todo presume de alta precisi\xf3n, incluso bajo condiciones variadas, y es ampliamente adoptado en dispositivos de consumo modernos.\\n\\nIntegrar la autenticaci\xf3n facial en el sistema SSO de Casdoor promete una experiencia de usuario segura, eficiente y sin interrupciones. Para asegurar un rendimiento \xf3ptimo, es crucial enfocarse en:\\n\\n1. _Medidas Anti-Spoofing_: Implementar detecci\xf3n de vivacidad, como seguimiento de movimientos oculares o gestos aleatorios, es esencial para contrarrestar intentos de suplantaci\xf3n y mantener la integridad del sistema.\\n\\n2. _Actualizaciones regulares para mejorar la precisi\xf3n_: Mantener la tecnolog\xeda de autenticaci\xf3n facial actualizada con los \xfaltimos avances en IA y biometr\xeda asegura la precisi\xf3n y mejora la experiencia del usuario. Es vital utilizar conjuntos de datos diversos para el entrenamiento de algoritmos para prevenir sesgos.\\n\\n3. _Equilibrar Seguridad y Comodidad_: Los mejores sistemas logran un equilibrio perfecto entre medidas de seguridad estrictas y comodidad del usuario. Este enfoque fomenta la adopci\xf3n del usuario asegurando un proceso de autenticaci\xf3n sencillo sin comprometer la seguridad.\\n\\nEn conclusi\xf3n, al integrar la autenticaci\xf3n facial con el SSO de Casdoor, las empresas pueden ofrecer una experiencia de inicio de sesi\xf3n segura, eficiente y amigable para el usuario. Esto no solo fortalece la seguridad, sino que tambi\xe9n se alinea con las expectativas modernas de los usuarios en el mundo digital."},{"id":"welcome","metadata":{"permalink":"/es/blog/welcome","editUrl":"https://github.com/casdoor/casdoor-website/edit/master/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","source":"@site/i18n/es/docusaurus-plugin-content-blog/2021-08-24-welcome.mdx","title":"Bienvenido","description":"\xa1Bienvenido al sitio web oficial de Casdoor!\u{1F44B}","date":"2021-08-24T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Casdoor","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casdoor"},{"inline":true,"label":"SSO","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/sso"},{"inline":true,"label":"Authentication","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/authentication"},{"inline":true,"label":"OAuth2.0","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/o-auth-2-0"},{"inline":true,"label":"Casbin","permalink":"/es/blog/tags/casbin"}],"readingTime":1.56,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"ErikQQY","title":"Casbin Member","url":"https://github.com/ErikQQY","imageURL":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/52615090?s=120&v=4","key":"ErikQQY","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"welcome","title":"Bienvenido","authors":["ErikQQY"],"tags":["Casdoor","SSO","Authentication","OAuth2.0","Casbin"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Mejorando la seguridad de la aplicaci\xf3n y la experiencia del usuario con Casdoor Face Authentication SSO","permalink":"/es/blog/face-authentication-casdoor-sso"}},"content":"\xa1Bienvenido al sitio web oficial de Casdoor!\u{1F44B}\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## \xbfQu\xe9 es Casdoor? {#what-is-casdoor}\\n\\nCasdoor es una plataforma [Identity Access Management (IAM)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Identity_management) / [Single-Sign-On (SSO)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_sign-on) centrada en la interfaz de usuario, basada en OAuth 2.0, OIDC, SAML y CAS.\\n\\nCasdoor est\xe1 desarrollado por [Casbin](https://casbin.org) utilizando Golang y React.\\n\\nCasdoor tiene como objetivo proporcionar una plataforma de autenticaci\xf3n poderosa para manejar la gesti\xf3n de usuarios.\\n\\n## \xbfPor qu\xe9 usar Casdoor? {#why-use-casdoor}\\n\\n* \u26A1\uFE0F Funcionalidad potente y configuraci\xf3n simple. With support for  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_google.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_github.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_facebook.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_twitter.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_linkedin.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_weibo.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wechat.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_qq.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_dingtalk.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>   <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitee.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img>  <img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_wecom.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img><img src=\\"https://cdn.casbin.org/img/social_gitlab.png\\" width=\\"30\\"></img> sign in and traditional username/password sign in, Casdoor helps you implement your users management system just in a few steps.\\n\\n* \u{1F4A5} Arquitectura de separaci\xf3n de front-end y back-end. Casdoor proporciona una interfaz web maniobrable y soporta alta concurrencia.\\n\\n* \u{1F942} Comunidad de c\xf3digo abierto activa. Casdoor es de c\xf3digo abierto y est\xe1 activamente mantenido.\\n\\n* \u{1F310} Soporte multilenguaje. Casdoor soporta m\xfaltiples idiomas, incluyendo chino, ingl\xe9s, franc\xe9s y muchos m\xe1s.\\n\\n\\n## \xbfC\xf3mo usar Casdoor? {#how-to-use-casdoor}\\n\\nPuede seguir la documentaci\xf3n detallada para conocer la [API de Casdoor](https://door.casdoor.com/swagger/) y [configurar su propio Casdoor](/docs/basic/server-installation). O utilice los [SDKs de Casdoor](/docs/how-to-connect/sdk) para implementar su propia aplicaci\xf3n."}]}}')}}]);